= Fluid mechanics
{wiki}

<Fluid mechanics> studies the motion and <forces> of <liquids> and <gases>. Its central simplification is the <continuum approximation>: replace individual <molecules> by bulk properties defined at every position and time. The averaging region must contain many <molecules> while remaining small compared with the distances over which those properties vary. A <fluid element> is such a small material parcel, rather than a single <molecule>. This connects <fluid mechanics> to <continuum mechanics> and makes <calculus> useful for describing flow. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-016-hydrodynamics-13-012-fall-2005/dc299d2ff05553b3515dccc23fc0f7d2_2005reading1.pdf[MIT, A. Techet, Hydrodynamics reading 1, pp. 2–3]{ref}

Three basic fields are the <velocity field> $\mathbf u(\mathbf x,t)$, the <mass density> $\rho(\mathbf x,t)$ and the <pressure> $p(\mathbf x,t)$. The first gives a parcel's instantaneous <velocity>; the second gives <mass> per unit <volume>. The <pressure> contributes an inward normal <traction> $-p\mathbf n$ across a surface with outward unit normal $\mathbf n$. Thus <pressure> is a <stress>, measured in $\mathrm{Pa}=\mathrm{N\,m^{-2}}$, and may vary with position even in <incompressible flow>. Additional <viscous stresses> can act tangentially. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-016-hydrodynamics-13-012-fall-2005/dc299d2ff05553b3515dccc23fc0f7d2_2005reading1.pdf[MIT, A. Techet, Hydrodynamics reading 1, p. 5]{ref}

The guiding questions are what must be conserved and which <forces> matter. <Mass conservation> constrains the <velocity field>; <momentum conservation> determines its response to <pressure gradients>, <viscosity> and body <forces>. An <incompressible flow> model, a <Reynolds number> estimate and <Bernoulli's principle> answer different parts of a problem. Their appeal is that a few carefully stated assumptions can determine useful quantities before the entire <velocity field> is known.

= Laminar flow
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

<Laminar flow> is a regime of <fluid flow> in which the motion remains organized, with neighboring fluid parcels following smooth, coherent paths rather than the irregular interacting fluctuations characteristic of <turbulence>. In a straight pipe, the simplest example consists of coaxial layers sliding past one another. This describes the character of the motion, not a requirement that every parcel have the same <velocity>. Nor does it require <steady flow>: a smooth profile can evolve in time as <viscosity> transmits motion from a newly moving wall into a resting <fluid>. The unsteady wall-driven solution provides an explicit example. https://notes.shreyasmandre.com/fluid-dynamics-notes/Ch3.html[Shreyas Mandre, Fluid Dynamics Lecture Notes, section 3.3]{ref}

The <Reynolds pipe-and-dye experiment> makes this distinction visible. A thin injected dye band can remain coherent in <laminar flow>, whereas irregular motion can fold it and carry dye across the pipe. However, <molecular diffusion> can spread dye even in <laminar flow>, and an inlet disturbance can move a coherent band without producing <turbulence>. A dye band is a <streakline>, not a measurement of the entire <velocity field>; in <steady flow> an ideal passive tracer's <streakline> coincides with a <streamline>. Reynolds explicitly distinguished a wandering band from one broken into eddies. https://archive.org/stream/philtrans06508925/06508925_djvu.txt[Osborne Reynolds, An Experimental Investigation (1883), section 11, pp. 941–943]{ref}

For a circular pipe of internal diameter $D$, define the mean axial <velocity> $U$ using the <volumetric flow rate> $Q$ and cross-sectional <area> $A$:
$$
A=\frac{\pi D^2}{4},\qquad U=\frac QA,\qquad
\mathrm{Re}_D=\frac{UD}{\nu}=\frac{\rho UD}{\mu}
=\frac{4Q}{\pi D\nu}.
$$
Here $\rho$ is <mass density>, $\mu$ is <dynamic viscosity> and $\nu=\mu/\rho$ is <kinematic viscosity>. This particular <Reynolds number> uses the diameter and mean <velocity>, not the radius or centreline <velocity>. Small <Reynolds number> generally favors <laminar flow>, but a single universal critical <Reynolds number> does not distinguish it from <turbulence> in all geometries and experiments. Even in a given pipe, inlet conditions and the magnitude of disturbances affect the observed onset. Reynolds demonstrated this dependence by deliberately inserting a wire coil. https://archive.org/stream/philtrans06508925/06508925_djvu.txt[Osborne Reynolds (1883), section 23, pp. 955–956]{ref}

\Image[laminar-pipe-velocity-profile.png]
{title=Ideal laminar pipe flow: zero velocity at the wall and twice the mean velocity at the centre}
{description=An illustrative fully developed Poiseuille solution, not measured data. The arrows and curve use a 10 mm diameter pipe with mean axial velocity 0.10 m/s. The dashed line marks the mean over the circular cross-section, not the mean along a diameter.}

The quantitative example above assumes <steady flow>, <incompressible flow>, a <Newtonian fluid> with constant <dynamic viscosity>, a straight horizontal circular pipe of radius $R=D/2$, stationary walls satisfying the <no-slip boundary condition>, and <fully developed flow>. The last assumption means that the axial <velocity> profile no longer changes downstream. A positive <pressure> drop $\Delta p=p_{\rm in}-p_{\rm out}$ over a developed length $L$ gives the <Hagen-Poiseuille equation>:
$$
u(r)=\frac{\Delta p}{4\mu L}(R^2-r^2)
=2U\left(1-\frac{r^2}{R^2}\right),\qquad
Q=\int_0^R u(r)\,2\pi r\,dr
=\frac{\pi R^4\Delta p}{8\mu L}.
$$
The <velocity> is zero at the wall and maximal at the centre. Averaging over the circular <area>, whose annuli have <area> $2\pi r\,dr$, gives $U=u(0)/2$; averaging along one diameter would give a different answer. These assumptions produce a <parabola>, rather than every <laminar flow> having a parabolic profile. https://notes.shreyasmandre.com/fluid-dynamics-notes/Ch3.html[Shreyas Mandre, Fluid Dynamics Lecture Notes, section 3.2.2, equations (3.9)–(3.11)]{ref}

As a stipulated calculation, take $D=0.010\,\mathrm m$, $U=0.10\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, $\rho=1000\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, $\mu=0.0010\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$ and $L=1.0\,\mathrm m$, and assume the developed <laminar flow> above. Then $\nu=10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$, $\mathrm{Re}_D=1000$, $u(0)=0.20\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, $Q=7.85\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m^3\,s^{-1}}$ (about $0.471\,\mathrm{L\,min^{-1}}$), and $\Delta p=32\mu LU/D^2=32\,\mathrm{Pa}$. These are predictions from chosen parameters, not measurements or a claim that $\mathrm{Re}_D=1000$ guarantees <laminar flow> in every apparatus.

The beauty of this example is that a spatially varying <velocity field> still gives an exact relation between two accessible measurements, <pressure> drop and <volumetric flow rate>. It predicts the <hydraulic resistance> $\Delta p/Q=8\mu L/(\pi R^4)$. Doubling the radius therefore multiplies $Q$ by sixteen at fixed <pressure> drop, length and <dynamic viscosity>, provided the model remains applicable. This makes the <laminar flow> model useful for sizing narrow tubes or determining <dynamic viscosity>. It also explains why a persistent narrow dye band can be informative without implying that the fluid is moving as a uniform plug.

= Reynolds pipe-and-dye experiment
{c}
{parent=Laminar flow}

In his 1883 paper, Osborne Reynolds investigated how the character of <pipe flow> depends jointly on <velocity>, pipe diameter and <viscosity>. His final dye apparatus drew water from a large glass tank through a horizontal glass tube with a smooth trumpet-shaped entrance. A separate reservoir supplied strongly colored water just upstream of the entrance; a valve below the tank controlled the outflow. The glass tube was immersed in the tank, so its internal dye band could be watched. Reynolds repeated the trials with different tube diameters and water <temperatures>. https://archive.org/stream/philtrans06508925/06508925_djvu.txt[Osborne Reynolds (1883), sections 11 and 21–22, pp. 941–943 and 951–953]{ref}

\Image[reynolds-pipe-dye-apparatus.png]
{title=Reynolds' pipe-and-dye experiment: observe the dye while measuring discharge and temperature}
{description=Original schematic of the historical operating principle, not a scale reconstruction. A falling tank level determines discharged volume; timing it gives flow rate. The lower dye patterns are conceptual sketches of a coherent band and irregular spreading, not measured trajectories or concentration records.}

The <volumetric flow rate> was measured independently of the appearance of the dye. Reynolds timed a one-inch fall of the tank's water level, corresponding to a calibrated discharged <volume>. In modern notation, if this volume is $V$ and the elapsed time is $\Delta t$, then $Q=V/\Delta t$ and $U=4V/(\pi D^2\Delta t)$. He determined the glass tubes' internal diameters by filling them with water. For a uniform bore, a known filled length $\ell$ and measured water <volume> $V_{\rm tube}$ determine $D=\sqrt{4V_{\rm tube}/(\pi\ell)}$; checking bore uniformity matters because the same <volume> would only give an average cross-section in a tapered tube. Thermometers at different tank levels checked both the <temperature> and its uniformity. Reynolds compared the <viscosity> at different <temperatures> using Poiseuille's results and checked his own water by separate experiments of that type. Thus the <Reynolds number> estimate did not come from judging the dye band or from assuming that transition occurred at a preset value. https://archive.org/stream/philtrans06508925/06508925_djvu.txt[Osborne Reynolds (1883), sections 21–22 and the reductions following Table I, pp. 951–955]{ref}

A practical repetition can instead collect a known outflow <volume> in a measuring vessel and time its collection, or weigh the collected water and divide its <mass> by <mass density>. Measure the internal diameter, rather than the external diameter, and obtain <kinematic viscosity> at the measured water <temperature> from independently established properties or a separate <viscosity> measurement. Record the dye appearance and observation position alongside $Q$, $D$, <temperature> and the resulting <Reynolds number>. These are alternative measurement procedures following $Q=V/\Delta t$, not additional historical claims about Reynolds' dye apparatus.

Begin with settled water and a small dye supply, then open the outlet valve in small steps. Reynolds saw a thin straight band at sufficiently low speeds. With a tank that had not fully settled, the band could wander while retaining its coherence. At higher discharge it could abruptly spread into the surrounding water downstream of the entrance. Short illumination by an electric spark resolved curls within what otherwise looked like a colored cloud. This connected the visible breakup with internal irregular motion rather than merely the appearance of an opaque pipe. https://archive.org/stream/philtrans06508925/06508925_djvu.txt[Osborne Reynolds (1883), sections 11 and 22, pp. 942 and 951–952]{ref}

The controls are part of the result. Reynolds used a smooth entrance and let the tank rest to reduce inlet disturbances. He found that a heated tank could retain slow circulation as it cooled, so measuring one <temperature> was insufficient: thermal uniformity and residual motion also mattered. His wire-coil trial broke up the band below the previous onset speed, but he explicitly cautioned that once the dye was mixed he could not tell from that dye alone whether the motion became orderly again farther along the tube. In a repetition, keep the dye supply small enough to act approximately as a <passive scalar>, avoid a strong injection jet, and record the inlet condition and downstream observation distance. If also testing the <Hagen-Poiseuille equation>, allow the profile to develop and measure <pressure> drop between positions within the developed region; do not treat the entrance and outlet losses as part of its uniform <pressure gradient>. The dye experiment by itself does not establish <fully developed flow>. https://archive.org/stream/philtrans06508925/06508925_djvu.txt[Osborne Reynolds (1883), sections 21–23 and 26, pp. 951–956 and 959]{ref}

The experiment's usefulness is that it compares a visible change in the motion with independently measured scales. Repeating it with several diameters and <temperatures>, while controlling disturbances, tests the organizing role of the <Reynolds number>. Its limitations are equally instructive: dye is a tracer of transport, <diffusion> still acts in <laminar flow>, and a local dye observation cannot by itself prove that <turbulence> persists throughout an arbitrarily long pipe.

= Void fraction
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$\phi=V_{\rm gas}/V_{\rm mixture}$}

The <void fraction> is the fraction of a mixture's local volume occupied by gas. For a horizontally uniform bubbly liquid it is the transported concentration in a one-dimensional gas-volume <continuity equation>. It is distinct from gas mass fraction because gas and liquid have different <mass densities>.

= Kinematic bubble transport in an exponentially narrowing vessel
{parent=Void fraction}
{title2=$\phi_t+V_s(1-2\phi)\phi_z=2\beta V_s\phi(1-\phi)$}

If the vessel radius is proportional to $e^{-\beta z}$, its area is proportional to $e^{-2\beta z}$. Gas-volume conservation gives the displayed equation. Along concentration <characteristic curves>, $\dot z=V_s(1-2\phi)$ and $\dot\phi=2\beta V_s\phi(1-\phi)$. Uniform initial concentration stays uniform in the uninterrupted bubbly region and follows this logistic law. Individual bubbles move at $V_s(1-\phi)$ instead: their rise distance there is $\eta=-(2\beta)^{-1}\log[\phi_0+(1-\phi_0)e^{-2\beta V_st}]$. Boundary interactions and dense-mixture backflow can invalidate the simplified closure.

= Bubble clearing in a cylindrical vessel
{parent=Void fraction}
{title2=$\Phi(t)=\phi_0[1-V_s(1-\phi_0)t/H]_+$}

For initially uniform bubbles with upward speed $V_s(1-\phi)$, no new gas production and free escape at the top, gas conservation gives flux $V_s\phi(1-\phi)$. A clearing <shock> rises from the impermeable bottom with speed $V_s(1-\phi_0)$ by the <Rankine-Hugoniot condition>. The displayed mean <void fraction> averages the remaining uniform region over the whole liquid depth $H$. This assumes the dilute regime and no retained foam layer.

= Strouhal number
{c}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$St=\omega h/U$}

The Strouhal number compares the advective time $h/U$ with a prescribed forcing time $1/\omega$. Some conventions use ordinary frequency instead of angular frequency, introducing a factor $2\pi$. For a longitudinal disturbance extending over length $\lambda h$, the relevant ratio is $\lambda St$ rather than $St$ alone.

= Lift
{disambiguate=force}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

= Lift
{synonym}

<Lift> is the component of the <fluid> <force> perpendicular to the far-field flow relative to the body. It need not point vertically: a tilted flow changes the lift direction, and a banked wing can produce a sideways component. In a planar problem choose a unit <vector> $\mathbf e_D$ along the incoming relative <velocity> $\mathbf U_\infty$ and a perpendicular unit <vector> $\mathbf e_L$ pointing towards the chosen positive-lift side. Then
$$
U_\infty=|\mathbf U_\infty|,\qquad
D=\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf e_D,\qquad
L=\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf e_L,\qquad
\mathbf F=D\mathbf e_D+L\mathbf e_L.
$$
Here $D$ is <drag> and $L$ is signed <lift>; $L<0$ means a <force> towards the opposite side. In three dimensions the plane perpendicular to $\mathbf U_\infty$ also admits a separate side-force direction, so a lift axis must be specified. <Lift> is also distinct from <buoyancy>, which can exist without a relative flow. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/aerodynamic-forces/[NASA Glenn, Aerodynamic Forces: components relative to the flow]{ref}

The <force> comes from adding local surface <tractions>, rather than assigning a single <pressure> to each side. Let $\Sigma$ be the wetted body surface and $\mathbf n$ its unit <normal vector> pointing out of the body into the <fluid>. The <pressure> contribution pushes inwards; the <viscous stress tensor> also contributes, including tangential <shear stress>:
$$
\mathbf F=\int_\Sigma\left[-(p-p_\infty)\mathbf n+\boldsymbol\sigma_{\mathrm{visc}}\mathbf n\right]dA,
\qquad
L=\int_\Sigma\left[-(p-p_\infty)\mathbf n+\boldsymbol\sigma_{\mathrm{visc}}\mathbf n\right]\cdot\mathbf e_L\,dA.
$$
Using a constant reference <pressure> $p_\infty$ is legitimate on a closed surface because $\int_\Sigma\mathbf n\,dA=0$. This <surface integral> makes the direction explicit: a surface-normal <force> is generally neither pure <lift> nor pure <drag>. <Pressure> and skin <shear stress> both enter the resultant, although the <pressure> term usually dominates <lift> on an <airfoil>. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/aerodynamic-forces/[NASA Glenn, Aerodynamic Forces: pressure and shear contributions]{ref}

\Image[lift-airfoils-pressure-geometry.png]
{title=Local <pressure> forces and <lift> relative to the far-field flow}
{description=Original toy geometry: a thin diamond-shaped section at $10\,{}^\circ$ <angle of attack>, with prescribed <pressure coefficients> $C_{p,u}=-0.8(1-x/c)$ above and $C_{p,l}=0.4(1-x/c)$ below. Arrows show gauge-pressure contributions: negative gauge pressure gives an outward contribution after the uniform ambient pressure has cancelled over the closed surface. The right panel resolves the integrated pressure force into flow axes. These prescribed pressures are neither measured data nor a solved flow; the corners and thickness are illustrative.}

The <lift coefficient> isolates a useful scale. For a finite wing of reference area $S$ (normally its projected planform area), and for a two-dimensional <airfoil> section of chord length $c$, respectively,
$$
q_\infty=\frac12\rho_\infty U_\infty^2,\qquad
C_L=\frac{L}{q_\infty S},\qquad
c_l=\frac{L'}{q_\infty c}.
$$
Here $q_\infty$ is <dynamic pressure>, $\rho_\infty$ is far-field <mass density>, and $L'$ is <lift> per unit span, with units $\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$. The signs of $C_L$ and $c_l$ follow the chosen lift axis. For stipulated values $\rho_\infty=1.20\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, $U_\infty=20\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, $S=0.50\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and $C_L=0.60$, $q_\infty=240\,\mathrm{Pa}$ and $L=72\,\mathrm N$. Doubling $U_\infty$ gives four times the <lift> only if $C_L$ stays fixed: changes in <Reynolds number>, <Mach number>, surface condition or <angle of attack> can change it. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/lift-coefficient/[NASA Glenn, Lift Coefficient: normalization and similarity limits]{ref}

A motivating experiment is to compare two independently observed quantities: the <lift> registered by a force balance, and the <lift> inferred from surface <pressures>. A low-speed wind tunnel holds the model fixed while air passes it at adjustable speed; a calibrated balance measures <forces>, while small flush holes on both surfaces connect by tubing to differential-pressure sensors referenced to $p_\infty$. Iowa State's GA(W)-1 teaching model has $43$ taps around a $101\,\mathrm{mm}$ chord; its instructions give their coordinates and close the contour by repeating the first tap. A classroom comparison can combine this pressure-panel method with a balance on the same model; this is an experiment design, not a claim that those instructions report a combined measurement. https://www.aere.iastate.edu/~huhui/teaching/2026-08F/AerE344/lab-instruction/AerE344L-Lab-05-Pressure-Airfoil-instruction.pdf[Hui Hu, Iowa State, AerE344 Lab 05, p. 2: GA(W)-1 taps and pressure panels]{ref} https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/drag-measurement/[NASA Glenn, Drag Measurement: force balances and calibration]{ref}

\Image[lift-airfoils-wind-tunnel.png]
{title=Compare a force balance with integrated <airfoil> <pressure> measurements}
{description=Original schematic of a proposed low-speed experiment, not a drawing of a particular facility. The model has a rotatable mount, surface taps and two independent measurement routes. Only a few taps are drawn. A far-field total/static pressure probe supplies the speed scale; balance calibration uses known loads, and pressure calibration uses known pressure differences. No measured curves or results are shown.}

Before turning on the wind, apply known positive and negative loads to the balance through its resolving centre; use $mg$ for the known load from a mass. Record all balance channels during loading and unloading, including response to <drag> and <torque> loads, to detect cross coupling, hysteresis and zero drift. Record a wind-off tare at each model orientation so its <weight> does not become an aerodynamic <lift> reading. Map tunnel flow direction and uniformity, rather than using the fan setting as a speed measurement. Covert's MIT guide describes these calibration, tare and flow-survey requirements. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-621-experimental-projects-i-spring-2003/f38ecde8773fd36c0d8f9069e6aa1df5_student_wt_guide.pdf[Eugene E. Covert, A Student's Introduction to the Wright Brothers Wind Tunnel at MIT, pp. 3–8]{ref}

For each pressure channel, apply zero and several known <pressure> differences spanning the expected range; determine the voltage-to-pressure relation and recheck it after a run. Inspect tubing for leaks and blocked taps, and wait for readings to settle before averaging: tubing can delay a pressure signal. This calibration principle is used in NASA's electronically scanned pressure systems, where several applied calibration pressures determine each sensor's response and repeated calibration tracks drift. A simple pressure standard can instead use a liquid-column height difference and the <hydrostatic pressure> law. https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19890009044/downloads/19890009044.pdf[Patsy S. Fulton, NASA-TM-101531, p. 8: pressure-module calibration]{ref}

Measure far-field static and total <pressure> outside the model's <fluid wake>; in low-speed <incompressible flow> their difference gives $q_\infty$ by the <Bernoulli equation>, so $U_\infty=\sqrt{2q_\infty/\rho_\infty}$. Record <temperature> and ambient <pressure> to determine <mass density> using the <ideal gas law>. At fixed $q_\infty$, step the <angle of attack>, repeat selected settings, and sweep back down to check reproducibility. Record balance <lift>, each tap's mean <pressure>, and fluctuation sizes. NASA describes pressure probes connected to differential transducers and emphasizes recording their reference <pressure>. https://www.grc.nasa.gov/www/k-12/airplane/tunptm.html[NASA Glenn, Total Pressure Measurement: probes, tubing and pressure references]{ref}

Convert each tap to a <pressure coefficient> $C_{p,i}=(p_i-p_\infty)/q_\infty$. For consecutive taps bounding a panel of length $\Delta s_i$ with outward <normal vector> $\mathbf n_i$, a <trapezoidal rule> estimate of the pressure <force> per unit span is
$$
\mathbf F'_p\simeq-q_\infty\sum_i\frac{C_{p,i}+C_{p,i+1}}2\mathbf n_i\Delta s_i,\qquad
L'_p=\mathbf F'_p\cdot\mathbf e_L.
$$
Compare this with balance <lift> divided by span only when the spanwise flow is approximately uniform. Agreement tests the pressure-resultant picture; disagreement calls for checking unresolved leading-edge pressure variations, skin <shear stress>, support loads and three-dimensional end effects before rejecting it. A force balance senses the total load, while taps alone reconstruct the pressure part. Tunnel walls and model blockage alter the effective flow and require corrections; end plates or a model extending between walls approximate two-dimensional flow but do not guarantee it. Record the <Reynolds number> and <Mach number> with the result, and do not interpret tunnel <lift coefficients> as universal wing data. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-621-experimental-projects-i-spring-2003/f38ecde8773fd36c0d8f9069e6aa1df5_student_wt_guide.pdf[Covert, pp. 8–9: flow characterization and wall effects]{ref} https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/lift-coefficient/[NASA Glenn, Lift Coefficient: Reynolds and Mach number dependence]{ref}

= Lift coefficient
{parent=Lift (force)}
{title2=$C_L=L/(q_\infty S)$}

The signed <lift> divided by <dynamic pressure> times a stated reference area. For a two-dimensional <airfoil>, use $c_l=L'/(q_\infty c)$ instead. Reference area and experimental flow conditions are part of the definition of a reported coefficient; see <lift> for a worked scale and a pressure/balance experiment.

= Dynamic pressure
{parent=Lift coefficient}
{title2=$q=\rho U^2/2$}

<Dynamic pressure> is the bulk <kinetic energy> per unit <volume> associated with <fluid> motion at <speed> $U$ in a specified <reference frame>:
$$
q=\frac12\rho U^2,\qquad
\frac{\tfrac12mU^2}{V}=\frac12\rho U^2,\qquad
\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}=\mathrm{Pa}.
$$
Here $\rho=m/V$ is <mass density>. The word "pressure" identifies its units and its use as a <pressure> scale; $q$ is not an extra isotropic <stress> to add to the local <static pressure> in every surface <traction>. A stationary <fluid> can have large <static pressure> with $q=0$. Conversely, knowing $U$ and $\rho$ fixes $q$ without fixing <static pressure>. The definition remains useful in <viscous fluid flow> and <compressible flow>, even when the simplest <Bernoulli equation> is inapplicable. <Dynamic Pressure by NASA Glenn Research Center>{ref}

For stipulated <mass density> $\rho_\infty=1.20\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ and reference <speed> $U_\infty=20\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, $q_\infty=240\,\mathrm{Pa}$. At $40\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ with the same <mass density>, $q_\infty=960\,\mathrm{Pa}$. This quadratic scaling makes <dynamic pressure> a useful normalization for the <lift coefficient>, <drag coefficient> and <pressure coefficient>; it predicts four times a particular <force> only if its corresponding coefficient remains unchanged.

\Image[pitot-static-pressure-difference.png]
{title=A <Pitot-static tube> compares <stagnation pressure> with <static pressure>}
{description=Original schematic with separate <pressure> passages and a differential readout. The right panel uses stipulated values, not measurements: ideal stopping in <incompressible flow> at unchanged height raises <static pressure> by 240 Pa as the <kinetic energy> per unit <volume> falls by 240 Pa. The diagram is not a probe design or a claim that real <static pressure> ports are error free.}

The connection with <stagnation pressure> requires hypotheses. For <steady flow>, constant <mass density>, <inviscid flow>, and <gravity> as the only <body force>, the <Bernoulli equation> gives, along one <streamline>,
$$
p+\frac12\rho U^2+\rho gz=B.
$$
Here $p$ is <static pressure>, $g$ is <gravitational acceleration>, and $z$ is height. Bring that flow to rest without losses at the same height and the limiting <static pressure> is the <stagnation pressure> $p_0$:
$$
p_0=p+q,\qquad p_0-p=\frac12\rho U^2.
$$
There must be no intervening pump supplying <work> or other energy input. Constancy along one <streamline> does not assert equal $B$ on all <streamlines>; <irrotational flow> in a connected region supplies that additional conclusion. Changing height requires retaining $\rho gz$. In <compressible flow> the stopping <pressure> difference generally differs from $\rho U^2/2$, and <viscous dissipation> can reduce recovered <stagnation pressure>. <Bernoulli’s Equation by NASA Glenn Research Center>{ref}

A <Pitot-static tube> turns this relation into a measurement: its upstream-facing opening senses <stagnation pressure>, while separate side openings sample <static pressure>. Connect these to the positive and negative ports of a differential <pressure transducer> or <manometer>. Under the hypotheses above, the measured difference $\Delta p$ gives
$$
U=\sqrt{\frac{2\Delta p}{\rho}}.
$$
Thus the stipulated $240\,\mathrm{Pa}$ and $1.20\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ imply $20\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, not an independently measured <speed>. Align the probe with the incoming flow and keep its passages clear: blockage and pinched tubing invalidate the comparison. At very low <speed>, the difference can be smaller than instrument error; at high <Mach number>, <compressibility> and possibly a <shock wave> require a different reduction. <Pitot – Static Tube – Speedometer by NASA Glenn Research Center>{ref}

A useful control is <differential-pressure calibration>. For a digital <manometer>, zeroing establishes the reading at equal port <pressures>; it does not establish the gain away from zero. The manufacturer's <Series 475 Handheld Digital Manometer: Specifications - Installation and Operating Instructions by Dwyer Instruments, Inc.> directs the user to vent both ports to the atmosphere before zeroing. Its stated accuracy is a fraction of full scale, not of the reading, and depends on instrument <temperature>. Select a suitable range and record the instrument variant and units; the manual covers several ranges.<Series 475 Handheld Digital Manometer: Specifications - Installation and Operating Instructions by Dwyer Instruments, Inc.>[p. 1: Specifications, Model Chart and ZERO instructions]{ref}

An independent physical check can use a liquid-column <manometer>: apply a stable <pressure> difference to both instruments and compare the digital reading with the <hydrostatic pressure> inferred from the vertical level difference. For equal-height connections carrying a gas of <mass density> $\rho_g$ above a manometric liquid of <mass density> $\rho_m$,
$$
|\Delta p|=(\rho_m-\rho_g)g|h|.
$$
This follows by balancing <hydrostatic pressure> down and up the two connected columns. Use vertical height, even if a tube is inclined. Known <mass densities>, measured height and <gravitational acceleration> set the comparison scale. Check several differences across the working range and reverse the port connections to check the sign. This is a proposed independent check, not a claim that the cited teaching experiment performed a full instrument calibration. Recheck zero after reconnecting the sealed tubing; a zero adjustment alone cannot detect a blocked passage or an incorrect scale.

= Pressure coefficient
{parent=Lift (force)}
{title2=$C_p=(p-p_\infty)/q_\infty$}

The <pressure coefficient> compares a local <static pressure> with a specified reference <static pressure>, using a fixed reference <dynamic pressure>:
$$
C_p(\mathbf x)=\frac{p(\mathbf x)-p_\infty}{q_\infty},\qquad
q_\infty=\frac12\rho_\infty U_\infty^2>0.
$$
The reference <mass density>, <speed>, <pressure> and location must accompany a reported <pressure coefficient>. Keep the same $q_\infty$ throughout a surface distribution; replacing it by local $\rho U^2/2$ gives a different quantity and fails at a <stagnation point>. The definition itself requires neither <Bernoulli's principle> nor <inviscid flow>, so measured <pressure coefficients> remain useful when those idealizations fail.

Positive $C_p$ means $p>p_\infty$; zero means equality; negative $C_p$ means $p<p_\infty$. This sign does not specify the sign of <absolute pressure>. For stipulated $p_\infty=101325\,\mathrm{Pa}$ absolute and $q_\infty=240\,\mathrm{Pa}$, $C_p=-3$ gives $p=100605\,\mathrm{Pa}$ absolute: a $720\,\mathrm{Pa}$ deficit, not a negative <absolute pressure>. If $p_\infty$ equals <atmospheric pressure>, the numerator is <gauge pressure>; a <wind tunnel>'s reference <static pressure> need not equal room <atmospheric pressure>. These are reference choices, not different physical <pressures>.

Under the same-height, constant-<mass density> $\rho=\rho_\infty$, <steady flow> and <inviscid flow> hypotheses of the <Bernoulli equation>, and with the same <Bernoulli invariant> as the reference flow,
$$
p+\frac12\rho U^2=p_\infty+\frac12\rho U_\infty^2,
\qquad C_p=1-\left(\frac{U}{U_\infty}\right)^2.
$$
The first equation immediately gives the second after subtracting $p_\infty$ and dividing by $q_\infty$. Hence a lossless <stagnation point> has $C_p=1$, $U=U_\infty$ gives $C_p=0$, and $U=2U_\infty$ gives $C_p=-3$. For a real stationary wall satisfying the <no-slip boundary condition>, the <velocity> is zero right at the wall; inserting that wall <velocity> in an <inviscid flow> formula would wrongly predict $C_p=1$ everywhere. A thin <boundary layer> approximation instead relates the wall <pressure> to the outer-flow <pressure> and uses the outer <velocity> where the <Bernoulli equation> applies.

\Image[cylinder-pressure-taps-ideal-coefficient.png]
{title=Ideal cylinder <pressure coefficients>, <pressure tapping> and cancellation of pressure <drag>}
{description=Original <potential flow around a circular cylinder> with zero <circulation>, not experimental data or a fit. <Angle> is measured counterclockwise from the downstream direction. Hollow circles mark sampling locations; arrows show only the reference-subtracted <pressure> <force> per unit <area>. A separate <pressure tapping> measures <static pressure>, rather than <stagnation pressure>. The front and rear ideal <pressure> <forces> cancel, so this model predicts zero <drag>.}

For <potential flow around a circular cylinder> of radius $a$ with zero <circulation>, the surface tangential <velocity> is $u_\theta=-2U_\infty\sin\theta$. Thus the ideal <pressure coefficient> is
$$
C_p(\theta)=1-4\sin^2\theta.
$$
With $\theta$ measured from downstream, the front is at $\pi$ and the rear at zero. Both ideal <stagnation points> have $C_p=1$, while the sides at $\pi/2$ and $3\pi/2$ have $C_p=-3$. This exact model is a useful baseline for an experiment; its complete rearward <pressure> recovery is not a prediction for a separated real <fluid wake>.

To obtain a <force>, integrate the local normal contributions with their directions. Let $\mathbf n$ be the unit <normal vector> from the body into the <fluid> and $\Sigma$ its closed wetted surface:
$$
\mathbf F_p=-\int_\Sigma p\mathbf n\,dA
=-q_\infty\int_\Sigma C_p\mathbf n\,dA,
\qquad \int_\Sigma\mathbf n\,dA=\mathbf0.
$$
The second equality removes a spatially constant reference <pressure> over the closed surface; it does not remove a nonuniform <hydrostatic pressure> or justify dropping the reference term on an isolated open patch. A negative $C_p$ contributes outwards in this reference-subtracted calculation, even though a positive <absolute pressure> still pushes inwards locally. Project $\mathbf F_p$ onto the chosen <lift> and <drag> directions. The full <fluid> <force> also includes $\int_\Sigma\boldsymbol\sigma_{\rm visc}\mathbf n\,dA$ from the <viscous stress tensor>, including tangential <shear stress>; <pressure tapping> does not measure that contribution.

For the two-dimensional cylinder, <force> per unit span follows from $dA=a\,d\theta$ per unit span and $\mathbf n=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$:
$$
(D'_p,L'_p)=-q_\infty a\int_0^{2\pi}C_p(\theta)(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)\,d\theta.
$$
Substituting $C_p=1-4\sin^2\theta$ gives $(0,0)$: opposite points have identical <pressure coefficients> and opposite <normal vectors>. Zero <drag> despite a strongly nonuniform <pressure> is the instructive limitation associated with <D'Alembert's paradox>. Real <viscosity>, <boundary layers> and <flow separation> can prevent rearward <pressure> recovery and produce <pressure drag>. Thus the measured curve can test the model and estimate a <pressure> contribution without proving that total <drag> vanishes.

A motivating measurement is Virginia Tech's <AOE 3054 laboratory manual/Experiment 3 - Flow Past a Circular Cylinder>. Its open-jet <wind tunnel> holds a rotatable $140\,\mathrm{mm}$ cylinder with end plates and 36 one-millimetre <pressure tappings> at ten-degree intervals. A reference <Pitot-static tube> supplies $p_{0\infty}$ and $p_\infty$ to one digital <manometer>; a second connects a surface tap to its positive port and reference <static pressure> to its negative port. Record the simultaneous readings:
$$
\Delta p_{\rm ref}=p_{0\infty}-p_\infty,\qquad
\Delta p_i=p_i-p_\infty,\qquad
C_{p,i}=\frac{\Delta p_i}{\Delta p_{\rm ref}}.
$$
The last equality uses the low-speed stopping relation, not an additional <speed> measurement. The manual requires sealed tubing and zero checks with the tunnel off. It also provides a water-column <manometer>: read each column's initial level, and subtract the independently measured reference-to-atmosphere <pressure> from the tap-to-atmosphere readings. Record tap positions after rotation. Compare the two instruments and check spanwise uniformity; end plates do not guarantee two-dimensional flow. <AOE 3054 laboratory manual/Experiment 3 - Flow Past a Circular Cylinder>{ref}

The inferential distinction matters: the <manometers> directly measure <pressure> differences, the ratio estimates <pressure coefficients>, and a <surface integral> estimates the pressure part of the <force>. Measure room <absolute pressure> and <temperature> if converting the reference difference to <speed> through the <ideal gas law> $\rho=p_{\rm abs}/(RT)$; use <thermodynamic temperature> in that equation. For <force> reconstruction, cover the complete contour and use <numerical integration> with the tap geometry. Finite tap spacing, unknown end effects, probe disturbance and fluctuations limit that reconstruction; tubes and instruments also average or delay changing <pressures>. Retain the signed raw readings, repeated readings and reference readings so that these limits can be assessed. A small reference difference magnifies any zero error in the ratio, rather than producing a reliably large $C_p$.

= Pressure tapping
{parent=Pressure coefficient}

A small flush opening communicating the local surface <static pressure> through tubing to a <manometer> or <pressure transducer>. An upstream-facing <Pitot tube> instead brings incident flow to rest to measure <stagnation pressure>.

= Static pressure
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}
{title2=$p$}

The local <fluid pressure>, distinguished from <dynamic pressure> and <stagnation pressure>. "Static" does not require the <fluid> to be stationary.

= Absolute pressure
{parent=Static pressure}
{title2=$p_{\rm abs}$}

<Pressure> referred to an ideal <vacuum>, rather than to <atmospheric pressure> or another chosen reference. <Gauge pressure> instead subtracts <atmospheric pressure>.

= Stagnation pressure
{parent=Static pressure}
{wiki}
{title2=$p_0$}

= Total pressure
{synonym}

The <pressure> obtained by bringing a flow to rest without losses; in <compressible flow> the usual definition specifies an <isentropic process> for stopping. For the constant-<mass density>, same-height <Bernoulli equation> discussed under <dynamic pressure>, $p_0=p+q$.

= Pitot tube
{c}
{parent=Stagnation pressure}
{wiki}

An upstream-facing probe that senses <stagnation pressure>. Determining <speed> also requires a separate <static pressure> and <mass density>.

= Pitot-static tube
{c}
{parent=Pitot tube}

A combined <Pitot tube> and <static pressure> probe with separate passages for the two <pressures>.

= Manometer
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

An instrument for measuring <pressure> relative to another <pressure>. A liquid-column <manometer> uses <hydrostatic pressure>; a digital <manometer> uses a <pressure transducer>.

= Pressure transducer
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

An instrument converting <pressure> or a <pressure> difference into a measurable signal. Its zero and scale must be checked separately, as illustrated by <differential-pressure calibration>.

= Differential-pressure calibration
{parent=Pressure transducer}

Checking the zero and scale of a differential <pressure transducer> against equal port <pressures> and independently known <pressure> differences. The <dynamic pressure> measurement discussion gives a liquid-column check.

= D'Alembert's paradox
{c}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

Ideal <steady flow> with <incompressible flow>, <inviscid flow> and <irrotational flow> can predict zero <drag> on a body that has nonzero <drag> in a real <fluid>. The zero-<circulation> cylinder <pressure coefficient> distribution gives an explicit cancellation example; <viscosity> and <flow separation> are absent from that model.

= Wind tunnel
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

An apparatus that produces controlled <fluid flow> past a model for measuring quantities such as <pressure>, <lift> and <drag>.

= Airfoil
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

= Aerofoil
{synonym}

An <airfoil> (British spelling: <aerofoil>) is the cross-sectional profile of a lifting surface, such as a wing or blade. Its chord is the straight segment from leading edge to trailing edge, with length $c$; its mean camber line runs midway between its upper and lower surfaces, and its thickness measures their separation. Camber is the departure of that mean line from the chord. The <angle of attack> describes the profile's orientation relative to the incoming flow. These independent choices let a designer alter the distribution of <pressure>, <lift> and <drag>, rather than simply enlarging the surface. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/wing-geometry/[NASA Glenn, Wing Geometry, Airfoil Geometry subsection]{ref}

The distinction between an <airfoil> and a finite wing matters. A two-dimensional section model assumes no variation along span and predicts <lift> per unit span $L'$. A wing of span $b$ has a spanwise distribution $L'(z)$ and total <lift> $L=\int_{-b/2}^{b/2}L'(z)\,dz$. Its tip flow and trailing <vortices> generate a downward induced <velocity>, called downwash, which changes the local flow seen by the sections. Thus one cannot generally multiply the isolated two-dimensional $L'$ by $b$. The wing aspect ratio $b^2/S$ measures its span relative to its reference area and helps characterize these three-dimensional effects. https://pressbooks.lib.vt.edu/aerodynamics/chapter/chapter-3-additional-aerodynamic-tools/[James F. Marchman III, Aerodynamics and Aircraft Performance, chapter 3.4: finite wings]{ref}

For a thin section with small camber slopes and small <angle of attack>, <thin airfoil theory> predicts
$$
c_l\simeq2\pi(\alpha-\alpha_{L=0}),\qquad
\frac{dc_l}{d\alpha}\simeq2\pi,
$$
where both <angles> are in <radians> and $\alpha_{L=0}$ is the zero-lift <angle of attack>. The model assumes two-dimensional, <steady flow>, <incompressible flow>, <inviscid flow>, an attached outer flow, and smooth departure at a sharp trailing edge (the Kutta condition). This last condition selects the <circulation> used by the <Kutta–Joukowski theorem>; with counterclockwise <circulation> $\Gamma$ positive and $\mathbf U_\infty=U_\infty\mathbf e_x$, that theorem gives $L'=-\rho_\infty U_\infty\Gamma$ for positive lift towards $+\mathbf e_y$. The beauty is that a detailed <velocity field> collapses to a simple force law, while the geometry enters through $\alpha_{L=0}$. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-01-unified-engineering-i-ii-iii-iv-fall-2005-spring-2006/d721171c42af48a056aaccec784a6d10_f03_0304.pdf[MIT Unified Engineering, F3 lecture notes, pp. 2–4: camber and lift slope]{ref} https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-01-unified-engineering-i-ii-iii-iv-fall-2005-spring-2006/cc3f20c40c9451bde6a595fdd96a7e9e_f02_mud_0304.pdf[MIT Unified Engineering, F2 questions 7 and 9: symmetric sections and Kutta condition]{ref}

A symmetric <airfoil> has $\alpha_{L=0}=0$ in this model: at $\alpha=4\,{}^\circ=\pi/45$ the predicted <lift coefficient> is $c_l\simeq0.439$, and reversing $\alpha$ reverses <lift>. A stipulated cambered section with $\alpha_{L=0}=-2\,{}^\circ$ instead predicts $c_l\simeq0.219$ at zero <angle of attack>. These are ideal calculations, not measurements or guarantees for a particular profile. Air parcels on the upper and lower surfaces have no obligation to arrive together at the trailing edge; the <pressure> and <velocity field> must satisfy the flow problem. A symmetric lifting section already makes an unequal-path-length explanation inadequate. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/bernoulli-and-newton/[NASA Glenn, Bernoulli and Newton: the equal-transit explanation is incorrect]{ref}

Real <airfoils> have a viscous <boundary layer>, even when <inviscid flow> approximates the outer motion. At sufficiently demanding incidence, <flow separation> can become extensive and the <lift coefficient> departs from its approximately linear law, reaches a maximum and may fall: this is <aerodynamic stall>. <Lift> does not generally vanish at <aerodynamic stall>. The onset depends on profile, <Reynolds number>, surface condition and motion history; $2\pi(\alpha-\alpha_{L=0})$ predicts neither a universal stall angle nor post-stall behavior. The pressure/balance experiment in <lift> makes this limit observable by measuring changes in the surface <pressure> distribution alongside the total <force>. https://pressbooks.lib.vt.edu/aerodynamics/chapter/chapter-3-additional-aerodynamic-tools/[James F. Marchman, chapter 3.3: separation invalidates thin-airfoil assumptions]{ref}

= Angle of attack
{parent=Airfoil}
{title2=$\alpha$}

The signed <angle> between a lifting surface's chord and its incoming relative flow. Positive incidence in the planar convention used in <airfoil> raises the leading edge relative to the trailing edge in a horizontal left-to-right flow. It is distinct from orientation relative to the ground; an induced <velocity> can change the local effective incidence.

= Thin airfoil theory
{parent=Airfoil}
{title2=$c_l\simeq2\pi(\alpha-\alpha_{L=0})$}

A small-disturbance, two-dimensional <potential flow> model for a thin <airfoil>, using surface tangency and smooth flow departure at its trailing edge to determine <circulation>. Its <lift coefficient> slope is $2\pi$ per <radian>, with camber shifting the zero-lift <angle of attack>. See <airfoil> for assumptions, examples and the <aerodynamic stall> limit.

= Aerodynamic stall
{parent=Airfoil}

A loss of the approximately attached-flow relation between <angle of attack> and <lift coefficient>, associated with substantial <flow separation>. A stalled <airfoil> can still produce <lift>; its behavior must be measured or modeled beyond <thin airfoil theory>.

= Uniform flow
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$\mathbf u(\mathbf x)=\mathbf U$}

A uniform flow has the same <velocity> at every position. A constant velocity field has zero <divergence> and zero <vorticity>; a <velocity potential> is $\phi(\mathbf x)=\mathbf U\cdot\mathbf x$. It describes the remote ambient flow in idealized obstacle and <line vortex> problems.

= Volume viscosity
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$\zeta_B$}
{wiki}

= Bulk viscosity
{synonym}

Bulk viscosity is the dissipative response to a fluid's volume expansion or compression, distinct from the shear response. For a Newtonian isotropic fluid its isotropic dissipative stress includes $\zeta_B(\nabla\cdot\mathbf v)I$, and nonnegative $\zeta_B$ gives nonnegative compression-related dissipation. A condensate's relative-density-flow bulk coefficient must be identified from its particular hydrodynamic equation; it is not automatically numerically identical to a dimensional Newtonian viscosity.

= Hydraulic resistance
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$R=\Delta p/Q$}

<Hydraulic resistance> relates the <pressure> drop across a resistive fluid element to its <volumetric flow rate>. In a linear model, $\Delta p=RQ$ with $R>0$, so dissipated mechanical power is $\Delta p\,Q=RQ^2$. A peripheral circulatory bed can be represented by such a resistance when its detailed dynamics are not retained. This terminal <pressure>-to-flow resistance differs from the area-dependent friction rate multiplying <velocity> in a distributed tube momentum equation.

= Physiological fluid dynamics
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

Physiological fluid dynamics studies the motion of fluids in biological systems, including blood flow in elastic arteries, flow through deformable vessels, and liquid layers lining airways. Wall compliance couples <pressure> to geometry, so wave propagation and mechanical feedback are as important as <viscosity>.

= Lubrication model of a compliant translating plug
{parent=Physiological fluid dynamics}
{title2=$p_x=-6\mu U/h^2+12\mu Q/h^3$}

For a freely translating compliant body separated from a cylindrical wall by a thin <lubrication> gap $h$, the body frame has wall speed $-U$ and body surface speed zero. The local axial flow is $w=-U(1-y/h)+p_xy(y-h)/(2\mu)$, so its flux per circumference is $-Uh/2-p_xh^3/(12\mu)=-Q$. Rearranging proves the displayed pressure gradient. A linear compliance $p=p_0+\alpha(r_0-r)$ couples this flow to deformation. Specified end pressures and zero net axial body force close the problem.

= Force-free pressure drop of an annular lubrication plug
{parent=Lubrication model of a compliant translating plug}
{title2=$\Delta p=\frac{2\mu}{a}\int(4U/h-6Q/h^2)\,dx$}

The pressure force on a plug-and-film control volume is $\pi a^2\Delta p$. Its opposing wall traction is $2\pi a\mu\int w_y(0)\,dx$, where $w_y(0)=4U/h-6Q/h^2$. Equating them for a freely moving body proves the displayed formula. Writing $s=2Q/U$, $h=sH$ and $x=\sqrt{s/\kappa}X$ converts it into $H(-\widetilde L)-H(\widetilde L)=C\lambda\int[4/(3H)-1/H^2]dX$, with $C=s/a$ and $\lambda=6\mu U/(\alpha\sqrt\kappa s^{5/2})$.

= Small-speed compliant-plug lubrication asymptotics
{parent=Force-free pressure drop of an annular lubrication plug}
{title2=$b^{-2}=\tfrac23(1-C)+O(C^2),\quad\Delta p\sim8\pi\sqrt{2/3}\mu U/(a\sqrt{\kappa s})$}

For $H_X+\lambda(H^{-2}-H^{-3})=X$, small $\lambda$ gives $H_0=(b^2+X^2)/2$. In the large-domain limit its inverse-power integrals are $I_1=2\pi/b$, $I_2=2\pi/b^3$ and $I_3=3\pi/b^5$. The force-free condition is $I_2-I_3=C(4I_1/3-I_2)$, or $2b^2-3=C(8b^4/3-2b^2)$. Expanding the root near $b^{-2}=2/3$ proves the displayed minus sign and leading pressure drop. The absolute downstream pressure relative to the elastic reference pressure fixes $s$ and therefore closes the speed-pressure relation.

= Annular inviscid-core displacement
{parent=Physiological fluid dynamics}
{title2=$u_1=A u_0'/(R+y),\quad v_1=-A_xu_0/(R+y)$}

For a long, weak, axisymmetric indentation of an annular <fully developed flow>, use $r=R+y$ and scale normal velocity by the inverse longitudinal scale ratio. The leading <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> give zero longitudinal pressure gradient and $u_0u_{1x}+v_1u_0'=0$. Together with $u_{1x}+(rv_1)_y/r=0$, this yields $\psi_1=A u_0$, hence the displayed displacement fields. Thin <viscous boundary layers> enforce tangential wall conditions separately.

= Forced annular displacement equation
{parent=Annular inviscid-core displacement}
{title2=$\sigma A_{xxx}-\beta\alpha_1A_t-\alpha_2AA_x=\beta\gamma_0F_t+\gamma_0^2FF_x+(\gamma_0^2/R)(AF)_x$}

A distinguished annular indentation limit has $\lambda^{-2}=O(\epsilon)$, $\lambda St=O(\epsilon)$ and $Re\gg\lambda\epsilon^{-3}$. Define $\beta=\lambda St/\epsilon$, $\sigma=(\lambda^2\epsilon)^{-1}\int_0^1u_0^2/(R+y)\,dy$, $\alpha_1=\gamma_0/R+\gamma_1/(R+1)$ and $\alpha_2=\gamma_0^2/R^2-\gamma_1^2/(R+1)^2$. Radial inertia supplies the pressure difference $\sigma A_{xx}$ between the walls. The axial momentum limits and the moving-wall condition supply its derivative, giving the displayed forced <KdV equation>. This reduction assumes the <viscous boundary layers> remain thin and their displaced flux is smaller than $O(\epsilon^2)$.

= Opposite propagation directions in the annular displacement equation
{parent=Forced annular displacement equation}
{title2=$\omega=\sigma k^3/(\beta\alpha_1),\quad A=-3\beta\alpha_1c\,\mathrm{sech}^2(k_s\xi)/\alpha_2$}

With no forcing, the linear <dispersion relation> has positive <phase velocity> $\sigma k^2/(\beta\alpha_1)$ and <group velocity> three times this. A decaying permanent wave with $\xi=x+ct$ instead obeys $\sigma(A')^2=A^2(\beta\alpha_1c+\alpha_2A/3)$. For positive coefficients a nonzero homoclinic wave requires $c>0$ and is negative, travelling upstream. Its inverse width is $k_s=\frac12\sqrt{\beta\alpha_1c/\sigma}$. The opposite directions reflect the nonlinear amplitude-dependent propagation speed.

= Peristaltic pumping
{parent=Physiological fluid dynamics}

<Peristaltic pumping> transports fluid by a travelling deformation or travelling external <pressure> acting on a tube. With wave coordinate $X=x-ct$, <mass conservation> makes the relative flux $Q=\alpha(u-c)$ constant. The laboratory flux is $\alpha u=c\alpha+Q$, so a negative relative flux does not rule out forward mean pumping.

= Weak peristaltic pumping of a collapsible tube
{parent=Peristaltic pumping}
{title2=$Q_2=\frac{c(n+1)}{2\alpha_0}\frac{P_e^2}{\widetilde P_0'^2+(R_0c/(k\alpha_0))^2}$}

For negligible inertia, periodic forcing $p_e=\epsilon P_e\sin kX$, resistance $R\propto\alpha^{-n}$ and prescribed mean area $\alpha_0$, write $Q=-c\alpha_0+\epsilon^2Q_2+\cdots$. First-order area obeys $\widetilde P'_0\alpha_1'+R_0c\alpha_1/\alpha_0=-kP_e\cos kX$. Its mean square is $P_e^2/[2(\widetilde P_0'^2+(R_0c/(k\alpha_0))^2)]$. Averaging the second-order friction balance gives $Q_2=c(n+1)\langle\alpha_1^2\rangle/\alpha_0$, proving the displayed forward pumping rate. Periodicity entails zero mean <pressure> drop; the mean-area convention fixes the expansion's otherwise free constant.

= Collapsible tube flow
{parent=Physiological fluid dynamics}

A one-dimensional collapsible tube model couples area $\alpha$, mean axial <velocity> $u$ and internal <pressure> $p$: $\alpha_t+(\alpha u)_x=0$ and $u_t+uu_x=-p_x-R(\alpha)u$. A wall relation specifies internal minus external <pressure> as a function of area. Decreasing area generally increases the friction coefficient $R$, while positive wall stiffness gives real small-amplitude <wave speed>.

= Choking by wall thickness in an elastic tube
{parent=Collapsible tube flow}
{title2=$h_c/R=1+\lambda/4-5\lambda^{1/5}/4$}

For steady uniform-density inviscid flow with pressure law $p=p_0+k(r-R)$ and a prescribed thickness $h$, mass conservation and the <Bernoulli equation> give $h/R=1-s^{-1/2}+\lambda(1-s^2)/4$, where $s=u/V$ and $\lambda=2\rho V^2/(kR)$. The function has a unique maximum at $s=\lambda^{-2/5}$, producing the displayed choking bound. Below it there are two positive roots; continuity from the upstream state selects the lower-speed root for $\lambda<1$ and higher-speed root for $\lambda>1$. At $\lambda=1$, the maximum is zero and any positive prescribed thickness is incompatible with this model.

= Subcritical tube flow
{parent=Collapsible tube flow}
{title2=$0<u<c$}

A positive <collapsible tube flow> is subcritical when its <characteristic speeds> $u+c$ and $u-c$ have opposite signs. A downstream boundary can then send information upstream. For a quadratic pressure-area law and fixed dimensionless flux $q$, this corresponds to $A/A_0>\sqrt q$.

= Supercritical tube flow
{parent=Collapsible tube flow}
{title2=$u>c$}

A positive <collapsible tube flow> is supercritical when both <characteristic speeds> $u\pm c$ are positive relative to the wall. Information then propagates downstream on both branches. For a quadratic pressure-area law and fixed dimensionless flux $q$, the condition is $A/A_0<\sqrt q$.

= Elastic jump in a quadratic pressure-area tube
{parent=Collapsible tube flow}
{title2=$q^2/\alpha+\alpha^3/3=\mathrm{constant}$}

An elastic jump is a short transition from a fast, narrow supercritical <collapsible tube flow> to a slower, wider subcritical flow. Neglecting body force and distributed friction across the jump conserves flux and the momentum flux shown above. Distinct upstream and downstream areas therefore obey $\alpha_2^3+\alpha_1\alpha_2^2+\alpha_1^2\alpha_2=3q^2/\alpha_1$. The flux function has its unique minimum at $\alpha=\sqrt q$, so an upstream state below this value has one conjugate state above it. The jump dissipates mechanical energy; it does not conserve a Bernoulli constant.

= Spatial attraction of a uniform downhill tube flow
{parent=Collapsible tube flow}
{title2=$(\alpha-q^2/\alpha^3)\alpha_x=(g\sin\theta/c_0^2)[1-qR(\alpha)/\beta]$}

For $p=P_0+\rho c_0^2\alpha^2/2$ and constant flux $c_0A_0q$, a downhill <collapsible tube flow> obeys the displayed steady equation. Uniform area satisfies $qR(\alpha_1)=\beta$. Since $R'<0$, linearizing about this root gives downstream exponential decay precisely when $\alpha_1^2<q$. This is spatial stability of a steady inlet-value problem, not a guarantee of temporal stability of the driven flow. The <wave speed> is $c_0\alpha$, so the stable spatial branch is supercritical.

= Friction-induced instability of a collapsible-tube flow
{parent=Collapsible tube flow}
{title2=$\nu^2+iR_0\nu-k^2c_0^2-inkR_0U=0$}

Let $R(\alpha)\propto\alpha^{-n}$, with $n>0$, and use disturbances $e^{i(kx-\omega t)}$ about uniform <velocity> $U$. The intrinsic <frequency> is $\nu=\omega-kU$. Linearized <mass conservation> and the decrease in friction as area grows give the displayed <dispersion relation>. For nonzero $k$ and positive flow, one branch grows exactly when $nU>c_0$. At $nU=c_0$, one branch is neutral, $\nu=kc_0$, while the other remains damped, $\nu=-kc_0-iR_0$. The maintained external-<pressure gradient> supplies the energy; positive friction alone does not imply linear stability of a driven flow.

= Pressure-area law of a collapsible tube
{parent=Collapsible tube flow}
{title2=$p-p_e=\widetilde P(\alpha),\quad c^2=\alpha\widetilde P'(\alpha)$}

The <pressure-area law of a collapsible tube> describes transverse mechanical equilibrium of a deformable tube wall. On a stable branch $\widetilde P'(\alpha)>0$. Linearizing mass and momentum conservation about a stationary uniform tube gives the displayed <wave speed>. External <pressure> is prescribed separately and is not part of the elastic wall stiffness.

= Tube wave speed
{parent=Pressure-area law of a collapsible tube}
{title2=$c^2=(A/\rho)\,dp/dA$}

Linearizing area conservation and the axial momentum equation of a <collapsible tube flow> gives waves travelling relative to the fluid at speeds $\pm c$, where the displayed relation uses the area derivative at fixed external pressure. For $p=P_0+\rho c_0^2(A/A_0)^2/2$, it gives $c=c_0A/A_0$.

= Arterial pressure wave
{parent=Physiological fluid dynamics}

A small <pressure> disturbance in an elastic artery propagates through the coupled inertia of blood and compliance of the wall. If $\mathcal A$ is the undisturbed area, $\rho$ the <mass density> and $c$ the <wave speed>, the linearized equations give $c^2=\mathcal A/\rho\,dp/d\mathcal A$. A travelling <pressure> amplitude $p_+$ carries flow amplitude $\mathcal A p_+/(\rho c)$; the oppositely travelling wave has the opposite flow sign.

= Bifurcation reflection of an arterial pressure wave
{parent=Arterial pressure wave}
{title2=$r=(Y_1-Y_d)/(Y_1+Y_d)$}

At a lossless vascular junction, <pressure> continuity and <volumetric flow rate> balance give $1+r=t$ and $Y_1(1-r)=Y_dt$, where $Y_d$ is the sum of daughter <characteristic admittances of an arterial wave>. Eliminating transmission amplitude $t$ gives the displayed <reflection coefficient>. A decrease in total area yields positive reflection if parent and daughter wave speeds are equal; differing stiffnesses require comparison of the admittances rather than areas alone.

= Pressure-flow phase lag from one arterial reflection
{parent=Bifurcation reflection of an arterial pressure wave}
{title2=$\omega\Delta t=\arg[1-r^2+2ir\sin\vartheta]$}

For incident and reflected harmonic <arterial pressure waves> with real $0<r<1$, the local <pressure> and flow phasors are proportional to $1+re^{-i\vartheta}$ and $1-re^{-i\vartheta}$. Their maximum-time difference is the displayed phase lag, on the branch $0<\vartheta<\pi$. Here $\vartheta=2\omega d/c$ is the round-trip phase to the reflector. Its derivative has the sign of $\cos\vartheta$. Raising the <wave speed> reduces the lag when $0<\vartheta<\pi/2$, but increases it when $\pi/2<\vartheta<\pi$. This conditional harmonic model does not establish an age trend for arbitrary vascular geometry or measurement stations beyond the sole reflector.

= Reflection of an arterial pressure wave
{parent=Arterial pressure wave}
{title2=$p_-/p_+=(RY-1)/(RY+1)$}

= Arterial wave reflection
{synonym}

A <hydraulic resistance> $R$ imposes $q=p/R$. Combining this with forward and backward <arterial pressure waves>, $p=p_++p_-$ and $q=Y(p_+-p_-)$, gives the displayed <reflection coefficient>. A matched load $RY=1$ has no reflected <pressure> wave, while a closed end has <reflection coefficient> one. At a network junction the combined downstream input admittance replaces $1/R$.

= Wave transfer matrix of an arterial segment
{parent=Arterial pressure wave}
{title2=$\begin{pmatrix}p_u\\q_u\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}\cos\beta&i\sin\beta/Y\\iY\sin\beta&\cos\beta\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}p_d\\q_d\end{pmatrix}$}

= Wave transfer matrices of an arterial segment
{synonym}

For time dependence $e^{i\omega t}$ and a segment of length $\ell$, let $\beta=\omega\ell/c$. Adding the forward and backward <arterial pressure waves> gives the displayed upstream-to-downstream matrix. The flow orientation is downstream at both ends. At a junction, <pressure> is continuous and incoming <volumetric flow rates> balance outgoing ones.

= Wave transmission through a resistively loaded arterial loop
{parent=Wave transfer matrix of an arterial segment}

Consider two identical two-segment paths from an inlet to a distal junction, with equal resistive beds at the two intermediate junctions and the distal junction. The segment transfer matrix and junction flow balances determine all <pressures>. If one inlet branch is removed and the beds are weakly loading the waves, the remaining segments form a three-segment chain with an effectively closed far end. The altered reflection pattern can raise peripheral oscillatory flow amplitudes even though a branch was lost; the incident wave amplitude, rather than total inlet <pressure> or flow, is the prescribed forcing.

= Characteristic admittance of an arterial wave
{parent=Arterial pressure wave}
{title2=$Y=\mathcal A/(\rho c)$}

= Characteristic admittances of an arterial wave
{synonym}

The <characteristic admittance of an arterial wave> relates the <volumetric flow rate> of a travelling <arterial pressure wave> to its <pressure>. For forward and backward amplitudes, $p=p_++p_-$ and $q=Y(p_+-p_-)$. It is the reciprocal of characteristic <pressure>-to-flow impedance, and should not be confused with the reciprocal <hydraulic resistance> of a steadily resistive segment.

= Fluid
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

A <fluid> is a material that flows under sustained <shear stress> and cannot maintain a static <shear stress> in its fluid state. <Liquids> and <gases> are the usual examples. A <Newtonian fluid> has <deviatoric stress> proportional to its <rate-of-strain tensor>, whereas more general fluid <constitutive equations> can be nonlinear or have memory. In a <porous medium>, the <fluid> occupies connected voids within a load-bearing <solid> framework. The important distinction is the response to a continuing tangential <force>: an ordinary elastic <solid> can settle into a fixed <deformation>, while an ordinary <fluid> continues to deform. A <fluid> can nevertheless be at rest when no unbalanced <shear stress> drives it. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-800-fluid-dynamics-of-the-atmosphere-and-ocean-fall-2004/7bb9e945405534b36776cbe404a8f839_lecture3.pdf[MIT, Fluid Dynamics of the Atmosphere and Ocean, lecture 3, pp. 4–5]{ref}

For a worked example, place a <Newtonian fluid> between parallel plates separated by $h=1\,\mathrm{mm}$, keep the lower plate fixed and move the upper one at $U=0.10\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. With <no-slip boundary conditions>, <steady flow>, no imposed <pressure gradient> and negligible edge effects, <Couette flow> has $u(y)=Uy/h$. For <dynamic viscosity> $\mu=0.001\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$, the <shear stress> magnitude is
$$
\tau=\mu\frac{du}{dy}=\mu\frac{U}{h}=0.10\,\mathrm{Pa}.
$$
The <velocity gradient> describes neighbouring layers sliding at different speeds. Doubling $U$ doubles the required <shear stress>; maintaining the same <velocity> keeps producing <deformation>, rather than fixing a final shape. This gives <viscosity> a concrete meaning: resistance to a rate of motion, not a prohibition on motion.

= Buoyant plume
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

A buoyant plume rises because its density differs from the surrounding fluid and entrains ambient fluid as it travels.

= Pathline
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$\dot{\mathbf x}=\mathbf u(\mathbf x,t)$}

A <pathline> records the motion of one identified <fluid element>: keep its starting position $\mathbf a$ and starting time $t_0$ fixed, and let physical time $t$ advance. In a <velocity field> $\mathbf u(\mathbf x,t)$ its <Lagrangian trajectory> $\mathbf X(t;t_0,\mathbf a)$ solves the <initial value problem>
$$
\frac{d\mathbf X}{dt}=\mathbf u(\mathbf X(t),t),\qquad \mathbf X(t_0;t_0,\mathbf a)=\mathbf a.
$$
A <streamline> instead freezes the time argument of the <velocity field>; a <streakline> fixes the observation time and varies the release time of many <fluid elements>. A <pathline> is useful for answering where a particular parcel goes and how long it takes, rather than merely which way the <fluid> is moving now. https://web.mit.edu/13.021/demos/lectures/lecture2.pdf[MIT Marine Hydrodynamics, lecture 2, sections 1.1–1.2]{ref}

For a concrete analytical model, take spatial coordinates $x,y$ in metres, time $t\geq0$ in seconds, and the spatially uniform but time-dependent <velocity field>
$$
\mathbf u(x,y,t)=(U,at),\qquad U=1\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}},\qquad a=1\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}.
$$
Its numerical components in these units are $(1,t)$. A <fluid element> starting at the origin at $t_0=0$ has the <pathline>
$$
x(t)=Ut,\qquad y(t)=\frac{at^2}{2},\qquad y=\frac{a}{2U^2}x^2.
$$
At $t=1\ \mathrm s$ it is at $(1,0.5)\ \mathrm m$; at $t=2\ \mathrm s$ it is at $(2,2)\ \mathrm m$. The <parabola> records earlier, shallower <velocity> directions as well as the final direction. Differentiating gives $(\dot x,\dot y)=(U,at)$ and <acceleration> $(0,a)$, so this curved <pathline> needs no spatial variation of the <velocity field>. More generally the <chain rule> along a <pathline> gives $d f(\mathbf X(t),t)/dt=\partial_t f+\mathbf u\cdot\nabla f$, the <material derivative> of a transported <scalar field>.

A motivating tracer demonstration appears in S. J. Kline's film notes: water passes through a contracting channel, and <hydrogen> bubbles mark a small element at a wire at time $t_0$. The illustrated element is shown again $4.7\ \mathrm s$ later, with its accumulated <pathline> superposed. Following that same marked element across successive film frames records a history; the entire bubble pattern in one frame has a different meaning. https://web.mit.edu/hml/ncfmf/05FV.pdf[S. J. Kline, Flow Visualization film notes, figures 7a–7c]{ref}

For a modern version of the readout, use sparse small markers in a transparent water channel, illuminate the viewed plane, and keep an <optical camera> fixed with a ruler in that plane. Record timestamps and track the same isolated marker between frames; successive positions estimate its <pathline> and $[\mathbf X(t+\Delta t)-\mathbf X(t)]/\Delta t$ estimates its <velocity>. A long exposure records a <pathline> segment but loses the timing within it, so its total length divided by exposure time gives only mean <speed>. These are proposed measurement steps based on Jean Hertzberg's teaching guidance, rather than additional measurements from Kline's film. Markers leaving the viewed plane, overlapping tracks and perspective can break particle identification; repeat with a thinner viewed slice and a lower marker <concentration>. https://www.flowvis.org/Flow%20Vis%20Guide/particles-4-dilute/[Jean Hertzberg and Flow Vis course students, Particles 4: Dilute Particle Techniques]{ref}

= Control volume
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$V$}

A <control volume> is a chosen region over which fluid <mass conservation> and momentum balance are integrated. It may be fixed or move with a prescribed <velocity>. Boundary fluxes must use <velocity> relative to the moving boundary. In <Stokes flow>, negligible inertia reduces the momentum balance to the sum of body <forces> and boundary tractions, making a carefully chosen <control volume> useful for finding drag without integrating directly over a curved body.

= Navier slip boundary condition
{c}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$u_{\mathrm{rel}}=b\partial_nu$}

= Navier slip
{c}
{synonym}

The <Navier slip boundary condition> allows a <fluid> to slide tangentially past a solid wall while resisting that motion through <wall friction in the Navier slip boundary condition>. For parallel flow past a flat, impermeable wall translating uniformly with tangential <velocity> $U_w$, write the fluid's tangential <velocity> as $u$ and its relative tangential <velocity> as $u_{\mathrm{rel}}=u-U_w$. Then
$$
u_{\mathrm{rel}}=b\partial_nu,\qquad b\ge0,
$$
where $b$ is the <slip length>, also written $\ell_s$. The <unit normal> $\mathbf n$ points from the wall into the <fluid>, and $\partial_nu=\mathbf n\cdot\nabla u$ is the <normal derivative>. The separate <no-penetration boundary condition> requires $(\mathbf u-\mathbf U_w)\cdot\mathbf n=0$: tangential sliding does not allow flow through the wall.

The sign convention matters. In a channel $0<z<H$, the lower wall has inward <unit normal> $+\mathbf e_z$ and the upper wall has inward <unit normal> $-\mathbf e_z$. If both walls obey the <Navier slip boundary condition>, their tangential <velocities> satisfy
$$
u(0)-U_0=b_0u'(0),\qquad
u(H)-U_H=-b_Hu'(H).
$$
Here the prime means the <derivative> with respect to $z$. Using $+b_Hu'(H)$ at the upper wall would reverse the friction law. The examples <Couette flow with a slipping lower wall> and <planar Poiseuille flow with equal wall slip> use different upper-wall <boundary conditions>.

This flat-wall formula is a local linear model. On a curved wall the <Newtonian fluid stress tensor> must supply the tangential <traction>; the <normal derivative> alone generally misses curvature terms. The author-written review by Lauga, Brenner and Stone states the full strain-based condition with the <unit normal> directed into the liquid. https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0501557[Lauga, Brenner and Stone, Microfluidics: The No-Slip Boundary Condition (2005), §2.1, equation (2)]{ref}

The same review credits Navier's 1823 treatise with the linear slip law. Its history is a reminder that the <no-slip boundary condition> is an experimentally successful model, whose validity depends on the interface and scale. The history here follows that review rather than a new reading of Navier's original memoir. https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0501557[Lauga, Brenner and Stone (2005), §2.1: the nineteenth-century boundary-condition debate]{ref}

= Slip length
{parent=Navier slip boundary condition}
{title2=$b=\ell_s$}

The <slip length> measures tangential wall mobility in the <Navier slip boundary condition>. Where the wall <shear rate> is nonzero,
$$
b=\frac{u_{\mathrm{rel}}}{\partial_nu}.
$$
The numerator is a <velocity> and the denominator has units $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, so $b$ has units of length. Geometrically, take a local inward normal coordinate $s$, with the wall at $s=0$. The tangent line to the relative <velocity> profile is
$$
u_{\mathrm{rel,lin}}(s)=u_{\mathrm{rel}}(0)+s\partial_nu(0)
=(b+s)\partial_nu(0).
$$
It reaches zero at $s=-b$, a fictitious distance $b$ inside the solid. This extrapolation does not assert that <fluid> exists there. At a moving wall, extrapolate to the wall's <velocity>, rather than to zero laboratory <velocity>. If both the wall <shear rate> and relative <velocity> vanish, their ratio cannot determine $b$.

The useful comparison is $b/H$, where $H$ is the channel gap. A small absolute <slip length> can noticeably change <volumetric flow rate> in a micrometre-scale channel. Measured effective <slip length> may summarize unresolved interfacial structure; it need not mean literal molecular sliding at every point. The review distinguishes intrinsic, apparent and effective slip and discusses how surface conditions affect measurements. https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0501557[Lauga, Brenner and Stone (2005), §§2.2 and 3.1: slip terminology and measurement]{ref}

= Couette flow with a slipping lower wall
{parent=Slip length}
{title2=$u(z)=U(z+b)/(H+b)$}

Consider <steady flow> of a <Newtonian fluid> in <incompressible flow> with constant <dynamic viscosity> $\mu>0$ between parallel plates at $z=0$ and $z=H$. The bottom plate is stationary and obeys the <Navier slip boundary condition> with <slip length> $b$. The top plate moves at <velocity> $U>0$ and obeys the <no-slip boundary condition>. There is no streamwise <pressure gradient> or body <force>. The <Couette flow> solution is
$$
u(z)=U\frac{z+b}{H+b},\qquad
u(0)=\frac{Ub}{H+b},\qquad
\tau=\mu u'(z)=\frac{\mu U}{H+b}.
$$
Here $\tau$ is the constant <shear stress> magnitude. The lower wall's <Navier slip boundary condition> holds exactly because $bu'(0)=bU/(H+b)=u(0)$. At the top, $u(H)=U$ is exactly the prescribed <no-slip boundary condition>; no finite upper-wall <slip length> is imposed in this example.

For a numerical example, stipulate $H=10\,\mathrm{\mu m}$, $b=2\,\mathrm{\mu m}$, $U=1\,\mathrm{mm\,s^{-1}}$ and $\mu=10^{-3}\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$. The bottom <velocity> is $0.167\,\mathrm{mm\,s^{-1}}$ and the <shear stress> is $0.0833\,\mathrm{Pa}$, compared with $0.100\,\mathrm{Pa}$ when both plates obey the <no-slip boundary condition>. The extrapolated profile reaches zero at $z=-2\,\mathrm{\mu m}$.

\Image[navier-slip-couette.png]
{title=Couette profiles reveal slip length by extrapolation beneath the lower wall}
{description=The bottom wall is stationary with <slip length> $b$; the upper wall at $z=H$ moves at <velocity> $U$ with a <no-slip boundary condition>. Solid lines show <Couette flow> for $b/H=0$, $0.25$ and $0.75$. Dashed continuations inside the shaded solid reach zero at $z=-b$. Increasing <slip length> reduces the <shear rate> required for the same upper-wall <velocity>.}

For fixed $H$, $U$ and $\mu$, $b\to0$ recovers $u=Uz/H$. As $b\to\infty$, $u\to U$ throughout the gap and $\tau\to0$: the moving upper wall selects the limiting uniform <velocity>, while the lower wall approaches a <stress-free boundary condition>. The <dynamic viscosity> stays positive; the disappearing <shear stress> comes from the disappearing <velocity gradient>.

A short derivation uses <unidirectional flow>: the <convective acceleration> vanishes and the <Navier-Stokes equation> reduces to $\mu u''=0$. Write $u=Az+B$. The bottom condition gives $B=bA$ and the top condition gives $A(H+b)=U$.

= Planar Poiseuille flow with equal wall slip
{parent=Slip length}
{title2=$q=GH^3(1+6b/H)/(12\mu)$}

Now both plates at $z=0$ and $z=H$ are stationary and both obey the <Navier slip boundary condition> with the same <slip length> $b$. Drive <steady flow> of a <Newtonian fluid> in <incompressible flow> by the constant <pressure gradient> $G=-dp/dx>0$, with constant <dynamic viscosity> $\mu>0$ and no other streamwise body <force>. The planar <Poiseuille flow> profile and its <volumetric flow rate> per unit width are
$$
u(z)=\frac{G}{2\mu}\left[z(H-z)+bH\right],\qquad
q=\int_0^H u(z)\,dz
=\frac{GH^3}{12\mu}\left(1+\frac{6b}{H}\right).
$$
The full <volumetric flow rate> for channel width $W$ is $Q=Wq$ when side-wall effects are negligible. Thus $q$ has units $\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$, while $Q$ has units $\mathrm{m^3\,s^{-1}}$. Denote the value at $b=0$ by $q_0=GH^3/(12\mu)$.

The two wall checks expose the normal signs:
$$
u'(z)=\frac{G}{2\mu}(H-2z),\qquad
u(0)=u(H)=\frac{GbH}{2\mu},
$$
$$
bu'(0)=\frac{GbH}{2\mu}=u(0),\qquad
-bu'(H)=\frac{GbH}{2\mu}=u(H).
$$
Both <Navier slip boundary conditions> therefore hold exactly. The two walls exert backward tangential <tractions> of magnitude $GH/2$ on the <fluid>. Increasing $b$ at fixed $G$ adds a uniform <velocity> offset, leaving the <velocity gradient> and wall <shear stress> unchanged. This differs from <Couette flow with a slipping lower wall>, where upper-wall <velocity> is fixed and <shear stress> decreases.

For $H=10\,\mathrm{\mu m}$, $b=2\,\mathrm{\mu m}$, $G=10^3\,\mathrm{Pa\,m^{-1}}$ and $\mu=10^{-3}\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$, the wall <velocity> is $10\,\mathrm{\mu m\,s^{-1}}$, the centre <velocity> is $22.5\,\mathrm{\mu m\,s^{-1}}$, and $q=1.83\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$. The enhancement is $q/q_0=2.2$. Equivalently, a prescribed <volumetric flow rate> needs only $1/2.2$ of the original <pressure gradient>. This makes <slip length> useful for estimating pumping requirements in small channels.

\Image[navier-slip-channel-flow.png]
{title=Equal slip at both channel walls increases pressure-driven flow}
{description=Both walls are stationary and obey the <Navier slip boundary condition>. Left: normalized planar <Poiseuille flow> profiles at fixed <pressure gradient> $G$, gap $H$ and <dynamic viscosity> $\mu$ for $b/H=0$, $0.10$ and $0.25$. Arrows show inward <unit normals> in opposite directions at the two walls. Right: <volumetric flow rate> per unit width increases by the factor $1+6b/H$ at fixed $G$.}

At fixed $H$, $G>0$ and $\mu$, $b\to0$ recovers ordinary planar <Poiseuille flow>. As $b\to\infty$, the <velocity> and $q$ grow without bound in this model, although the varying part of the profile stays the same. At exactly zero tangential wall <traction>, no steady solution can balance this nonzero <pressure gradient>: $u'(0)=u'(H)=0$ conflicts with $\mu[u'(H)-u'(0)]=-GH$. Unlimited enhancement is consequently not a prediction for a real channel; finite wall friction, finite driving capacity and the validity of the assumed <steady flow> must be assessed. At fixed finite $q$ instead, $G\to0$ and the profile approaches uniform <velocity> $q/H$.

A short derivation integrates the reduced <Navier-Stokes equation> $\mu u''=-G$ twice. Equal <slip lengths> and the opposite inward <unit normals> give $u=G[z(H-z)+bH]/(2\mu)$. Its <integral> over the gap supplies the enhancement factor $1+6b/H$.

= Wall friction in the Navier slip boundary condition
{parent=Navier slip boundary condition}
{title2=$\mathcal D_w=\mu u_{\mathrm{rel}}^2/b$}

For a <Newtonian fluid> and a flat wall with $0<b<\infty$, the <Navier slip boundary condition> is equivalent to a tangential wall-on-fluid <traction>
$$
t_{w,t}=-\frac{\mu}{b}u_{\mathrm{rel}}.
$$
The sign expresses resisting <friction>: $\partial_nu$ uses the inward <unit normal>, whereas the wall-on-fluid <traction> uses the outward <unit normal> of the fluid domain. The irreversible loss of mechanical <energy> per unit wall <area> and time is
$$
\mathcal D_w=-t_{w,t}u_{\mathrm{rel}}
=\frac{\mu}{b}u_{\mathrm{rel}}^2\ge0,
\qquad [\mathcal D_w]=\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}.
$$
For two tangential directions replace $u_{\mathrm{rel}}^2$ by the squared <Euclidean norm> of relative tangential <velocity>. At a moving wall this relative-<velocity> loss must be distinguished from the <power> supplied by the wall's motion. Bulk <viscous dissipation> also remains present. A finite <slip length> models reduced interfacial resistance, without declaring every boundary ideal or removing <dynamic viscosity>.

The limiting loss depends on how the flow is driven. In <Couette flow with a slipping lower wall>,
$$
\mathcal D_w=\frac{\mu U^2b}{(H+b)^2}.
$$
It tends to zero for both $b\to0$ and $b\to\infty$ at fixed $H$ and $U$. At $b=0$ impose the <no-slip boundary condition> directly, rather than dividing by zero. In <planar Poiseuille flow with equal wall slip> at fixed $G$, each wall instead dissipates $G^2bH^2/(4\mu)$, which grows with $b$ because relative <velocity> grows. The bulk loss per unit width is $G^2H^3/(12\mu)$; adding both wall losses gives $Gq$, the <pressure> work per unit channel length and width. This <energy> balance shows why small friction coefficients do not guarantee small losses under every driving constraint.

= Fluid free boundary
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

A <fluid free boundary> is an initially unknown material interface separating a fluid from its exterior. For a <polytropic elliptical patch in a shearing sheet> bounded by vacuum, it is the level set $p=\rho=Q=0$, and the velocity is tangent to it in a steady solution.

= Material surface
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$S(t)$}

A material surface is transported with its <fluid elements>, including its boundary when open. Its oriented <material surface element> evolves by tangent transport. <Magnetic flux freezing> conserves the flux through such an open surface.

= Material surface element
{parent=Material surface}
{title2=$D\mathbf A/Dt=(\nabla\cdot\mathbf u)\mathbf A-(\nabla\mathbf u)^T\mathbf A$}

If $\mathbf A=\mathbf t_a\times\mathbf t_b\,da\,db$ and each tangent satisfies $D\mathbf t/Dt=(\nabla\mathbf u)\mathbf t$, differentiation of the cross product gives $D\mathbf A/Dt=(\nabla\cdot\mathbf u)\mathbf A-(\nabla\mathbf u)^T\mathbf A$. Combining this with ideal induction proves pointwise conservation of $\mathbf B\cdot\mathbf A$.

= Material curve
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$\mathbf X(a,t)$}

= Material line
{synonym}

A material curve consists of the same <fluid elements> at all times. Differentiating their flow map gives $D\boldsymbol\ell/Dt=(\boldsymbol\ell\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u$ for its tangent. In <ideal magnetohydrodynamics> a magnetic-field tangent obeys this same transport law after division by <mass density>.

= Scalar transport
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

<Scalar transport> follows a material quantity through <advection> and <diffusion equation> terms, for example $\Theta_t+\mathbf U\cdot\nabla\Theta=\kappa\Delta\Theta$. A <passive scalar> does not affect the <velocity field>; an <active scalar> feeds back through forces such as <buoyancy>. With <incompressible flow> bounded by impermeable walls and homogeneous <Neumann boundary conditions>, the spatial mean is conserved.

= Active scalar
{parent=Scalar transport}

An <active scalar> is a transported <scalar field> that feeds back on the <velocity field>, for example a density anomaly producing <buoyancy>. In an <adjoint equations for Boussinesq scalar mixing> calculation its dynamical coupling must also be transposed; a passive-scalar sensitivity equation generally omits that feedback.

= Passive scalar
{parent=Scalar transport}

A <passive scalar> is an advected and diffusing <scalar field> whose value has no dynamical effect on the transporting <velocity field>. Concentration of a dilute nonreacting dye is a common example. Its transport can depend on the flow while the flow equations remain independent of it.

= Gaussian scalar packet in a linear incompressible flow
{c}
{parent=Passive scalar}
{title2=$\dot J=CJ+JC^T+2\kappa QI$}

For $\chi=f\exp(-\boldsymbol x^TB\boldsymbol x)$ in two dimensions, take $B$ positive definite, $\operatorname{tr}C=0$ and positive conserved mass $Q=\int\chi\,dA$. The <Gaussian integral> gives $Q=\pi f/\sqrt{\det B}$, $J=(Q/2)B^{-1}$ and $f=Q^2/(2\pi\sqrt{\det J})$. Substitution in the <advection-diffusion equation> gives $\dot B=-C^TB-BC-4\kappa B^2$ and $\dot f/f=-2\kappa\operatorname{tr}B$, equivalently the displayed moment equation. If $\dot F=CF$, its solution is $J=F[J_0+2\kappa Q\int_0^tF^{-1}(s)F^{-T}(s)ds]F^T$, with $\det F=1$.

= Diffusion arrest of Gaussian packet compression
{parent=Gaussian scalar packet in a linear incompressible flow}
{title2=$\ell_B\sim\sqrt{\kappa/\lambda}$}

For a chaotic incompressible planar flow with <Lyapunov exponent> pair $\lambda,-\lambda$, an initially broad <Gaussian scalar packet in a linear incompressible flow> stretches and compresses exponentially while diffusion is negligible. Its compressed variance stops decreasing around $\kappa/\lambda$, the <Batchelor scalar microscale> balance, while the larger variance continues to grow. In a constant principal strain, the normalized covariance eigenvalues obey $\dot j_\pm=\pm2\lambda j_\pm+2\kappa$, proving that the compressed one approaches $\kappa/\lambda$. Random finite-correlation strain instead gives a fluctuating compressed width; stationarity and integrability assumptions are required to turn this into exponential statistics of the packet amplitude.

= Large-deviation decay rates of a Gaussian scalar packet
{parent=Diffusion arrest of Gaussian packet compression}
{title2=$\gamma_\mu=\inf_{s\ge0}[\mathcal I(s)+\mu s]$}

After <diffusion arrest of Gaussian packet compression>, define the logarithmic area increase $A_t=\tfrac12\log[\det J(t)/\det J(0)]$. It is nonnegative. Assume $A_t/t$ has a <large deviation principle> with convex <rate function> $\mathcal I$, minimized at $\lambda>0$. The exact amplitude relation $f(t)=f(0)e^{-A_t}$ and the <Laplace principle for probability measures> give the displayed moment decay rate. The rate function refers to area, so rare width fluctuations are included rather than discarded without justification. For differentiable strictly convex $\mathcal I$, the interior minimizer satisfies $\mathcal I'(s_\mu)=-\mu$. If $\mu_*=-\mathcal I'(0)$ is finite, then $\gamma_\mu=\mathcal I(0)$ for $\mu\ge\mu_*$: rare nearly unstretched packets dominate high moments. Below the threshold, $d(\gamma_\mu/\mu)/d\mu=-\mathcal I(s_\mu)/\mu^2<0$. These statements concern a positive Gaussian packet with flow-only averaging; a homogeneous signed random initial scalar requires a different averaging kernel. Arbitrary random $C(t)$ alone does not imply this statistical model.

= Schmidt number
{disambiguate=fluid mechanics}
{parent=Passive scalar}
{c}
{title2=$\mathrm{Sc}=\nu/\kappa$}
{wiki=Schmidt_number}

= Molecular Schmidt number
{synonym}

The molecular Schmidt number is the ratio of <kinematic viscosity> to scalar <diffusivity>. A large value allows a <passive scalar> to develop structure below the viscous smoothing scale of the <velocity field>. It is distinct from the <turbulent Schmidt number>, which compares effective turbulent transport coefficients, and from the <Schmidt number> used for quantum entanglement.

= Covector evolution of a material scalar gradient
{parent=Passive scalar}
{title2=$D_t\nabla\theta=-(\nabla\boldsymbol u)^T\nabla\theta$}

Differentiate the <advection equation> componentwise: $D_t(\partial_i\theta)=-(\partial_i u_j)\partial_j\theta$. Along a <Lagrangian trajectory>, the scalar <gradient> therefore evolves by the negative transpose of the <velocity gradient tensor>. If $F$ is the <deformation gradient>, this says $\boldsymbol k(t)=F(t)^{-T}\boldsymbol k(0)$, as expected for a <covector>. Fluid material vectors and scalar gradients transform oppositely under stretching.

= Scalar-gradient inclination in a steady planar strain
{parent=Covector evolution of a material scalar gradient}
{title2=$\alpha\to |D|/\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$}

For horizontal strain matrix $S=\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\b&-a\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$ and zero vertical velocity, horizontal scalar gradients obey $\dot k_h=-Sk_h$. Since $S^2=c^2I$ with $c=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$, the growing projection is $(I-S/c)k_h(0)/2$. If nonzero it equals $k_0(\cos\phi,\sin\phi)$ and grows as $e^{ct}$. Fixed vertical shears give $\dot k_3=-\sigma_{13}k_1-\sigma_{23}k_2$, so $k_3\sim-k_0D e^{ct}/c$ with $D=\sigma_{13}\cos\phi+\sigma_{23}\sin\phi$. The ratio $\alpha=|k_3|/|k_h|$ tends to the displayed value. It measures scalar-isosurface orientation; zero growing projection or $D=0$ must not be described as generic exponential vertical-gradient growth.

= White-noise vertical shear gives an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck inclination
{parent=Scalar-gradient inclination in a steady planar strain}
{title2=$d\beta=-c\beta\,dt-g\,dW$}

In the long-time growing horizontal-gradient branch, independent vertical shear noises combine as $\cos\phi\,dW_1+\sin\phi\,dW_2=dW$. The signed inclination $\beta=k_3e^{-ct}/k_0$ satisfies the displayed <Ornstein-Uhlenbeck stochastic differential equation>. For $c>0$ its stationary density is $\sqrt{c/(\pi g^2)}e^{-cB^2/g^2}$, with variance $g^2/(2c)$. The <Fokker-Planck probability current> vanishes at infinity and gives this Gaussian by one integration. At $c=0$ and nonzero noise there is no normalizable stationary density.

= Gaussian-over-Rayleigh inclination distribution
{c}
{parent=Scalar-gradient inclination in a steady planar strain}
{title2=$F_\alpha(A)=\frac{\Gamma A}{\sqrt{\Gamma^2A^2+\Lambda^2}}$}

Let $c=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$ with independent $a,b\sim N(0,\Gamma^2)$, and let $D\sim N(0,\Lambda^2)$ be independent of them. Then $c$ has <Rayleigh distribution> and $\alpha=|D|/c$ has the displayed <cumulative distribution function> for $A\ge0$ and density $\Lambda^2\Gamma(\Gamma^2A^2+\Lambda^2)^{-3/2}$. Indeed, conditioning on $D$ gives $\mathbb P(c\ge|D|/A)=\exp[-D^2/(2\Gamma^2A^2)]$, and a <Gaussian integral> completes the calculation. Independence is essential: choosing $D=(\Lambda/\Gamma)a$ has the same Gaussian marginal but makes the ratio bounded by $\Lambda/\Gamma$.

= Scalar structure function
{parent=Passive scalar}
{title2=$S_C(r)=\langle[C(\boldsymbol x+\boldsymbol r)-C(\boldsymbol x)]^2\rangle$}

For a homogeneous scalar, $S_C(r)=2[\langle C^2\rangle-\langle CC'\rangle]$. Decorrelation at large separation gives saturation at twice the variance. If the scalar is mean-square differentiable and isotropic, $S_C(r)=r^2\langle|\nabla C|^2\rangle/3+o(r^2)$ at small separation. Statistical isotropy and zero mean alone do not imply decorrelation at infinity.

= Obukhov-Corrsin theory
{c}
{parent=Passive scalar}
{title2=$S_C(r)\sim\epsilon_c\epsilon^{-1/3}r^{2/3}$}

= Kolmogorov-Obukhov-Corrsin theory
{c}
{synonym}

Obukhov-Corrsin theory concerns transfer of <scalar variance> across scales in turbulent mixing. In the inertial-convective range, a constant scalar-variance flux and an eddy turnover time $\epsilon^{-1/3}r^{2/3}$ predict the displayed second-order <scalar structure function>. It assumes a passive scalar, local transfer, approximate statistical equilibrium and separation from the molecular diffusion and forcing scales.

= Inertial-diffusive scalar range
{parent=Obukhov-Corrsin theory}
{title2=$S_C(r)\simeq\epsilon_cr^2/(3\alpha)$}

For scalar diffusivity much larger than <kinematic viscosity>, scalar diffusion dominates at lengths between the Kolmogorov and scalar cutoff scales. The velocity may still have inertial-range variations, but the scalar is smooth to leading order. The leading isotropic <scalar structure function> is quadratic, with coefficient fixed by the mean-square gradient and the <scalar dissipation rate>, rather than by a continuing inertial-convective two-thirds law.

= Corrsin scalar microscale
{c}
{parent=Inertial-diffusive scalar range}
{title2=$\eta_C=(\alpha^3/\epsilon)^{1/4}$}

In the large-diffusivity regime, equate diffusion rate $\alpha/r^2$ with inertial eddy rate $\epsilon^{1/3}r^{-2/3}$. This gives the Corrsin scalar microscale, which exceeds the <Kolmogorov length scale> when $\alpha\gg\nu$.

= Viscous-convective scalar range
{parent=Obukhov-Corrsin theory}
{title2=$S_C(r)\sim\epsilon_c\sqrt{\nu/\epsilon}\log(r/\eta_B)$}

For scalar diffusivity much smaller than <kinematic viscosity>, a scalar has structure below the <Kolmogorov length scale> while the velocity is smooth. The strain rate $s\sim(\epsilon/\nu)^{1/2}$ transfers scalar variance, giving $dS_C/dr\sim\epsilon_c/(sr)$. Integration produces a logarithm plus an additive matching constant.

= Batchelor scalar microscale
{c}
{parent=Viscous-convective scalar range}
{title2=$\eta_B=(\alpha^2\nu/\epsilon)^{1/4}$}

At the Batchelor scalar microscale, molecular diffusion rate $\alpha/r^2$ balances the smooth Kolmogorov strain rate $(\epsilon/\nu)^{1/2}$. For $\alpha\ll\nu$ this scale is much smaller than the <Kolmogorov length scale>.

= Inertial-convective scalar range
{parent=Obukhov-Corrsin theory}

This range lies above both the <Kolmogorov length scale> and the scalar diffusion cutoff, but below the forcing scale. Molecular viscosity and scalar diffusion have negligible direct influence on transfer at those scales. The <Obukhov-Corrsin theory> predicts a two-thirds <scalar structure function> there.

= Horizontally integrated plume concentration
{parent=Passive scalar}
{title2=$c(z,t)=\int C(x,z,t)\,dx$}

The horizontally integrated plume concentration is the <integral> of a local <concentration> $C$ across the plume. It measures scalar amount per unit vertical length and unit span, and its vertical <integral> is total scalar amount per unit span. Unlike a local <concentration>, its dimensions include one horizontal length. It must be distinguished from <horizontally averaged plume concentration> when a plume's width varies with height.

= Horizontally averaged plume concentration
{parent=Horizontally integrated plume concentration}
{title2=$\overline C=c/w$}

For a plume of full width $w(z)$, its horizontally averaged <concentration> is $\overline C(z,t)=c(z,t)/w(z)$, where $c$ is <horizontally integrated plume concentration>. In a <line plume> with $w=\mu z$, division by $z$ generally changes the location of maximum <concentration>. A gradient closure for $c$ differs from one for $\overline C$: $w\partial_z\overline C=c_z-c/z$, so integrated diffusive flux $-Kw\partial_z\overline C$ equals $-K(c_z-c/z)$.

= Adjoint equations for Boussinesq scalar mixing
{parent=Scalar transport}

For a smooth incompressible direct trajectory with total <velocity field> $\mathbf U$ and <active scalar> $\Theta$, the negative-constraint <Lagrangian function in constrained optimization> convention gives
$$
\partial_t\mathbf u^\dagger+\mathbf U\cdot\nabla\mathbf u^\dagger-(\nabla\mathbf U)^T\mathbf u^\dagger+\nabla p^\dagger+\nu\Delta\mathbf u^\dagger-\theta^\dagger\nabla\Theta=0,\qquad \nabla\cdot\mathbf u^\dagger=0,
$$
$$
\partial_t\theta^\dagger+\mathbf U\cdot\nabla\theta^\dagger+\kappa\Delta\theta^\dagger-\mathrm{Ri}_B u_y^\dagger=0.
$$
The transpose term is the <formal adjoint> of $\delta\mathbf u\cdot\nabla\mathbf U$; the two couplings transpose <scalar transport> by <advection> and <buoyancy>. For terminal cost $\int\Theta(T)^2$, the terminal data are $\mathbf u^\dagger(T)=0$, $\theta^\dagger(T)=2\Theta(T)$. They are integrated backward along the stored direct trajectory. <No-slip boundary conditions> and <Dirichlet boundary conditions> for the adjoint <velocity> and homogeneous <Neumann boundary conditions> for the adjoint scalar remove the spatial boundary terms.

= Scalar variance
{parent=Scalar transport}
{title2=$V=|\Omega|^{-1}\int_\Omega(\Theta-\overline\Theta)^2\,dV$}

<Scalar variance> measures spatial departure from the conserved mean. For zero mean, <incompressible flow> bounded by impermeable walls and homogeneous scalar <Neumann boundary conditions>, <integration by parts> gives $d\int\Theta^2/dt=-2\kappa\int|\nabla\Theta|^2$. <Advection> preserves the instantaneous quadratic integral but can sharpen <gradients>, allowing the <diffusion equation> to remove <scalar variance> faster.

= Scalar dissipation rate
{parent=Scalar variance}
{title2=$\epsilon_c=\alpha\langle|\nabla C|^2\rangle$}

With this half-variance convention, the unforced scalar equation gives $d\langle C^2/2\rangle/dt=-\epsilon_c$ in a homogeneous incompressible flow. Authors defining dissipation of the full variance instead use $2\alpha\langle|\nabla C|^2\rangle$. Stating the convention is necessary when relating the dissipation rate to a <scalar structure function>.

= Fluid flow
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

A <fluid flow> is motion described by a <velocity field> $\mathbf u(\mathbf x,t)$. The trajectories of <fluid elements> satisfy $d\mathbf x/dt=\mathbf u(\mathbf x,t)$; the <mass density> and other fields obey their <conservation laws> and <constitutive equations>. This physical motion differs from a <flow> on a <flow network>.

= Unidirectional flow
{parent=Fluid flow}
{title2=$\boldsymbol u=u(y)\boldsymbol e_x$}

A unidirectional flow has velocity parallel to a single fixed direction, for example $\boldsymbol u=u(y)\boldsymbol e_x$. If the speed is independent of the coordinate along that direction, the convective acceleration $(\boldsymbol u\cdot\nabla)\boldsymbol u$ vanishes. The steady <Navier-Stokes equation> then reduces to a linear balance of the pressure gradient and viscosity, as in <Couette flow> and <Poiseuille flow>.

= Steady flow
{parent=Fluid flow}
{title2=$\partial_t\mathbf u=0$}

In steady flow, the velocity and other relevant flow fields are independent of time in the chosen reference frame. <Streamlines> then coincide with the paths of moving particles, while <convective acceleration> can still be nonzero.

= Inviscid flow
{parent=Fluid flow}

An inviscid flow neglects <viscosity> in the <fluid flow>. Its <momentum> balance is described by the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid>.

= Inviscid falling jet and radial impact film
{parent=Inviscid flow}
{title2=$u^2=u_0^2+2g(H-z),\quad \pi R^2u=\pi a^2u_0$}

A steady incompressible jet at atmospheric <pressure> conserves <Bernoulli's equation> and <volume flux>. Its radius contracts as $R=a[u/u_0]^{-1/2}$. After impact, a thin nearly horizontal axisymmetric film has $2\pi rhv=\pi a^2u_0$. Evaluating <Bernoulli's equation> on its <free surface> gives $v^2=u_0^2+2g(H-h)$, hence $a^4/(4r^2h^2)=1+2g(H-h)/u_0^2$. This inviscid thin-film description applies away from the impact region and does not describe a dissipative hydraulic jump.

= Sediment transport
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

= Sediment resuspension
{parent=Sediment transport}

Sediment resuspension transfers deposited bed particles into a <particle suspension>. It can supply particle <buoyancy flux> to a <particle-laden gravity current> if erosion or upward mixing exceeds settling losses. Ambient fluid without particles dilutes concentration without supplying particle mass.

= Equilibrium settling-diffusion profile
{parent=Sediment resuspension}
{title2=$\phi(z)=\phi_1(z/z_1)^{-W_s/K}$}

For downward <settling velocity> magnitude $W_s>0$ and <eddy diffusivity> $\kappa_T=Kz$, the total upward particle flux is $J=-W_s\phi-Kz\phi_z$. A stationary profile tending to zero at infinity has $J=0$, giving the displayed power law. The exponent compares settling with turbulent mixing. It describes a region above the bed; entrainment from the bed and viscous near-wall effects determine the reference concentration separately.

= Sediment transport saturation
{parent=Sediment transport}

= Equilibrium sediment flux
{parent=Sediment transport saturation}
{title2=$q_{\mathrm{sat}}$}

= Saturated sediment flux
{synonym}

= Saturated sand flux
{synonym}

The <equilibrium sediment flux> is the transport supported locally by a prescribed bed <shear stress>. A common idealization is $q_{\mathrm{sat}}=\phi_b\chi(\tau-\tau_{\mathrm{th}})_+^\gamma$. Linearization above the <sediment entrainment threshold> requires perturbations small compared with the positive excess stress.

= Saturation length
{parent=Sediment transport saturation}
{title2=$L_{\mathrm{sat}}$}

The <saturation length> measures downstream relaxation of a transported grain population toward its equilibrium <sediment transport> flux. The elementary steady relaxation law is $L_{\mathrm{sat}}q_x=q_{\mathrm{sat}}-q$; it introduces a phase lag between forcing and actual transport.

= Exner equation
{c}
{parent=Sediment transport}
{title2=$\phi_b\eta_t=-q_x$}
{wiki}

The <Exner equation> is local sediment <mass conservation> expressed in terms of solid volume: accumulation raises the bed and flux divergence lowers it. Here $\phi_b$ is the solid volume fraction of the packed bed and $q$ is solid volume flux per unit width.

= Shields parameter
{c}
{parent=Sediment transport}
{title2=$\Theta$}
{wiki}

= Shields number
{c}
{synonym}

The <Shields parameter> is $\Theta=\tau/[(\rho_p-\rho_f)gd]$, comparing bed <shear stress> with a grain-scale <submerged weight> per area. Its critical value depends on the assumed <drag coefficient>, <static friction>, lift, and contact geometry.

= Sediment entrainment threshold
{parent=Shields parameter}
{title2=$\tau_{\mathrm{th}}$}

= Threshold shear stress
{synonym}

The <sediment entrainment threshold> is the bed <shear stress> at which resting grains begin moving. An elementary <force balance> using spherical grains, tangential <quadratic drag>, and <static friction> gives $\Theta_{\mathrm{th},0}=4\mu_s/(3C_D)$ when the <drag coefficient> is defined relative to the bed <shear velocity>.

= Inclined-bed sediment threshold
{parent=Sediment entrainment threshold}

For locally bed-tangent <drag force> and an upslope angle $a$, a spherical-grain <force balance> gives $\tau_{\mathrm{th}}(a)=\tau_{\mathrm{th},0}(\cos a+\sin a/\mu_s)$. The small-slope term is $\delta\tau_{\mathrm{th}}=(\tau_{\mathrm{th},0}/\mu_s)\eta_x$. A horizontal <drag force> instead changes the normal contact <force> and gives a different slope correction.

= Bedform
{parent=Sediment transport}
{wiki}

= Sediment-bed linear instability
{parent=Bedform}

Combining the <Exner equation>, <sediment transport saturation>, <bed shear response>, and <inclined-bed sediment threshold> gives a competition between upstream forcing and downstream relaxation. With $a=\tau_0A$, $b=\tau_0B-\tau_{\mathrm{th},0}/\mu_s$, $C=\chi\gamma(\tau_0-\tau_{\mathrm{th},0})^{\gamma-1}$, and positive <wavenumber> $k$, the <growth rate> is $\sigma=Ck^2(b-akL_{\mathrm{sat}})/(1+k^2L_{\mathrm{sat}}^2)$. Thus $b>0$ permits long-wave growth when $a>0$, while a finite <saturation length> stabilizes shorter waves.

= Bed shear response
{parent=Bedform}

For a small sinusoidal <bedform>, a fluid-dynamical closure can be written $\hat\tau=\tau_0k(A+iB)\hat\eta$ for $k>0$. The real coefficient $A$ is in phase with the bed height and $B$ describes an upstream phase lead. Neither coefficient follows from the <Exner equation> or sediment relaxation alone; they require a flow calculation or measurement.

= Ram pressure
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$P_{\rm ram}\sim\rho v^2$}
{wiki}

The momentum-flux <pressure> associated with a moving fluid. Some energy-density balances use $\rho v^2/2$ instead; the numerical convention must be stated when balancing <magnetic pressure>.

\Include[turbulence]{parent=Fluid mechanics}

= Acoustic wave
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

An acoustic wave is a propagating pressure and density disturbance in a material medium. Linear sound waves oscillate when pressure restores a displaced fluid element.

= Burgers reduction of weakly nonlinear rightgoing acoustics
{c}
{parent=Acoustic wave}
{title2=$p_X-\tfrac{\gamma+1}{2}p\,p_\theta=\tfrac{\beta}{2M}p_{\theta\theta}$}

For a pure rightgoing <acoustic wave>, retarded time $\theta=t-x$ and slow distance $X=Mx$ separate fast oscillations from nonlinear steepening. Expanding the <equation of state> gives $p=\rho+M(\gamma-1)\rho^2/2+O(M^2)$. The leading perturbations satisfy $u_0=\rho_0=p_0$. Adding the first-order <mass conservation> and <momentum conservation> equations cancels the unknown first corrections and yields the displayed <viscous Burgers equation>. Inviscid characteristics satisfy $\theta=s-(\gamma+1)F(s)X/2$, $p=F(s)$; loss of invertibility marks <shock> formation and the end of the smooth inviscid approximation.

= Adiabatic vertical modes of a Gaussian atmosphere
{parent=Acoustic wave}
{title2=$\sigma_n^2=\Omega^2[1+\gamma(2n+1)]$}

A <plane-parallel atmosphere> with <isothermal sound speed> $c_s$ in gravity $-\Omega^2z$ has <mass density> $\rho_0e^{-z^2/(2H^2)}$, $H=c_s/\Omega$. Above a rigid base, vertically directed <adiabatic fluid perturbations> satisfy $w''-zw'/H^2+(\sigma^2/\Omega^2-1)w/(\gamma H^2)=0$ and $w(0)=0$. Set $x=z/H$ and $\lambda=(\sigma^2/\Omega^2-1)/\gamma$. The substitution $y=e^{-x^2/4}w$ converts finite Gaussian-weighted <kinetic energy> into square integrability for $-y''+x^2y/4=(\lambda+1/2)y$. Odd extension across the base and the oscillator lowering operator force $\lambda$ to be an odd positive integer. Thus $w_n$ is proportional to the <probabilists' Hermite polynomial> $\mathrm{He}_{2n+1}(z/H)$. These are vertical <acoustic waves>; a general stably stratified atmosphere also admits <internal gravity waves> with horizontal structure.

= Acoustically rigid boundary
{parent=Acoustic wave}
{title2=$v_n=0$}

= Acoustically rigid
{synonym}

An acoustically rigid boundary has zero perturbation <normal velocity>. For a reflected <acoustic plane wave>, <pressure> amplitudes add in phase and the <reflection coefficient> is $+1$. It is the infinite-<surface acoustic impedance> limit. A <pressure-release boundary> instead fixes the <pressure> perturbation to zero and reflects with coefficient $-1$.

= Acoustic cutoff frequency
{parent=Acoustic wave}
{title2=$\omega_{\rm ac}\simeq c_s/(2H_\rho)$}

A stratified atmosphere can reflect low-frequency <acoustic waves> while permitting sufficiently high-frequency waves to escape. In a plane-parallel isothermal atmosphere, the angular acoustic cutoff is approximately $c_s/(2H_\rho)$, where $H_\rho$ is the density scale height. Its detailed form depends on the atmospheric profile and on the wave-variable approximation.

= Pressure-release boundary
{parent=Acoustic wave}
{title2=$\widetilde p=0$}

A pressure-release boundary imposes zero acoustic pressure disturbance, $\widetilde p=0$. The incident and reflected pressure amplitudes therefore obey $1+R=0$, so the pressure <reflection coefficient> is $R=-1$. It contrasts with a rigid inviscid boundary, where zero normal velocity gives $R=+1$. A much lower normal impedance on the far side approximates the pressure-release condition.

= Velocity field
{title2=$\mathbf u(\mathbf x,t)$}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

A velocity field assigns a velocity vector to every position and time in a moving continuum. Its spatial derivatives describe local deformation, rotation, and volume change.

= Secondary flow
{parent=Velocity field}

A secondary flow is a usually smaller velocity component superposed on a primary flow selected by symmetry or a simplified force balance. Examples include meridional circulation around a rotating sphere, or transverse circulation in a curved channel. Its direction depends on the forcing mechanism: elastic <normal stresses> and inertial effects can produce opposite circulations.

= Velocity gradient tensor
{parent=Velocity field}
{title2=$A_{ij}=\partial_j u_i$}

The <velocity gradient tensor> is $A_{ij}=\partial_j u_i$. Its symmetric part is the <rate-of-strain tensor> $S$ and its antisymmetric part describes local rotation. For <incompressible flow>, $\operatorname{tr}A=\operatorname{tr}S=0$. The sign convention for a cubic invariant must be specified explicitly.

= Vertical velocity
{parent=Velocity field}
{title2=$w=\boldsymbol u\cdot\widehat{\boldsymbol z}$}

The vertical velocity is the component of a fluid <velocity field> along the selected upward vertical direction. In <incompressible flow>, impermeable horizontal boundaries impose $w=0$. Eliminating <pressure> often yields a closed <hydrodynamic stability> equation for this component.

= Axisymmetric flow
{parent=Velocity field}

An axisymmetric flow is invariant under rotations about one fixed axis, so scalar fields and cylindrical-coordinate velocity components do not depend on the azimuthal angle.

= Advection
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

Advection transports a quantity with a <velocity field>. For a scalar field $f$, its local advective rate is $\mathbf u\mathbin\cdot\nabla f$, the spatial contribution to the <material derivative>. The same term transports the components of a vector field.

= Chaotic advection
{parent=Advection}

Chaotic advection is complicated, sensitive material motion generated by a smooth <velocity field>, often even when the Eulerian flow is periodic and simple. Transverse intersections of <stable manifolds> and <unstable manifolds> create stretching, folding and lobe transport across former <separatrices>. <Incompressible flow> preserves parcel area and advected scalar values in the absence of <diffusion>, so chaotic motion can create fine filaments without independently producing molecular homogenization.

= Ballistic transport
{parent=Advection}
{title2=$\operatorname{Var}X(t)\propto t^2$}

A trajectory with a persistent random velocity $V$ has $X(t)-X(0)=Vt$. An ensemble with finite velocity variance therefore has displacement variance $t^2\operatorname{Var}V$, contrasting with the linear-in-time variance of <diffusion>. In an <impulsively kicked sinusoidal shear dispersion> model with zero transverse diffusivity, the shear phase never changes and repeated identical kicks have this ballistic scaling. Transverse diffusion instead decorrelates the velocity and permits a long-time diffusive limit.

= Convection
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

Convection transports properties such as <energy> or composition through bulk fluid motion. <Thermal convection> is driven by temperature-dependent <buoyancy>.

= Convection cell
{parent=Convection}

An organized region of buoyant rising fluid and compensating descending flow that transports heat or composition. <Convection rolls> are a simple planar example; <solar granules> are evolving compressible examples. A cellular pattern does not require every material trajectory to form a closed streamline, especially near a radiating free surface.

= Convection zone
{parent=Convection}

A stellar <convection zone> is a region where buoyant fluid motion transports a significant part of the stellar heat flux. Rising and sinking parcels carry enthalpy, so heat transport is not entirely radiative. The <Sun>'s outer <convection zone> extends from near $0.7R_\odot$ to the surface and overlies its radiative interior. The <tachocline> lies near this transition, and the <convection> helps power the <solar dynamo>.

= Compositional convection
{parent=Convection}

Compositional convection is buoyancy-driven motion caused by composition-dependent density variations. In a planetary core, freezing can expel light elements into the surrounding liquid, making that liquid buoyant and supplying mechanical power for a <planetary dynamo>. It can operate alongside thermally driven <convection> and release of <latent heat>.

= Long-wave convection equation with broken Boussinesq symmetry
{parent=Convection}
{title2=$\Theta_t=-\Theta-\mu\Theta_{xx}-\Theta_{xxxx}-s(\Theta_x^2)_x+(\Theta_x^3)_x$}

A reduced long-wave <convection> model can combine linear damping, destabilizing second derivatives, stabilizing fourth derivatives and gradient nonlinearities. In the displayed normalization the quadratic coefficient $s$ breaks <Boussinesq symmetry>, while the cubic gradient term dissipates the mean-square temperature. The linear <dispersion relation> is $\lambda(k)=-1+\mu k^2-k^4$, with critical <wavenumber> $k=1$ at $\mu=2$. Smoothness and spatial averaging hypotheses must be specified before applying the <energy method> on an infinite interval.

= Second-harmonic feedback in a long-wave convection amplitude equation
{parent=Long-wave convection equation with broken Boussinesq symmetry}
{title2=$g=3-8s^2/9$}

In a <weakly nonlinear expansion> of the <long-wave convection equation with broken Boussinesq symmetry> at $\mu=2$, a critical <Fourier mode> $\Theta_1=Ae^{ix}+\mathrm{c.c.}$ generates $\Theta_2=(2is/9)A^2e^{2ix}+\mathrm{c.c.}$. The quadratic interaction of these first and second harmonics feeds back $8s^2|A|^2A/9$ into the critical <Fourier mode>, while the cubic gradient nonlinearity contributes $-3|A|^2A$. The <Fredholm solvability condition> is consequently $\mu_2A-g|A|^2A=0$. The sign of $g$ distinguishes supercritical and subcritical branches; $g=0$ requires a higher-order <amplitude equation>.

= Energy square completion for long-wave convection
{parent=Long-wave convection equation with broken Boussinesq symmetry}
{title2=$\mu_E=2-s^2/4$}

For a real smooth solution of the <long-wave convection equation with broken Boussinesq symmetry>, assume a periodic spatial average or an existing long-interval average with vanishing endpoint fluxes. <Integration by parts> gives the exact <energy method> identity
$$
\frac12\partial_t\langle\Theta^2\rangle
=-\langle(\Theta+\Theta_{xx})^2\rangle
-\langle\Theta_x^2(\Theta_x-s/2)^2\rangle
+(\mu-2+s^2/4)\langle\Theta_x^2\rangle.
$$
Thus $\mu<\mu_E$ prevents growth of the mean-square temperature at arbitrary amplitude in this averaging class. This sufficient nonlinear bound need not equal the linear instability threshold, and it does not imply pointwise monotonicity of the temperature.

= Thermal convection
{parent=Convection}

Thermal convection is fluid motion driven by temperature-dependent <buoyancy>. It transports <heat> by bulk motion in addition to <thermal conduction>.

= Horizontal convection
{parent=Thermal convection}
{wiki}

Horizontal convection is <buoyancy>-driven circulation forced by lateral variations of <temperature> along one horizontal boundary. Hot and cold surface regions drive a circulation even though all thermal forcing is applied at the same height. An insulated bottom and sides require zero net heat exchange through the remaining surface in steady state, but permit heat to enter its warmer region and leave its colder region.

= Shallow horizontal convection with an insulated bottom
{parent=Horizontal convection}
{title2=$\psi=-\operatorname{Ra}\epsilon^4y(1-y)^2(2y+1)/48$}

For downward depth $z=\epsilon y$, take $(u,w)=(\psi_z,-\psi_x)$, surface temperature $T=x$, a flat impermeable stress-free upper boundary and a no-slip insulated bottom. In the steady infinite-<Prandtl number> thin-layer interior, $\psi_{zzzz}=-\operatorname{Ra}$ with $\psi=\psi_{zz}=0$ at the surface and $\psi=\psi_z=0$ at the bottom. The displayed profile follows. Sidewall end layers close the circulation; the interior profile must not be imposed unchanged at those walls.

= Advection correction in shallow horizontal convection
{parent=Shallow horizontal convection with an insulated bottom}
{title2=$T-x=-\operatorname{Ra}\epsilon^5(2y^5/5-3y^4/4+y^2/2)/48$}

The interior first temperature correction obeys $\Theta_{zz}=u=\psi_z$, $\Theta(0)=0$ and $\Theta_z(\epsilon)=0$. Hence $\Theta_z=\psi$, which integrates to the displayed polynomial. Its contribution to depth-integrated horizontal advective <heat flux> is $\int u\Theta\,dz=-\int\psi^2dz=-19\operatorname{Ra}^2\epsilon^9/1451520$. The leading conductive flux is $-\epsilon$. These statements apply away from the sidewall end layers at fixed Rayleigh number as the layer becomes shallow.

= Forced convection
{parent=Thermal convection}
{wiki}

Heat-carrying fluid motion dominated by an externally imposed flow or shear. Buoyancy may still influence it; forced and free contributions can coexist. In surface-layer scaling, $z\ll|\ell_O|$ is predominantly shear-driven, while $z\gg|\ell_O|$ is predominantly buoyancy-driven for lower-boundary heating.

= Natural convection
{parent=Thermal convection}
{wiki}

= Free convection
{synonym}

Convection driven primarily by buoyancy generated by heating or cooling, rather than an externally imposed mean flow. In a constant-flux unstable surface layer, the local speed scales as $(B_0z)^{1/3}$.

= Free-convection surface-layer scaling
{parent=Natural convection}
{title2=$w_c\sim(B_0z)^{1/3},\quad\epsilon\sim B_0$}

For a positive approximately constant <vertical turbulent buoyancy flux>, dimensional turnover scaling gives $K_b\sim B_0^{1/3}z^{4/3}$ and $N^2\sim-B_0^{2/3}z^{-4/3}$. The negative squared frequency describes unstable overturning, not a real restoring oscillation. The leading dissipation is height-independent, unlike neutral wall turbulence.

= Hexagonal convection amplitude equations
{parent=Thermal convection}
{title2=$A_T=\mu_2KA+\gamma K\bar B\bar C-3K^2A\sum|A_j|^2$}

A finite-<wavenumber> convection instability with a <resonant three-wave triad> can yield three coupled <amplitude equations>. For the gradient nonlinearity $\nabla\cdot(|\nabla\Theta|^2\nabla\Theta)$ and weak quadratic asymmetry $-\epsilon\gamma\nabla\cdot(\Theta\nabla\Theta)$, projection onto a triad of length squared $K$ gives the stated equation and its cyclic copies. The quadratic coefficient is $+\gamma K$, while both self and cross cubic coefficients are $-3K^2$ for angles of $120$ degrees. The finite critical mode requires positive linear damping in the underlying long-wave equation.

= Rotational hexagon amplitude equations
{parent=Hexagonal convection amplitude equations}

For a <resonant three-wave triad> with rotational and translational <symmetry> but no spatial <reflection>, the cubic <amplitude equations> have the cyclic form $\dot A=\mu A+\overline B\,\overline C-\nu_1|A|^2A-(\nu_2+\delta)|B|^2A-(\nu_2-\delta)|C|^2A$. A half-turn induces <complex conjugation>, so all coefficients are real. The coefficient $\delta$ distinguishes the two cyclic cross-couplings that a <reflection> would interchange.

= Roll and hexagon spectra without reflection symmetry
{parent=Rotational hexagon amplitude equations}

For the <rotational hexagon amplitude equations>, put $d=\nu_2-\nu_1$ and $N=\nu_1+2\nu_2$. A roll with $r^2=\mu/\nu_1$ has transverse <eigenvalues> $-dr^2\pm\sqrt{\delta^2r^4+r^2}$, each twice, radial <eigenvalue> $-2\mu$, and one neutral translation. A hexagon with $\mu=Nh^2-h$ has real-amplitude <eigenvalues> $h-2Nh^2$ and $-2h+2dh^2\pm2i\sqrt3\delta h^2$; its imaginary-amplitude <eigenvalues> are $-3h,0,0$. These follow by diagonalizing the cyclic <Jacobian matrix>. Stability is understood modulo translations. If $\nu_2>\nu_1>0$ and $\delta\ne0$, the upper positive hexagon branch reaches a <Hopf bifurcation> at $h=1/d$, $\mu=(2\nu_1+\nu_2)/d^2$.

= Axial isotropy of rolls and hexagons
{parent=Rotational hexagon amplitude equations}

Write the translation <group action> as $(A,B,C)\mapsto(e^{i\theta_1}A,e^{i\theta_2}B,e^{-i(\theta_1+\theta_2)}C)$. Modulo lattice translations, the <stabilizer subgroup> of $(h,h,h)$, with nonzero real $h$, is the sixfold rotation group. Its <fixed-point subspace of a group action> is the real line $(a,a,a)$. The <stabilizer subgroup> of $(r,0,0)$, with nonzero real $r$, consists of the circle $\theta_1=0$ together with a half-turn; its <fixed-point subspace of a group action> is the real line $(a,0,0)$. The <equivariant branching lemma> therefore supplies generic branches of both axial types.

= Roll instability from broken up-down symmetry
{parent=Hexagonal convection amplitude equations}
{title2=$\lambda_\perp=\pm|\gamma|K|A|$}

In the <hexagonal convection amplitude equations>, a pure <convection roll> has $|A|^2=\mu_2/(3K)$ and $B=C=0$. The transverse diagonal linear growth cancels because self and cross cubic coefficients coincide. Broken up-down symmetry leaves the off-diagonal coupling $\gamma K A$, giving eigenvalues $\pm|\gamma|K|A|$ and an unstable orientation perturbation whenever $\gamma\ne0$. With zero quadratic coupling these directions are neutral at the retained order, so that degenerate case cannot be declared strictly unstable by this calculation.

= Convection roll
{parent=Thermal convection}

A <convection roll> is a circulating cell pattern whose ideal <velocity> and <temperature> fields are periodic across a chosen horizontal direction and independent of the coordinate along the <convection roll> axis. In a two-dimensional <stream function> representation, its <velocity field> is $(-\psi_z,0,\psi_x)$. <Rayleigh-Bénard convection> can select stationary or oscillatory <convection rolls>; a two-dimensional <convection roll> may be unstable to three-dimensional disturbances, a different <convection roll> orientation, or slow amplitude and phase modulations. Existence of a saturated <Landau amplitude equation> branch does not alone establish its stability.

= Oscillatory convection
{parent=Thermal convection}

<oscillatory convection> is time-dependent convection emerging through nonzero-frequency <overstability> or a <Hopf bifurcation>. At onset a complex-conjugate growth-rate pair crosses the imaginary axis. Travelling and standing <convection rolls> are distinct nonlinear possibilities, and their stability cannot be decided from the linear <angular frequency> alone.

= Travelling and standing convection rolls
{parent=Oscillatory convection}

= Traveling and standing convection rolls
{synonym}

At fixed orientation, opposite travelling amplitudes can obey $\dot Z_\pm=(r+i\omega_0)Z_\pm-(g|Z_\pm|^2+h|Z_\mp|^2)Z_\pm$. For $r>0$ and $g_r=\operatorname{Re}g>0$, a travelling branch has one nonzero intensity $r/g_r$ and is stable to its counter-propagating amplitude when $h_r>g_r$. A standing branch has both intensities $r/(g_r+h_r)$ and is stable within the pair when $g_r>h_r$ and $g_r+h_r>0$. These follow by linearizing the real intensity equations. Spatial modulation and other orientations remain separate stability tests.

= Stationary convection
{parent=Thermal convection}

= Steady convection
{synonym}

<stationary convection> has a time-independent <velocity> and <temperature> field, in the chosen fixed frame. Its onset from a conduction state corresponds to a real growth rate passing through zero. Stability of a resulting steady <convection roll> is a separate nonlinear question.

= Convective stability
{parent=Thermal convection}
{title2=$N^2>0$}

A stable stratification gives a restoring <buoyancy> force and positive squared <buoyancy frequency> for an adiabatically displaced parcel. An adverse <specific entropy> gradient can instead drive <convection>; other forces such as rotation can modify the full stability criterion.

= Convective overshoot
{parent=Thermal convection}
{wiki}

= Convective overshooting
{synonym}

Convective overshoot carries material from an unstable convecting region into an adjacent stably stratified region because its <momentum> does not vanish at the stability boundary. In stars this mixes material beyond the boundary determined by a local <stellar convective stability> criterion, changing stellar core masses and nuclear lifetimes.

= Double-diffusive convection
{parent=Thermal convection}
{wiki}

Double-diffusive convection occurs when two properties, such as <temperature> and composition, affect <buoyancy> but diffuse at different rates. A stabilizing composition gradient can oppose a destabilizing thermal gradient and change the form of heat transport.

= Thermosolutal convection
{parent=Double-diffusive convection}

<Thermosolutal convection> is <double-diffusive convection> driven by temperature and concentration gradients. Their buoyancy contributions can oppose one another, while unequal <thermal diffusivity> and <solutal diffusivity> allow steady, oscillatory and finite-amplitude states. For a layer heated from below with a stabilizing concentration gradient, thermal buoyancy is destabilizing and solutal buoyancy restoring.

= Double-diffusive overflow reservoir
{parent=Thermosolutal convection}
{title2=$\theta_s=C(1-\theta_s)^{10/3}/(1-\eta_s)^2$}

A steady well-mixed hot salty reservoir receives volume flux $Q$ and loses the same flux over a weir. Heat and salt balances are $Q(T_i-T)=AF_T$ and $Q(S_i-S)=AF_S$. If $\beta F_S/(\alpha F_T)=R_F$ and $\alpha F_T=b(\alpha\Delta T)^{4/3}/R_\rho^2$, define $R_0=\beta(S_i-S_\infty)/[\alpha(T_i-T_\infty)]$. Then $\eta_s=(R_F/R_0)\theta_s$ and $C=Ab[\alpha(T_i-T_\infty)]^{1/3}/(Q R_0^2)$. For $R_0>1$ and $0\leq R_F<1$ the interface is statically stable while preferential heat transfer can drive <double-diffusive convection>.

= Post-shutdown double-diffusive reservoir cooling
{parent=Double-diffusive overflow reservoir}
{title2=$\eta=q\theta,\quad q=R_F/R_0$}

After inflow and overflow cease, a fixed-volume reservoir initially in the preceding steady state obeys $\eta=q\theta$ and $\dot\theta=k(1-\theta)^{10/3}/(1-q\theta)^2$, with $k=Ab[\alpha(T_i-T_\infty)]^{1/3}/(V R_0^2)$. Integrating gives $k(t-t_0)=G(\theta)-G(\theta_s)$, where $G=3(1-q)^2(1-\theta)^{-7/3}/7+3q(1-q)(1-\theta)^{-4/3}/2+3q^2(1-\theta)^{-1/3}$. For $q<1$, heat contrast tends to zero only at infinite time, while a nonzero salt contrast remains.

= Lorenz model of thermosolutal convection
{c}
{parent=Thermosolutal convection}
{title2=$a,b,c,d,e$}

A five-mode <Galerkin method> model retains the roll velocity $a$, its correlated temperature and concentration modes $b,d$, and the mean-profile distortions $c,e$. Its parameters include the <Prandtl number> $\sigma$, diffusivity ratio $\tau$, thermal forcing $r$, stabilizing solutal forcing $r_s$, and a positive geometric decay ratio $\varpi$. The velocity equation is $\dot a=\sigma(-a+rb-r_sd)$, while the scalar equations are $\dot b=-b+a(1-c)$, $\dot c=\varpi(-c+ab)$, $\dot d=-\tau d+a(1-e)$ and $\dot e=\varpi(-\tau e+ad)$. The solutal forcing is normalized using the thermal diffusion scale, so its steady stabilizing effect is $r_s/\tau$.

= Oscillatory threshold of a thermosolutal Lorenz model
{parent=Lorenz model of thermosolutal convection}
{title2=$r_H=1+\tau(\sigma+1+\tau)/\sigma+r_s(\sigma+\tau)/(\sigma+1)$}

Linearization of the <Lorenz model of thermosolutal convection> at conduction yields $(\lambda+\sigma)(\lambda+1)(\lambda+\tau)-\sigma r(\lambda+\tau)+\sigma r_s(\lambda+1)=0$. A <Hopf bifurcation> requires the cubic coefficient identity $a_3=a_1a_2$ and positive frequency squared. Its threshold is the stated $r_H$, with $\omega^2=\sigma r_s(1-\tau)/(\sigma+1)-\tau^2>0$. If $r_s=\beta^2\tau$ and $\delta=\sigma/(1+\sigma)$, then $r_H=1+\tau/\delta+\beta^2\tau\delta+O(\tau^2)$. Comparing with the <steady fold of a thermosolutal Lorenz model> gives a leading nonnegative difference $\tau(\beta\delta-1)^2/\delta$ for $\beta\ge0$.

= Steady fold of a thermosolutal Lorenz model
{parent=Lorenz model of thermosolutal convection}
{title2=$r_{\rm min}=(\sqrt{r_s\tau}+\sqrt{1-\tau^2})^2$}

A nonzero steady <Lorenz model of thermosolutal convection> branch obeys $r(x)=(1+x)+r_s\tau(1+x)/(\tau^2+x)$, with $x=a^2$. For $0<\tau<1$, it has an interior <saddle-node bifurcation> precisely when $r_s>\tau^3/(1-\tau^2)$. Differentiating gives $x_*=-\tau^2+\sqrt{r_s\tau(1-\tau^2)}$ and the stated minimum. The weaker-versus-stronger threshold distinction matters: a sufficient bound need not be the sharp subcriticality condition. Steady-branch existence alone does not decide oscillatory stability.

= Layered convection
{parent=Double-diffusive convection}

Layered convection consists of mixed convecting layers separated by more weakly transporting interfaces. In a giant planet, composition gradients can support such interfaces and reduce heat loss, contributing to <delayed cooling of an inflated giant planet>.

= Buoyancy
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$F_b$}
{wiki}

= Buoyant force
{synonym}

<Buoyancy> is the resultant upward <force> exerted by a resting <fluid> on an immersed body in a downward <gravitational field>. The <fluid> pushes inward, perpendicular to each part of the body's surface. Because <hydrostatic pressure> increases with depth, the upward push from below exceeds the downward push from above. Opposite horizontal <forces> cancel; <buoyancy> is the remaining vertical <force>. A sinking body still receives this upward support. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-4-archimedes-principle-and-buoyancy[OpenStax, University Physics, §14.4: origin of buoyancy]{ref}

More precisely, <pressure> $p$ on a small surface of <area> $dA$ with outward unit <normal vector> $\mathbf n$ exerts
$$
d\mathbf F=-p\mathbf n\,dA.
$$
The minus sign says the <force> points into the body. On a <cube>, the top <force> points down, the bottom <force> points up, and the side <forces> cancel in pairs because opposite sides have the same <pressure> at each depth. This is a surface <traction>; <gravity> on the body itself is a separate <body force>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-016-hydrodynamics-13-012-fall-2005/dc299d2ff05553b3515dccc23fc0f7d2_2005reading1.pdf[MIT, A. Techet, Hydrodynamics reading 1, p. 5: pressure traction]{ref}

For a computed example, stipulate resting <water> with constant <mass density> $\rho_f=1000\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ and uniform <gravitational acceleration> $g=9.81\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. A fully immersed <cube> has side $L=0.10\ \mathrm m$, top depth $h_t=0.20\ \mathrm m$ and bottom depth $h_b=0.30\ \mathrm m$. Each horizontal face has <area> $A=L^2=0.010\ \mathrm{m^2}$, and its <volume> is $V=L^3=0.001\ \mathrm{m^3}$. Its top and bottom <gauge pressures> are
$$
p_{g,t}=\rho_fgh_t=1962\ \mathrm{Pa},\qquad
p_{g,b}=\rho_fgh_b=2943\ \mathrm{Pa}.
$$
The corresponding <forces> relative to the common surface <pressure> are $p_{g,t}A=19.62\ \mathrm N$ downward and $p_{g,b}A=29.43\ \mathrm N$ upward. Therefore the net <buoyancy> is
$$
F_b=(p_{g,b}-p_{g,t})A=9.81\ \mathrm N.
$$
Using absolute <pressures> adds the same $p_{\mathrm{surface}}A$ to both face <forces>, leaving their difference unchanged. Zero <gauge pressure> at the surface does not mean zero absolute <pressure>. Increasing both depths by the same amount also leaves <buoyancy> unchanged in this constant-<mass density> model.

\Image[hydrostatic-pressure-and-buoyancy.png]
{title=How a hydrostatic pressure difference supports an immersed cube}
{description=Left: a fully immersed <cube> of side $0.10\ \mathrm m$ has its top at depth $0.20\ \mathrm m$ and bottom at $0.30\ \mathrm m$. The inward <pressure> <forces> on its sides cancel; its horizontal faces have <area> $0.010\ \mathrm{m^2}$. The displayed face <forces> use <gauge pressure>, whose common surface offset cancels in the net $9.81\ \mathrm N$ upward <buoyancy>. Right: <gauge pressure> increases linearly with depth, marking $1962\ \mathrm{Pa}$ at the top and $2943\ \mathrm{Pa}$ at the bottom.}

<Archimedes' principle> turns this calculation into a rule for bodies of any shape. It explains <floating equilibrium> and lets us infer an immersed body's <volume> from the difference between its <force> from <gravity> and the <force> needed to support it. In particular, <buoyancy> depends on the displaced <volume> and the surrounding <mass density>, whereas the body's own <mass density> decides how its <force> from <gravity> compares with that support.

= Buoyancy gradient
{parent=Buoyancy}
{title2=$\nabla b$}

The spatial <gradient> of <buoyancy> determines the buoyancy anomaly produced by a small parcel <displacement>: $b'=-\boldsymbol\xi\cdot\nabla\bar b$. Its vertical component is the squared <buoyancy frequency> in a stably stratified <Boussinesq approximation>. A horizontal component enters <thermal wind> and the propagation of a <boundary Rossby wave>.

= Magnetic buoyancy
{parent=Buoyancy}
{title2=$p_{\rm in}+B^2/(2\mu_0)=p_{\rm out}$}

<Magnetic buoyancy> is the tendency of magnetized fluid to rise when <magnetic pressure> support reduces its density relative to its surroundings. For a thin flux concentration in lateral balance, $p_{\rm in}+B^2/(2\mu_0)=p_{\rm out}$. At equal <temperature> and composition an ideal-gas equation of state then gives $\rho_{\rm in}<\rho_{\rm out}$, producing an upward buoyant force. Thermal exchange, <magnetic tension> and stable stratification modify this simple argument. In a <solar dynamo>, buoyant toroidal flux can emerge as tilted bipolar active regions.

= Archimedes' principle
{c}
{parent=Buoyancy}
{title2=$F_b=\rho_fgV_{\mathrm{displaced}}$}
{wiki}

<Archimedes' principle> states that <buoyancy> equals the downward <gravitational weight>, or weight, of the displaced <fluid>. In a resting <fluid> of constant <mass density> $\rho_f$ under uniform <gravitational acceleration> $g$, its magnitude is
$$
F_b=\rho_fgV_{\mathrm{displaced}}.
$$
Here $V_{\mathrm{displaced}}$ is the <volume> from which the body excludes the <fluid>: the whole external <volume> for a fully immersed solid body, and just the immersed <volume> for a partly immersed one. The body's own <mass> does not enter this <buoyancy> formula. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-4-archimedes-principle-and-buoyancy[OpenStax, University Physics, §14.4: Archimedes' principle]{ref}

A short proof outline for a fully immersed body is to imagine replacing it by exactly the <fluid> it displaces. The surrounding <hydrostatic pressure> is unchanged. That replacement <fluid> is at rest, so its upward <pressure> <force> must balance its downward <force> from <gravity>. The original body receives the same inward <pressure> <forces> on the same closed surface, hence the same upward <buoyancy>.

Equivalently, let $\Omega$ be the displaced region, $\partial\Omega$ its closed surface and $\mathbf e_z$ the upward unit direction. The <divergence theorem>, applied component by component to the <surface integral>, and <hydrostatic pressure> give
$$
\mathbf F_b=-\int_{\partial\Omega}p\mathbf n\,dA
=-\int_\Omega\nabla p\,dV
=\rho_fgV_{\mathrm{displaced}}\mathbf e_z.
$$
This also explains why horizontal <forces> cancel even for an irregular shape. A spatially constant surface <pressure> contributes zero to the closed-surface <integral>. If the <mass density> varies with depth, replace $\rho_fV_{\mathrm{displaced}}$ by the <mass> $\int_\Omega\rho_f\,dV$ of the displaced <fluid>.

The closed-surface argument assumes that the <fluid> applies <pressure> over the entire boundary of a fully immersed body. A body sealing against a tank bottom may have no <fluid> beneath it; then the lower-face <pressure> <force> used in the proof is absent, and a supporting contact <force> must be treated separately. For partial immersion, the usual <buoyancy> formula assumes the exposed surface receives the common atmospheric <pressure>, neglects the much smaller <buoyancy> from the air, and neglects <surface tension>.

For the $0.10\ \mathrm m$ <cube> in the stipulated <water>, full immersion displaces $0.001\ \mathrm{m^3}$, so $F_b=1000\times9.81\times0.001=9.81\ \mathrm N$. If its uniform <mass density> is $600\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, its <mass> is $m=600\times0.001=0.600\ \mathrm{kg}$ and its downward <force> from <gravity> is $mg=5.886\ \mathrm N$. To hold it fully immersed at rest requires an additional downward <force>
$$
F_{\mathrm{hold}}=F_b-mg=9.81-5.886=3.924\ \mathrm N.
$$
On release from rest it initially moves upward; its eventual <floating equilibrium> has a smaller displaced <volume>. A same-size <cube> of uniform <mass density> $1200\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ has <mass> $1.200\ \mathrm{kg}$ and downward <force> from <gravity> $11.772\ \mathrm N$. Full immersion still gives $9.81\ \mathrm N$ of <buoyancy>, leaving an initial net downward <force> of $11.772-9.81=1.962\ \mathrm N$. Subsequent motion also involves <drag>.

= Floating equilibrium
{parent=Archimedes' principle}
{title2=$V_{\mathrm{displaced}}/V_{\mathrm{body}}=\rho_{\mathrm{body}}/\rho_f$}

A freely floating body at rest has no supporting contact or applied holding <force>. Neglecting <surface tension> and the <buoyancy> of air, its upward <buoyancy> balances its downward <force> from <gravity>:
$$
F_b=mg.
$$
For a body of uniform <mass density> $\rho_{\mathrm{body}}$ and total <volume> $V_{\mathrm{body}}$, its <mass> is $m=\rho_{\mathrm{body}}V_{\mathrm{body}}$. Substitution into <Archimedes' principle> gives
$$
\frac{V_{\mathrm{displaced}}}{V_{\mathrm{body}}}
=\frac{\rho_{\mathrm{body}}}{\rho_f}.
$$
Partial immersion is possible when $\rho_{\mathrm{body}}<\rho_f$. A nonuniform body uses its mean <mass density> $m/V_{\mathrm{body}}$ instead; for a hollow body this <volume> must count the enclosed space that excludes <water>. This explains how the amount of displaced <water> can support a vessel whose walls alone are denser than <water>. https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/11-7-archimedes-principle[OpenStax, College Physics, §11.7: floating fraction and mean density]{ref}

For the $600\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ <cube> in <water> of <mass density> $1000\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, the displaced fraction is $600/1000=0.60$: sixty percent of its <volume> lies below the surface. With a horizontal top and bottom, the immersed depth $d$ satisfies $V_{\mathrm{displaced}}=Ad$, so
$$
V_{\mathrm{displaced}}=0.000600\ \mathrm{m^3},\qquad
d=\frac{0.000600}{0.010}=0.060\ \mathrm m.
$$
Its remaining height is $0.040\ \mathrm m$, and the <buoyancy> is $1000\times9.81\times0.000600=5.886\ \mathrm N$, exactly balancing its downward <force> from <gravity>.

\Image[buoyancy-floating-and-sinking.png]
{title=Floating, neutral buoyancy and sinking for three equal-size cubes}
{description=All three <cubes> have side $0.10\ \mathrm m$ in <water> with stipulated <mass density> $1000\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ and <gravitational acceleration> $9.81\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. Left: the $600\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ body floats with $0.060\ \mathrm m$ immersed and equal upward and downward <forces> of $5.886\ \mathrm N$. Middle: a fully immersed $1000\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ body has <neutral buoyancy>, with both <forces> equal to $9.81\ \mathrm N$. Right: a fully immersed $1200\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ body has downward <force> from <gravity> $11.772\ \mathrm N$ against upward <buoyancy> $9.81\ \mathrm N$, so release from rest gives an initial net downward <force> $1.962\ \mathrm N$.}

= Neutral buoyancy
{parent=Buoyancy}
{title2=$\rho_{\mathrm{body}}=\rho_f$}
{wiki}

<Neutral buoyancy> means a fully immersed body's upward <buoyancy> equals its downward <force> from <gravity>. For a body of fixed <volume> in a constant-<mass density> <fluid> under a uniform <gravitational field>, this occurs when its mean <mass density> equals the surrounding <mass density>. A $0.10\ \mathrm m$ <cube> of <mass density> $1000\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ in the stipulated <water> has <mass> $1.000\ \mathrm{kg}$, so both <forces> are $9.81\ \mathrm N$.

Ideally it can remain at rest at any depth for which it stays fully immersed and clear of boundaries. Raising or lowering it changes the absolute <pressure>, but neither its displaced <volume> nor the difference between the vertical <pressure> <forces>. There is therefore no vertical <restoring force> toward a preferred depth in this model. <Neutral buoyancy> is a vertical <force> balance, not a claim about resistance to tilting or about a compressible body whose <volume> changes with <pressure>.

= Submerged weight
{parent=Buoyancy}

For a grain of <mass density> $\rho_p$ and volume $\mathcal V$ in a fluid of <mass density> $\rho_f$, its effective downward <force> is $(\rho_p-\rho_f)g\mathcal V$, after subtracting <buoyancy> from weight.

= Buoyancy perturbation
{title2=$b'$}
{parent=Buoyancy}

A buoyancy perturbation is the departure $b'$ from a prescribed basic buoyancy field. In a fluid with constant <buoyancy frequency> $N$, vertical velocity $w'$ changes it according to $(\partial_t+\mathbf U\cdot\nabla)b'=-N^2w'$.

= Buoyancy displacement variable
{parent=Buoyancy perturbation}
{title2=$b_x=-N^2\theta$}

= Scaled potential-temperature perturbation
{synonym}

In a radially stratified <shearing sheet>, one can normalize the scalar <buoyancy perturbation> by writing $b_x=-N^2\theta$ and $D_t\theta=u_x+\xi\nabla^2\theta$. With this convention $\theta$ has dimensions of length, rather than <temperature>; $\xi$ is <thermal diffusivity>.

= Isopycnal
{parent=Buoyancy}
{wiki}

An isopycnal is a surface of constant <mass density>. In a <Boussinesq approximation>, it is equivalently a surface of constant buoyancy.

\Include[hydrodynamic-stability]{parent=Fluid mechanics}

= Barotropic fluid
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

= Barotropic
{synonym}

A barotropic fluid has pressure determined by density alone, $P=P(\rho)$, so pressure and density surfaces coincide in hydrostatic equilibrium.

= Barotropic cylindrical rotation theorem
{parent=Barotropic fluid}
{title2=$\partial_z\Omega^2=0$}

= Poincaré-Wavre theorem
{synonym}
{c}

In a steady axisymmetric <barotropic fluid> with purely azimuthal velocity $r\Omega\mathbf e_\phi$ and a conservative body force, the <barotropic enthalpy function> obeys $\nabla(w+\Phi)=r\Omega^2\mathbf e_r$. Taking its curl yields the displayed cylindrical rotation law. The first integral is $w+\Phi-\int r\Omega^2dr=C$ in a connected fluid region. Baroclinic pressure and density need not satisfy this conclusion.

= Barotropic spherical accretion equation
{parent=Barotropic fluid}
{title2=$(u-c_s^2/u)u^{\prime}=2c_s^2/r-\Phi^{\prime}$}

Steady spherical <continuity equation> gives $r^2\rho u=\text{constant}$. Combining this with <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> and $c_s^2=dp/d\rho$ gives the displayed equation. Smooth transonic passage requires $u^2=c_s^2$ and $\Phi^{\prime}=2c_s^2/r$ simultaneously. The <barotropic enthalpy function> gives the integral $u^2/2+h+\Phi=\text{constant}$.

= Polytropic accretion in a mixed inverse-power potential
{parent=Barotropic spherical accretion equation}
{title2=$m c_\infty^2R_s^2-\frac{2m-3}{4}\mu R_s-\frac{2m-1}{2}\lambda=0$}

For $p=K\rho^{1+1/m}$, the <barotropic enthalpy function> is $mc_s^2$. In $\Phi=-\lambda/R^2-\mu/R$, a regular <sonic point> obeys $c_{s,s}^2=\lambda/R_s^2+\mu/(2R_s)$, and its Bernoulli integral yields the displayed quadratic. For $K,\lambda,\mu>0$ and the usual positive polytropic index, its unique positive root exists precisely when $m>1/2$. The <mass accretion rate> is $4\pi R_s^2\rho_\infty c_{s,s}(c_{s,s}^2/c_\infty^2)^m$. If indices with $0<1+1/m<1$ are admitted, $m<-1$ also permits a positive sonic root; negative compressibility indices do not.

= Critical-point slope of barotropic spherical accretion
{parent=Barotropic spherical accretion equation}
{title2=$C=\rho\,d(c_s^2)/d\rho$}

At a regular <sonic point>, differentiating the vanishing coefficient and numerator yields $(2+C/c_s^2)(u^{\prime})^2+4Cu^{\prime}/(ru)+(4C+2c_s^2)/r^2+\Phi^{\prime\prime}=0$. A real slope compatible with the desired branch is needed in addition to the sonic equalities. For constant <sound speed> this reduces to $(u^{\prime})^2=-c_s^2/r^2-\Phi^{\prime\prime}/2$.

= Barotropic enthalpy function
{parent=Barotropic fluid}
{title2=$h(\rho)=\int_{\rho_1}^\rho \frac{dp/d\rho^{\prime}}{\rho^{\prime}}d\rho^{\prime}$}

= Barotropic pressure potential
{synonym}

This <barotropic pressure potential> obeys $\nabla h=\nabla p/\rho$ for a <barotropic fluid>. It gives the <pressure> contribution to a steady <Bernoulli equation>. In isentropic thermodynamics it agrees with <specific enthalpy> up to a constant; for an externally maintained isothermal ideal gas it is $c_s^2\log\rho$ and is not the constant thermodynamic enthalpy at fixed temperature.

= Centrifugal potential of cylindrical rotation
{parent=Barotropic fluid}
{title2=$\Psi=\Phi-\int_0^R R'\Omega^2(R')\,dR'$}

For <angular velocity> depending only on cylindrical radius, the centrifugal acceleration is a <gradient>. Steady barotropic balance is $\nabla p/\rho=-\nabla\Psi$, hence <specific enthalpy> satisfies $h(\rho)+\Psi=\text{constant}$. On an invertible equation-of-state branch, <mass density> and <pressure> are functions of $\Psi$. Cylindrical rotation is essential to this scalar centrifugal-potential representation.

= Barotropic energy density
{parent=Barotropic fluid}
{title2=$\rho U^{\prime}-U=P$}

For a <barotropic fluid> with <pressure> $P=P(\rho)$, choose an energy density $U$ satisfying $\rho U'(\rho)-U=P$. The <continuity equation> then gives $\partial_tU+\nabla\cdot(U\mathbf u)=-P\nabla\cdot\mathbf u$. For an <isothermal equation of state> $P=c_s^2\rho$, one choice is $U=c_s^2\rho\ln(\rho/\rho_{\rm ref})$. A fixed reference density changes $U$ only by a conserved mass term. The same construction holds with <surface density> and vertically integrated <pressure>. This closure energy need not equal the microscopic internal energy of a gas maintained at fixed <temperature>.

= Barotropic magnetic energy equation
{parent=Barotropic fluid}

For a <barotropic fluid>, an energy per mass $e$ can be defined by $de/d\rho=p/\rho^2$. For the <isothermal equation of state>, $e=c_s^2\ln(\rho/\rho_0)$. Adding kinetic, gravitational and <magnetic energy> gives the ideal magnetic energy flux $\mathbf u(E+p+B^2/(2\mu_0))-\mathbf B(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf B)/\mu_0$. This is a mechanical barotropic energy identity; it does not say that an isothermal gas is thermally isolated.

= Barotropic vorticity transport
{parent=Barotropic fluid}
{title2=$\partial_t\boldsymbol\omega=\nabla\times(\mathbf u\times\boldsymbol\omega)$}

For an inviscid <barotropic fluid> with only potential body forces, the <vorticity> $\boldsymbol\omega=\nabla\times\mathbf u$ obeys
$$
\partial_t\boldsymbol\omega=\nabla\times(\mathbf u\times\boldsymbol\omega).
$$
The pressure force is a <gradient> because $\rho^{-1}\nabla p=\nabla\int^\rho p'(s)\,ds/s$. A spatially uniform <specific entropy> makes a fixed-composition fluid barotropic. Merely having $Ds/Dt=0$ does not exclude <baroclinic vorticity generation> from spatial entropy gradients.

= Shear flow
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

A shear flow has a <velocity field> varying across adjacent fluid layers, giving a nonzero <shear rate>. A parallel example is $\mathbf u=U(z)\mathbf e_x$, with vertical shear $U'$. <Linear shear flow> has constant shear, while a <shear layer> concentrates the velocity change in a narrow region.

= Renovating shear flow
{parent=Shear flow}
{title2=$S(a)=\begin{pmatrix}1&a\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\quad a=\Lambda T$}

A renovating shear flow holds a planar <shear flow> fixed for an interval $T$, then chooses a fresh orientation independently of preceding intervals. Each interval acts on a <material line> by a rotated copy of the displayed <deformation gradient>. The orientation law must be specified: independent uniformly distributed orientations define the isotropic model, whereas a biased orientation law generally gives another <Lyapunov exponent>.

= Mean logarithmic stretching in an isotropic renovating shear
{parent=Renovating shear flow}
{title2=$\mu=\log(1+a^2/4)/(2T)$}

For independent uniform orientations in a <renovating shear flow>, the incident angle relative to each new shear is uniform even after earlier deformations. One interval stretches a unit <material line> by $\ell(\theta)^2=1+a\sin2\theta+a^2\sin^2\theta$. Write this as $A+B\cos(2\theta-\delta)$, where $A=1+a^2/2$ and $A^2-B^2=1$. The angular logarithmic integral gives $\langle\log\ell\rangle=\tfrac12\log[(A+1)/2]$. Add these independent logarithmic increments to get the displayed rate. At fixed shear rate, small $T$ gives $\mu\sim\Lambda^2T/8$ and large $T$ gives $\mu\sim\log(|\Lambda|T/2)/T$. The unique positive maximum is at $|\Lambda|T\approx3.9605826$, obtained from $\log z=2(1-z^{-1})$ with $z=1+a^2/4$.

= Log-stretch fluctuations in an isotropic renovating shear
{parent=Mean logarithmic stretching in an isotropic renovating shear}
{title2=$\operatorname{Var}(\log\lambda_N)=\frac N2\sum_{n\geq1}q^{2n}/n^2,\quad q^2=a^2/(a^2+4)$}

The logarithm of the stretch after $N$ independent uniform renovations is a sum of independent identically distributed bounded increments. For singular stretching $e^\gamma$, $\gamma=\operatorname{arsinh}(|a|/2)$, the increment has Fourier expansion $r+\sum_{n\geq1}(-1)^{n+1}q^n\cos(2n\theta)/n$, with $q=\tanh\gamma$ and $r=\tfrac12\log(1+a^2/4)$. Orthogonality proves the displayed variance. The <central limit theorem> gives an approximate central <log-normal distribution> of $\lambda_N$, not an exact description of extreme tails. For $|a|\ll1$, both the mean and variance of $\log\lambda_N$ are $Na^2/8$ to leading order. Direct expansion of fixed-power moments gives $\langle\lambda_N^p\rangle=\exp[Np(p+2)a^2/16+O(Na^4)]$; in particular mean stretch grows faster than typical stretch.

= Linear shear flow
{parent=Shear flow}

A linear shear flow has velocity that varies linearly in a transverse coordinate, such as $\mathbf u=-Sx\mathbf e_y$. Its velocity gradient and shear rate are spatially constant.

= Lagrangian displacement
{title2=$\boldsymbol\xi$}
{disambiguate=fluid mechanics}
{c}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

= Fluid displacement
{synonym}

= Fluid Lagrangian displacement
{synonym}

A Lagrangian displacement maps each equilibrium fluid element to its perturbed position. Its time derivative is the velocity perturbation when the equilibrium is static.

= Eulerian perturbation of a fluid variable
{parent=Lagrangian displacement (fluid mechanics)}
{c}
{title2=$\delta q=q_{\rm perturbed}(x,t)-q_{\rm background}(x)$}

= Fluid Eulerian perturbation
{synonym}

The Eulerian perturbation compares field values at the same spatial location, rather than following a fluid element. To first order it satisfies $\delta q=\Delta q-\xi\cdot\nabla q$. Thus a field advected unchanged by individual elements can still have a nonzero Eulerian perturbation in an inhomogeneous background.

= Lagrangian perturbation of a fluid variable
{parent=Lagrangian displacement (fluid mechanics)}
{c}
{title2=$\Delta q=\delta q+\xi\cdot\nabla q$}

= Fluid Lagrangian perturbation
{synonym}

The Lagrangian perturbation compares a variable on the same moving fluid element before and after a displacement. Taylor expansion of the background value at the displaced position gives the displayed relation to an Eulerian perturbation. For an adiabatic <ideal gas>, $\Delta(p/\rho^\gamma)=0$ even when the equilibrium <entropy> varies spatially.

= Adiabatic fluid perturbation
{parent=Lagrangian perturbation of a fluid variable}
{title2=$\Delta s=0,\quad\Delta p=\gamma p\,\Delta\rho/\rho$}

An adiabatic fluid perturbation preserves the <specific entropy> of each displaced parcel. For a <perfect gas>, <mass conservation> gives $\Delta\rho=-\rho\nabla\cdot\boldsymbol\xi$ and the <adiabatic equation of state> gives $\Delta p=\gamma p\Delta\rho/\rho$. The corresponding <Eulerian perturbation of a fluid variable> is $\delta p=-\gamma p\nabla\cdot\boldsymbol\xi-\boldsymbol\xi\cdot\nabla p$. An isothermal equilibrium may still have adiabatic perturbations, because the thermodynamic constraint on displaced parcels differs from the equilibrium temperature profile.

= Eulerian and Lagrangian fluid perturbations
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

For a background scalar field $f$ and fluid displacement $\boldsymbol\xi$, the Lagrangian perturbation follows the displaced fluid element while the Eulerian perturbation compares fields at one fixed position. To first order,
$$
\Delta_L f=\delta f+\boldsymbol\xi\mathbin\cdot\nabla f.
$$

= Eulerian fluid perturbation
{c}
{parent=Eulerian and Lagrangian fluid perturbations}
{title2=$f_1=\Delta f-\boldsymbol\xi\cdot\nabla f$}

An Eulerian fluid perturbation compares a perturbed field with its equilibrium value at one fixed spatial point. It differs from the perturbation following a displaced material element: $\Delta f=f_1+\boldsymbol\xi\cdot\nabla f$. Linearizing a static fluid velocity gives $\mathbf v_1=\partial_t\boldsymbol\xi$.

= Surface density perturbation of a displaced uniform interface
{parent=Eulerian and Lagrangian fluid perturbations}
{title2=$\rho^{\prime}=\rho_0\eta_+\delta(z-H)-\rho_0\eta_-\delta(z+H)$}

Moving a uniform-density boundary produces a delta-function sheet in the Eulerian <mass density> perturbation. For a slab displaced vertically by $\eta_\pm$, $\rho^{\prime}=\rho_0\eta_+\delta(z-H)-\rho_0\eta_-\delta(z+H)$. <Poisson equation> then gives a continuous gravitational potential with derivative jumps fixed by those sheets.

= Lagrangian pressure perturbation
{parent=Eulerian and Lagrangian fluid perturbations}
{c}
{title2=$\Delta p$}

The <Lagrangian pressure perturbation> is the change in <pressure> experienced by a displaced <fluid element>: $\Delta p=\delta p+\boldsymbol\xi\cdot\nabla p_0$. For a free surface against fixed external <pressure>, $\Delta p=0$, rather than $\delta p=0$ at the original surface.

= Torricelli's law
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

An ideal fluid issuing from a small hole a vertical depth $h$ below a free surface has speed
$$
v=\sqrt{2gh}.
$$

= Drainage time of a conical tank
{parent=Torricelli's law}
{title2=$T\sim\frac{2h_0^{5/2}}{5\epsilon^2\sqrt{2g}}$}

For a cone of half angle $\pi/4$ draining through a small circular aperture of radius $\epsilon$, the horizontal area at height $h$ is $\pi h^2$. <Torricelli's law> gives speed $\sqrt{2gh}$, so <conservation of mass> gives $h^2\dot h=-\epsilon^2\sqrt{2gh}$. Integration yields the displayed leading drainage time when $h_0\gg\epsilon$. The final layer $h=O(\epsilon)$ is outside the quasi-steady approximation and contributes only order $\sqrt{\epsilon/g}$, compared with the leading time of order $h_0^{5/2}/(\epsilon^2\sqrt g)$. This is an ideal inviscid estimate without a discharge coefficient.

= Density
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

= Mass density
{synonym}

Mass density is mass per unit volume.

= Linear mass density
{parent=Density}
{title2=$\mu=dm/d\ell$}

Linear mass density is <mass> per unit <length>, measured in $\mathrm{kg\,m^{-1}}$. A uniform string of mass $m$ and length $L$ has $\mu=m/L$; with constant <tension> $T$, its transverse <wave> speed is $\sqrt{T/\mu}$. <Mass density> instead measures mass per unit <volume>.

= Gas clumping factor
{parent=Density}
{title2=$C_e=\langle n_e^2\rangle/\langle n_e\rangle^2$}

The <gas clumping factor> measures unresolved inhomogeneity in electron <number density> within a chosen averaging region. Nonnegative <variance> gives $C_e\ge1$. At a uniform <temperature>, <thermal bremsstrahlung> scales with $\langle n_e^2\rangle$, whereas the <thermal Sunyaev-Zeldovich effect> scales with $\langle n_e\rangle$. A homogeneous <cluster distance from Sunyaev-Zeldovich and X-ray signals> inference then gives $d_{A,\mathrm{inferred}}=d_{A,\mathrm{true}}/C_e$, for the same geometry and temperature. Correlated temperature variations require additional weighted averages.

= Projected surface mass density
{parent=Density}
{title2=$\Sigma(\boldsymbol\xi)=\int\rho(\boldsymbol\xi,l)\,dl$}

= Surface mass density
{synonym}

Projected surface mass density is mass per unit projected area, obtained by integrating <mass density> along the line of sight. It determines the enclosed cylindrical mass used by a <thin gravitational lens equation>. It need not equal a physical disk's density, and its aperture mass differs from a spherical three-dimensional enclosed mass.

= Fluid pressure
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

Fluid pressure is the isotropic normal compressive stress in a fluid at rest.

= Cavitation
{parent=Fluid pressure}

Cavitation is the formation of gas or vapour cavities when a liquid's <pressure> becomes sufficiently low. A <journal bearing> model with <cavitation> must specify how a partly filled film and its boundaries behave, so periodic <pressure> alone cannot determine the flow there.

= Pressure gradient
{title2=$\nabla p$}
{parent=Fluid pressure}

The pressure gradient is the vector $\nabla p$ pointing in the direction of fastest pressure increase. The force density exerted by pressure on a continuum is $-\nabla p$.

= Drag
{disambiguate=physics}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

= Drag force
{synonym}

= Drag
{synonym}

A drag force opposes an object's motion relative to a surrounding fluid and transfers momentum between them.

= Linear drag
{parent=Drag (physics)}
{title2=$\mathbf F_d=-\alpha\mathbf v$}

Linear drag opposes the <velocity> relative to the surrounding medium with magnitude proportional to its <speed>, where $\alpha>0$. Its <power> is $\mathbf F_d\cdot\mathbf v=-\alpha|\mathbf v|^2$, so it dissipates <kinetic energy>. Under a constant external <force>, the resulting <linear ordinary differential equation> relaxes exponentially on the <time> scale $m/\alpha$.

= Overdamped particle dynamics
{parent=Linear drag}

When momentum relaxes much faster than the imposed motion, <force> balance replaces the inertial equation: $\zeta\dot x=F(x,t)$ in a deterministic one-dimensional model. The <linear friction coefficient> sets the time scale. Thermal forcing, if included, gives <overdamped Langevin dynamics>.

= Linear friction coefficient
{parent=Linear drag}
{title2=$\zeta$}

The dimensional linear friction coefficient relates <drag force> and <velocity>, $F_d=-\zeta v$, and has units of <force> divided by speed. It differs from the dimensionless aerodynamic <drag coefficient>. For an isolated sphere in <Stokes flow>, the <Stokes drag law> gives $\zeta=6\pi\eta a$.

= Vertical ascent under linear drag
{parent=Linear drag}
{title2=$T=(m/\alpha)\log(1+\alpha u_0/(mg))$}

For constant downward gravitational <acceleration> $g$ and <linear drag> $-\alpha v$, the upward <velocity> solves $m\dot v=-mg-\alpha v$ with $v(0)=u_0\ge0$. Its first zero occurs at the displayed <time>. The dimensionless control variable is $\alpha u_0/(mg)$, and the limit $\alpha\to0$ gives $u_0/g$.

= Drag coefficient
{parent=Drag (physics)}
{title2=$C_D$}
{wiki}

The <drag coefficient> measures a <drag force> against a specified scale of <force>. Let $\mathbf U=\mathbf v_{\rm body}-\mathbf u_\infty$ be the body's <velocity> relative to the distant, undisturbed <fluid>, and let $U=|\mathbf U|>0$ be its relative <speed>. For <fluid> <density> $\rho$, <drag force> magnitude $F_D$ and reference <area> $A>0$, define
$$
C_D=\frac{F_D}{\tfrac12\rho U^2A},\qquad F_D=\tfrac12\rho U^2AC_D.
$$
The <drag coefficient> is a <dimensionless quantity>: the denominator has units of <force>. The reference <area> must be reported with it. For a <sphere> of radius $a$, the usual choice is the frontal <area> $A=\pi a^2$. Choosing its entire surface <area> $4\pi a^2$ instead divides the reported <drag coefficient> by four for the same physical <drag force>. Comparing coefficients without comparing their reference <areas> can therefore be misleading. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/drag-coefficient/[NASA Glenn Research Center, Drag Coefficient, definition and Determining Value for Drag Coefficient]{ref}

For a rigid <sphere> in <steady flow> through an unbounded <Newtonian fluid> with constant <dynamic viscosity> $\mu$, <incompressible flow> and the <no-slip boundary condition>, the creeping-flow limit requires the diameter-based <particle Reynolds number> $\mathrm{Re}_d=2a\rho U/\mu\ll1$. The <Stokes drag law> gives $F_D=6\pi\mu aU$. Substitution into the definition with $A=\pi a^2$ gives the short derivation
$$
C_D=\frac{6\pi\mu aU}{\tfrac12\rho U^2\pi a^2}=\frac{12\mu}{\rho aU}=\frac{24}{\mathrm{Re}_d}.
$$
Thus the <drag coefficient> is neither necessarily constant nor bounded above by one. The formula containing $U^2$ still describes <linear drag> here, because the <drag coefficient> varies as $1/U$. The relation $C_D=24/\mathrm{Re}_d$ is the leading small-<Reynolds number> approximation, not a measured curve valid at every <Reynolds number>. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336L/Fluid/node142.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Axisymmetric Stokes Flow Around a Solid Sphere, equations 10.126 and 10.129]{ref}

For a worked example, stipulate <dynamic viscosity> $\mu=0.10\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$, <density> $\rho=1000\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, radius $a=10^{-4}\,\mathrm m$ and relative <speed> $U=10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Within the stated <Stokes drag law> assumptions,
$$
\mathrm{Re}_d=0.002,\qquad C_D=12000,\qquad F_D=6\pi\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm N\approx1.88495559\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm N.
$$
Doubling the relative <speed> with all other parameters fixed gives $\mathrm{Re}_d=0.004$ and $C_D=6000$: the predicted <drag force> doubles to $3.76991118\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm N$. Falsely holding the <drag coefficient> at $12000$ would instead quadruple the <drag force>. Both <particle Reynolds numbers> remain small, so the comparison is within the creeping-flow approximation.

\Image[sphere-drag-coefficient-stokes-limit.png]
{title=Sphere drag coefficient in the creeping-flow approximation}
{description=The mathematical approximation $C_D=24/\mathrm{Re}_d$ is plotted only for small diameter-based <particle Reynolds number>, using frontal reference <area> $A=\pi a^2$. The two dots mark the worked example and its doubled relative <speed>. This is a creeping-limit curve, not empirical data or an extension of the <Stokes drag law> to all <Reynolds numbers>.}

The useful idea is to separate the <force> scale from the dependence on geometry and flow conditions. A <drag coefficient> can help compare experiments, but changing <Reynolds number> can change the coefficient itself; NASA Glenn explicitly stresses matching the relevant flow conditions. The elegant cancellation above also exposes a common mistake: a <drag coefficient> definition does not by itself impose a <quadratic drag> law.

= Quadratic drag
{parent=Drag (physics)}

Quadratic drag has magnitude proportional to the square of the relative speed and points opposite to the relative velocity.

For an <isotropic> model of a body of fixed shape, let $\mathbf U=\mathbf v_{\rm body}-\mathbf u_\infty$ denote its <relative velocity> through the distant <fluid>, with $U=|\mathbf U|$. A constant positive resistance $k$ gives
$$
\mathbf F_D=-k|\mathbf U|\mathbf U,\qquad F_D=kU^2,\qquad k=\tfrac12\rho AC_D.
$$
Here <density> $\rho$, reference <area> $A$ and <drag coefficient> $C_D$ are held fixed over the modeled range of relative <speeds>. The vector formula makes the direction explicit: the <drag force> on the body opposes its motion relative to the <fluid>, and vanishes at $U=0$. For a general body, orientation and other flow conditions can affect the <drag coefficient>; treating it as a constant requires a regime in which that is an adequate approximation. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/drag-coefficient/[NASA Glenn Research Center, Drag Coefficient, Air Viscosity and Compressibility Effects]{ref}

Compare two distinct models at a chosen relative <speed> $U_0>0$. A <linear drag> model has $F_D(U)/F_D(U_0)=U/U_0$, while a constant-coefficient <quadratic drag> model has $F_D(U)/F_D(U_0)=(U/U_0)^2$. At $U=2U_0$ their <drag force> ratios are two and four respectively.

\Image[linear-versus-quadratic-drag-scaling.png]
{title=Normalized linear and quadratic drag models: doubling speed gives force ratios two and four}
{description=Each model is divided by its own <drag force> at $U_0$. The <linear drag> curve applies, for example, to a rigid <sphere> under the <Stokes drag law> assumptions while its <particle Reynolds number> remains small. The <quadratic drag> curve assumes constant <density>, reference <area> and <drag coefficient> over its modeled range. The comparison specifies neither an exact transition nor a single set of physical parameters that realizes both laws throughout the plotted range.}

This distinction matters when estimating <terminal velocity> or trajectories such as <descent from rest under quadratic drag>: doubling relative <speed> increases the resistance much more strongly in the <quadratic drag> model. An approximately constant <drag coefficient> can occur in another <Reynolds number> regime; its use must be justified there. Conversely, substituting the creeping-flow <sphere> coefficient $C_D=24/\mathrm{Re}_d$ into $F_D=\tfrac12\rho U^2AC_D$ recovers <linear drag>, so writing this general formula does not make the resistance quadratic. Neither approximation should be extended across all <Reynolds numbers>.

The distinction already appears in the original investigation underlying the <Stokes drag law>, read on 9 December 1850: article 42 identifies resistance proportional to relative <speed> and distinguishes it from a contribution proportional to the square of relative <speed>. https://mural.uv.es/daroig/documentos/stokes1850.pdf[George Gabriel Stokes, On the Effect of the Internal Friction of Fluids on the Motion of Pendulums, article 42, collected-paper reprint]{ref}

= Descent from rest under quadratic drag
{parent=Quadratic drag}
{title2=$v(t)=\sqrt{mg/\alpha}\,\tanh(\sqrt{\alpha g/m}\,t)$}

For positive downward speed, gravity and <quadratic drag> give $m\dot v=mg-\alpha v^2$, $v(0)=0$. Separation and the integral of $(1-s^2)^{-1}$ give the displayed approach to <terminal velocity>. The fallen distance is $(m/\alpha)\log\cosh(\sqrt{\alpha g/m}\,t)$. The limit as $\alpha\to0$ recovers $v=gt$ and distance $gt^2/2$.

= Quadratic-drag turning angle
{parent=Quadratic drag}
{title2=$\tan\theta=\mu|f|/(\rho_wC_wU)$}

For an ice mass in still water, approximate the wind stress by a fixed vector $a\mathbf e_x$ while retaining <quadratic drag> in the water. With mass per water-drag area $\mu=m/A_w$, water coefficient $b=\rho_wC_w$ and $f=2\Omega\sin\phi$, the steady balance is $a\mathbf e_x=bU\mathbf u+\mu f\widehat{\mathbf z}\times\mathbf u$. Resolving along the drift and perpendicular to it gives $a\cos\theta=bU^2$, $a\sin\theta=\mu|f|U$. Thus $b^2U^4+(\mu f)^2U^2=a^2$ and $\tan\theta=\mu|f|/(bU)$. The direction is right of the wind in the northern hemisphere and left in the southern hemisphere. This angle neglects intrinsic boundary-layer turning, ocean currents and contact forces; it is not a universal observed sea-ice turning angle.

= Mass per drag area
{parent=Quadratic-drag turning angle}
{title2=$\mu=m/A_w$}

The normalization $\mu=m/A_w$ has units $\mathrm{kg\,m^{-2}}$. It converts the <Coriolis force> into a force per water-drag area and allows comparison with wind and water stresses. <Icebergs> generally have much larger $\mu$ than thin <ice floes>, but their effective air-to-water drag-area ratio also matters. A geometric depth becomes a mass per area only after multiplication by density.

= Quadratic damping
{parent=Quadratic drag}
{title2=$\dot\omega\supset-c\omega|\omega|$}

A resistive velocity term proportional to the speed squared removes energy at rate proportional to $|\omega|^3$. Its derivative at zero velocity vanishes, so it does not appear in the <linearization of a dynamical system> at rest. Nonlinear energy arguments can still prove <asymptotic stability>.

= Pressure drag
{parent=Drag (physics)}

Pressure drag is the component of force produced by integrating pressure over a body's surface. For flow over topography $z=h(x)$, the horizontal force on the fluid is $-\overline{p h_x}$ to leading order.

= Stokes number
{title2=$\operatorname{St}$}
{parent=Drag (physics)}
{c}
{wiki}

The Stokes number compares a particle's drag stopping time $\tau$ with a characteristic flow time $T$: $\operatorname{St}=\tau/T$. In an orbiting disk, the natural choice is $\operatorname{St}=\tau\Omega$.

= Gas drag
{parent=Drag (physics)}

Gas drag is a <drag force> on a solid body due to its motion relative to surrounding gas. With <aerodynamic stopping time> $t_s$, the linear approximation gives $\dot{\mathbf v}|_{\rm drag}=-(\mathbf v-\mathbf v_g)/t_s$.

= Aerodynamic stopping time
{title2=$\tau$}
{parent=Drag (physics)}

The aerodynamic stopping time is the characteristic time on which <drag force> damps a solid particle's velocity relative to the surrounding fluid. Linear drag gives relative acceleration $-\mathbf v_{\rm rel}/\tau$.

= Suspension
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

= Particle suspension
{synonym}

A particle suspension is a dispersed population of solid particles carried by a fluid. Its dynamics couple particle transport, <drag force>, gravity, and the compensating motion of the carrier fluid.

= Density inversion in a settling suspension
{parent=Suspension (chemistry)}

More suspended heavy particles above fewer particles puts denser fluid above lighter fluid. Such a <particle suspension> may develop <Rayleigh-Taylor instability> and overturning in addition to one-dimensional <kinematic sedimentation>. A rarefaction solution alone does not establish hydrodynamic stability.

= Heated particle-laden layer
{parent=Suspension (chemistry)}

A dilute <particle suspension> can supply both excess <mass density> and heat. To first order its <equation of state> is $\rho/\rho_0=1+\gamma\phi-\theta$, where $\phi$ is the <particle volume fraction> and $\theta$ is fractional <thermal expansion>. <Particle deposition flux> reduces $\phi$, while particle heating increases $\theta$. The competition can reverse the <reduced gravity> and create <unstable density stratification>.

= Particle volume fraction
{title2=$\phi$}
{parent=Suspension (chemistry)}

The particle volume fraction is the fraction of a representative mixture volume occupied by particles. Several particle species have fractions $\phi_i$, while the carrier-fluid fraction is $1-\sum_i\phi_i$.

= Bidisperse particle suspension
{parent=Suspension (chemistry)}

A bidisperse particle suspension contains two distinguishable particle populations, commonly with different sizes, densities, or <settling velocities>.

= Composition wave in a bidisperse suspension
{parent=Bidisperse particle suspension}

When two species share the same local settling velocity, their total <particle volume fraction> supports a nonlinear <kinematic wave>, while their relative composition is transported as a contact-like wave at the common particle speed.

= Settling velocity
{title2=$W$}
{parent=Suspension (chemistry)}

The settling velocity is a particle's vertical velocity relative to a stated frame. With height increasing upward, a sedimenting particle has $W<0$.

= Settling-velocity radius scaling
{parent=Settling velocity}
{title2=$W_s\propto r^2\ \text{or}\ r^{1/2}$}

For spheres with the same density contrast, balance submerged weight, proportional to $r^3$, against <Stokes drag law>, proportional to $rW_s$, to obtain $W_s\propto r^2$. With constant <drag coefficient>, <quadratic drag> is proportional to $r^2W_s^2$, giving $W_s\propto r^{1/2}$. The two radius laws require their corresponding <particle Reynolds number> limits.

= Particle Reynolds number
{parent=Settling velocity}
{title2=$\operatorname{Re}_p=2r\rho_fW_s/\mu$}

A <Reynolds number> based on particle diameter and relative speed in the carrier fluid. Small values justify <Stokes drag law>; a high-Reynolds-number regime with nearly constant <drag coefficient> instead gives <quadratic drag>. The drag law must be evaluated in the appropriate regime for every species.

= Particle deposition flux
{parent=Settling velocity}
{title2=$J_s=W_s\phi_b$}

= Deposition flux
{synonym}

For downward <settling velocity> magnitude $W_s$ and near-bed <particle volume fraction> $\phi_b$, a horizontal absorbing bed receives particle volume per area per time $J_s=W_s\phi_b$. A bed inclined at angle $\vartheta$ to the horizontal receives $W_s\phi_b\cos\vartheta$ per unit actual area, in the absence of resuspension or additional normal fluid motion.

= Stokes settling velocity
{c}
{parent=Settling velocity}
{title2=$U_s=2(\rho_p-\rho_f)ga^2/(9\mu)$}

= Stokes settling speed
{c}
{synonym}

\Image[stokes-settling-size-scaling.png]
{title=Sphere settling in water: terminal speed grows as radius squared while Reynolds number grows as radius cubed}

Take downward as positive and let <gravity> have magnitude $g$. For a rigid <sphere> of <density> $\rho_p$ in otherwise stationary <fluid> of <density> $\rho_f$, its weight minus <buoyancy> is $\tfrac43\pi a^3(\rho_p-\rho_f)g$. At <terminal velocity>, this balances the <Stokes drag law>:
$$
6\pi\mu aU_s=\frac43\pi a^3(\rho_p-\rho_f)g,\qquad
U_s=\frac{2(\rho_p-\rho_f)ga^2}{9\mu}.
$$
This signed downward <settling velocity> is negative for a buoyantly rising <sphere> and zero for equal <densities>. For fixed <density> contrast and <dynamic viscosity>, the submerged weight grows as $a^3$ while <linear drag> at fixed <speed> grows as $a$; their balance gives the <settling-velocity radius scaling> $U_s\propto a^2$. Meanwhile,
$$
\mathrm{Re}_d=\frac{2a\rho_f|U_s|}{\mu}
=\frac{4\rho_f|\rho_p-\rho_f|ga^3}{9\mu^2}.
$$
Thus doubling the radius multiplies the predicted <Stokes settling speed> by four and the <particle Reynolds number> by eight. This makes the formula useful for separating particles by size, while also providing its own check against extrapolation to large particles.

For a computed water example, take $\rho_f=1000\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, $\mu=1.00\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{Pa\,s}$, $\rho_p=2500\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, $g=9.81\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, and $a=10.0\ \mathrm{\mu m}$. These are specified rounded inputs rather than a claim of exact water properties at every <temperature>. They give
$$
U_s=3.27\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}=0.327\ \mathrm{mm\,s^{-1}},\qquad
\mathrm{Re}_d=\frac{1000(3.27\times10^{-4})(20.0\times10^{-6})}{10^{-3}}=0.00654.
$$
The diameter is $20.0\ \mathrm{\mu m}$, and both the net downward weight and the upward <drag force> are $6.16\times10^{-11}\ \mathrm N$. At this <terminal velocity>, falling $1.00\ \mathrm{mm}$ takes $3.06\ \mathrm s$. At $a=20.0\ \mathrm{\mu m}$ the prediction is $1.308\ \mathrm{mm\,s^{-1}}$ with $\mathrm{Re}_d=0.05232$; at $a=50.0\ \mathrm{\mu m}$ it is $8.175\ \mathrm{mm\,s^{-1}}$ with $\mathrm{Re}_d=0.8175$, which is no longer a convincing small-<Reynolds number> case. The figure labels these as predictions of the <Stokes settling velocity> formula and shows where its <inertia> check fails.

The calculation describes the eventual <terminal velocity>, not the release transient governed by particle <acceleration> and possibly the <unsteady Stokes equation>. Walls, <hydrodynamic interactions>, departures from a rigid <sphere>, or a different surface condition require a different resistance. For sufficiently small particles, <Brownian motion> can obscure individual settling trajectories; the formula still specifies the gravitational drift within its assumptions. The balancing argument already appears in article 43 of https://mural.uv.es/daroig/documentos/stokes1850.pdf[Stokes's original investigation, collected-paper reprint]{ref}.

= Hindered settling
{parent=Settling velocity}

Hindered settling is the reduction or modification of particle settling by the displaced carrier-fluid backflow and by interactions with other particles. A simple dilute mixture model gives every species the same volume-averaged backflow.

= Kinematic sedimentation
{parent=Hindered settling}
{title2=$\phi_t+\partial_zj(\phi)=0$}

= Kinematic sedimentation theory
{synonym}

A one-dimensional <particle suspension> settling without particle diffusion or overturning obeys the <scalar conservation law> $\phi_t+\partial_zj(\phi)=0$. The upward flux is negative for downward settling. <Characteristic curves>, compressive <shocks> and <rarefaction waves> describe concentration transport rather than individual particle paths.

= Hindered-settling flux inflection
{parent=Kinematic sedimentation}
{title2=$f''(s)=V_0\alpha(1-s)^{\alpha-2}[2-(\alpha+1)s]$}

For <hindered settling> with normalized <particle volume fraction> $s$, upward coordinate and downward isolated <settling velocity> magnitude $V_0$, the <scalar conservation law> has <conservation law flux> $f(s)=-V_0s(1-s)^\alpha$. For $\alpha>0$, its <convexity> changes at $s=2/(\alpha+1)$ when this lies in the physical interval. This change distinguishes spreading <rarefaction waves> from compressing <characteristic curves>; a flux with an inflection can produce both a <shock wave> and a <rarefaction wave> in one transition. At $\alpha=0$ the flux is linear and the profile simply translates.

= Bidisperse kinematic sedimentation
{parent=Kinematic sedimentation}

A <particle suspension> contains two particle species with separate <particle volume fractions>. In an independent-settling model, each species obeys its own flux conservation equation, while a shared stationary deposit can couple the jump conditions through its maximum total <packing fraction>. Such independence is an imposed model; general <hindered settling> need not decouple species.

= Two-stage bidisperse batch sedimentation
{parent=Bidisperse kinematic sedimentation}

Initially, separate clearing fronts of fast and slow species coexist with a mixed deposition front. After the fast clearing front meets that deposit, the remaining suspension contains only the slow species. Its later <sedimentation shock> builds a pure-slow layer above the earlier mixed deposit. Final layer thicknesses and composition follow from separate particle-volume conservation.

= Deposit composition from sedimentation jump conditions
{parent=Bidisperse kinematic sedimentation}
{title2=$s(p_i-q_i)=W_iq_i(1-q_i)$}

For a <bidisperse kinematic sedimentation> model with normalized suspension concentrations $q_i$, isolated speeds $W_i$, and a stationary deposit with $p_1+p_2=1$, the upward deposit-front speed magnitude obeys $s(p_i-q_i)=W_iq_i(1-q_i)$. Adding gives $s=\sum_iW_iq_i(1-q_i)/(1-\sum_iq_i)$; substitution determines each deposited fraction. These are <Rankine-Hugoniot conditions> with a stationary compacted branch.

= Sedimentation shock
{parent=Kinematic sedimentation}
{title2=$U=[j]/[\phi]$}

A moving discontinuity of <particle volume fraction> in <kinematic sedimentation>. Conservation of particle <volume> gives the <Rankine-Hugoniot condition> $U[\phi]=[j]$ in a consistently oriented coordinate. A clearing front and a deposit front can both be <sedimentation shocks>. A stationary deposit uses zero particle flux; its compacted branch need not obey the suspension flux law.

= Sediment mass determines final deposit thickness
{parent=Kinematic sedimentation}
{title2=$h_c=[\phi_1(H-z_m)+\phi_2z_m]/\phi_{\max}$}

Particle <mass conservation> gives $h_c=[\phi_1(H-z_m)+\phi_2z_m]/\phi_{\max}$. Averaging concentration over the entire original container gives $\bar\phi=\phi_{\max}h_c/H$. This average differs from the concentration inside the deposit itself.

= Two-layer sedimentation with a compression shock
{parent=Kinematic sedimentation}

With upper concentration $a$ and lower concentration $2a$, the clearing, internal and deposit fronts have speeds $-u_s(1-a)$, $-u_s(1-3a)$ and $2u_sa$. If the lower two merge, the resulting deposit/upper-suspension front has speed $u_sa$. Final deposit thickness is $a(H+z_m)$ by <mass conservation>.

= Triple-shock sedimentation point
{parent=Two-layer sedimentation with a compression shock}
{title2=$(z_m,t_c,h_c)=\left(\frac{H(1-a)}{1+a},\frac{H}{u_s(1+a)},\frac{2aH}{1+a}\right)$}

For initial concentrations $a$ above $z_m$ and $2a$ below it, all three straight <shocks> meet when $z_m=H(1-a)/(1+a)$. The common time is $H/[u_s(1+a)]$ and height is $2aH/(1+a)$. Afterwards only a stationary deposit/clear-fluid boundary remains.

= Quadratic hindered-settling flux
{parent=Kinematic sedimentation}
{title2=$j(\phi)=-u_s\phi(1-\phi)$}

For downward settling speed $u_s(1-\phi)$ in upward coordinate $z$, particle flux is $j=-u_s\phi(1-\phi)$. The concentration-characteristic speed is $j^{\prime}=u_s(2\phi-1)$ and differs from particle speed. Since $j^{\prime\prime}=2u_s>0$, decreasing concentration with height creates a compressive <shock>, while increasing concentration creates a <rarefaction wave>.

= Parabolic-profile sedimentation shock
{parent=Quadratic hindered-settling flux}

For $\phi_0(z)=\phi_{\max}(1-z^2/h^2)/3$ on $0\le z\le h$, with clear fluid above and no imposed bed discontinuity, the first compression of the smooth profile occurs at $t=3h/(4V_s)$ and $z=h/4$. The nascent shock enters clear fluid with initial signed speed $-V_s$. Its downward speed subsequently decreases as the state behind it becomes more concentrated, until a boundary interaction changes the problem. This is characteristic breaking, not the time of initiation of a separately imposed packed-bed front.

= Settling rarefaction fan
{parent=Quadratic hindered-settling flux}
{title2=$\phi=\tfrac12[1+(z-z_m)/(u_st)]$}

If lower initial concentration $b$ is less than upper concentration $a$, the <entropy solution> expands into a fan with $\phi=[1+(z-z_m)/(u_st)]/2$. Its characteristic speeds range from $u_s(2b-1)$ to $u_s(2a-1)$. The fan can be clipped by a clearing or deposition <shock>.

= Early extinction of a settling rarefaction fan
{parent=Settling rarefaction fan}
{title2=$a_*=1-\sqrt{2/3}$}

For upper concentration $a$, lower $a/2$ and interface height $4H/5$, both curved fronts meet inside the fan only if $a\geq1-\sqrt{2/3}$. At smaller $a$, the deposition front reaches the fan upper edge first, then advances through uniform concentration $a$. The clearing front remains straight and final time is $H(1-3a/5)/[u_s(1-a)]$.

= Curved settling fronts within a rarefaction fan
{parent=Settling rarefaction fan}
{title2=$z_u,z_d$}

For upper concentration $a$, lower concentration $b<a$ and $L=H-z_m$, define $A=aL$ and $D=(1-b)z_m$. Once inside the <settling rarefaction fan>, clearing and deposition <shocks> obey $z_u=z_m-u_st+2\sqrt{u_sAt}$ and $z_d=z_m+u_st-2\sqrt{u_sDt}$. These curves apply only while their neighboring concentration remains within the fan.

= Last surviving characteristic of a settling fan
{parent=Curved settling fronts within a rarefaction fan}
{title2=$\phi_*=\sqrt A/(\sqrt A+\sqrt D)$}

If both <shocks> meet inside a <settling rarefaction fan>, $t_c=(\sqrt A+\sqrt D)^2/u_s$ and the last characteristic has concentration $\phi_*=\sqrt A/(\sqrt A+\sqrt D)$. It runs from the initial internal interface to the final deposit point. This construction requires $b\leq\phi_*\leq a$; otherwise a front exits the fan before final deposition.

= Settling shock speed
{parent=Quadratic hindered-settling flux}
{title2=$V=u_s(\phi_L+\phi_R-1)$}

The <Rankine-Hugoniot condition> for the <quadratic hindered-settling flux> gives $V=u_s(\phi_L+\phi_R-1)$, with lower and upper states labelled $L,R$. It includes a downward clearing front ($\phi_R=0$), an upward deposition front ($\phi_L=1$), and a stationary final deposit/clear-fluid interface.

= Dynamic viscosity
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$\mu$}

= Viscosity
{synonym}

= Shear viscosity
{synonym}

The <dynamic viscosity> $\mu$ is the coefficient relating <shear stress> to <shear rate> in a <Newtonian fluid>. For a <simple shear flow> $\mathbf u=(u(y),0,0)$,
$$
\tau_{xy}=\mu\frac{du}{dy}=\mu\dot\gamma,\qquad
[\mu]=\mathrm{Pa\,s}=\mathrm{kg\,m^{-1}\,s^{-1}}.
$$
Here $\tau_{xy}$ is the $x$ component of viscous <traction> on a plane with <normal vector> $+\mathbf e_y$. The <shear rate> $\dot\gamma=du/dy$ has units $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Thus <dynamic viscosity> measures the <stress> required to sustain a given rate of deformation. It is the shear coefficient; the separate coefficient for <volume> change is <volume viscosity>. https://www.homepages.ucl.ac.uk/~uceseug/Fluids2/Slides_1.pdf[UCL, Fluid Mechanics II, Forces in Fluids, slides 4–5]{ref}

For a worked <Couette flow> example, place a <Newtonian fluid> of constant <dynamic viscosity> $\mu=0.10\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$ between parallel plates separated by $h=0.0010\,\mathrm m$. Hold the lower plate fixed and move the upper plate at $U=0.020\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Assume <steady flow>, no streamwise <pressure gradient>, and the <no-slip boundary condition>. Then
$$
u(y)=\frac{Uy}{h},\qquad
\dot\gamma=\frac Uh=20\,\mathrm{s^{-1}},\qquad
\tau_{xy}=\frac{\mu U}{h}=2.0\,\mathrm{Pa}.
$$
The fluid pulls backward on the moving plate with <force> magnitude $F=\tau_{xy}A$, where $A$ is its wetted <area>. For $A=0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ this is $0.020\,\mathrm N$. Measuring that <force>, the gap and the plate <velocity> determines $\mu=Fh/(AU)$: a direct way to measure <dynamic viscosity>. The linear profile and opposing wall <tractions> are the standard plane <Couette flow> solution. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-25-advanced-fluid-mechanics-fall-2013/1a114d602956fa0dd328155f9b45f93d_MIT2_25F13_Couet_and_Pois.pdf[MIT, Couette and Poiseuille Flows, p. 1]{ref}

\Image[viscosity-couette-shear.png]
{title=Couette flow: a constant velocity gradient produces a constant shear stress}
{description=Blue arrows show the linear velocity profile between the plates. Orange arrows show viscous traction on the upper and lower faces of a fluid slice; reversing the surface normal reverses the traction. The slice has zero net viscous force although each face carries stress.}

The <velocity gradient> matters, rather than the <velocity> itself. Adding the same constant <velocity> to both plates and the fluid leaves $du/dy$, and hence <shear stress>, unchanged. In <uniform flow>, every layer translates together and $du/dy=0$. More generally, even a nonzero <velocity gradient> need not generate <viscous stress>: <solid-body rotation> has zero <strain-rate tensor>, as the <viscous stress tensor> example shows. Thin gaps amplify <shear stress> for a fixed plate-speed difference, making <dynamic viscosity> useful for predicting the <force> needed to move lubricated surfaces.

= Zero-shear viscosity
{parent=Dynamic viscosity}
{title2=$\mu=\int_0^\infty G(s)\,ds$}

For an integrable <relaxation modulus>, a small constant shear rate sustained from the remote past gives stress equal to that rate times the displayed integral, including any instantaneous solvent mass. For the linear <Oldroyd-B model> it is $\mu_0+G_0\tau$. A nondecaying elastic modulus would make this integral infinite rather than describe a fluid with finite steady viscosity.

= Kinematic viscosity
{parent=Dynamic viscosity}
{title2=$\nu=\mu/\rho$}

The <kinematic viscosity> is <dynamic viscosity> divided by <mass density>:
$$
\nu=\frac\mu\rho,\qquad [\nu]=\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}.
$$
It measures the rate at which viscous transport smooths differences in <velocity>. For constant $\mu$ and $\rho$ in <incompressible flow>, dividing the <Navier-Stokes equation> by <mass density> makes the viscous acceleration $\nu\nabla^2\mathbf u$, where $\nabla^2$ is the <Laplacian>. https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/fluids/fluids.pdf[David Tong, Fluid Mechanics, section 3.1.1, p. 61]{ref}

For example, stipulate two <Newtonian fluids> with the same <dynamic viscosity> $0.10\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$ but different <mass densities>. In the same steady <Couette flow> as above, both have <shear stress> $2.0\,\mathrm{Pa}$, but
$$
\begin{aligned}
\rho_1&=1000\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}},&\nu_1&=1.0\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}},\\
\rho_2&=500\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}},&\nu_2&=2.0\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The same <viscous stress> transports <momentum> through less <mass> in the second fluid, so its <velocity> responds faster to a given nonuniform viscous <force>.

To see the <diffusion equation> directly, consider an unsteady parallel <velocity field> $\mathbf u=(u(y,t),0,0)$ with constant material coefficients and no streamwise <pressure gradient> or body <force>. Its <convective acceleration> vanishes, and the <Navier-Stokes equation> reduces to
$$
\partial_tu=\nu\partial_y^2u.
$$
Balancing $u/t$ against $\nu u/h^2$ gives the <viscous diffusion time> $t_\nu\sim h^2/\nu$. For the $1.0\,\mathrm{mm}$ gap, the two fluids have scales $0.010\,\mathrm s$ and $0.0050\,\mathrm s$. This is a characteristic scale, not an exact settling time. For example, a perturbation of the <Couette flow> profile satisfying zero perturbation <velocity> at both plates is
$$
\delta u(y,t)=V\sin\left(\frac{\pi y}{h}\right)
\exp\left(-\frac{\pi^2\nu t}{h^2}\right).
$$
Substituting verifies the <diffusion equation>; its amplitude falls by a factor $e$ in $h^2/(\pi^2\nu)$. <Kinematic viscosity> is therefore useful for predicting how quickly a moving wall influences the interior, while <dynamic viscosity> gives the <shear stress> at a prescribed <shear rate>.

= Density-weighted mean kinematic viscosity
{parent=Kinematic viscosity}
{title2=$\bar\nu=\Sigma^{-1}\int\rho\nu\,dz$}

For a vertically stratified <accretion disk> with <surface density> $\Sigma=\int\rho\,dz$, the density-weighted mean <kinematic viscosity> is $\bar\nu=\Sigma^{-1}\int\rho\nu\,dz$. In an <alpha disk> with $\nu=\alpha p/(\rho\Omega)$, this becomes $\bar\nu=\alpha(\Sigma\Omega)^{-1}\int p\,dz$.

// Target: astrophysics.bigb

= Density-weighted viscosity of a disk
{parent=Kinematic viscosity}
{title2=$\bar\nu=\Sigma^{-1}\int\rho\nu\,dz$}

If angular <velocity> is independent of height, integrating the viscous stress $\rho\nu r\Omega'$ gives $\Sigma\bar\nu r\Omega'$. Thus the <surface density> weighted average, rather than an unweighted height average, enters <vertically averaged viscous disk equations>.

= Viscous stress tensor
{parent=Dynamic viscosity}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\sigma_{\mathrm{visc}}=2\mu D$}

= Viscous stress
{synonym}

The <viscous stress tensor> is the part of the <Cauchy stress tensor> associated with deformation rates. For an isotropic <Newtonian fluid> in <incompressible flow>,
$$
\boldsymbol\sigma=-pI+\boldsymbol\sigma_{\mathrm{visc}},\qquad
\boldsymbol\sigma_{\mathrm{visc}}=2\mu D,\qquad
D=\frac12\left[\nabla\mathbf u+(\nabla\mathbf u)^T\right],\qquad
\operatorname{tr}D=\nabla\cdot\mathbf u=0.
$$
Here $p$ is <pressure>, $I$ is the <identity matrix>, $\mu$ is <dynamic viscosity>, and $D$ is the <strain-rate tensor>. The convention is $(\nabla\mathbf u)_{ij}=\partial_j u_i$; $T$ denotes <transpose> and $\operatorname{tr}$ denotes <trace>. Across a plane with unit <normal vector> $\mathbf n$, the viscous <traction> is $\boldsymbol\sigma_{\mathrm{visc}}\mathbf n$. The <tensor> makes this rule work for any plane orientation. https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/fluids/fluids.pdf[David Tong, Fluid Mechanics, section 3.1.1, equations (3.5)–(3.6)]{ref}

In the worked <Couette flow>, $\mathbf u=(Uy/h,0,0)$, so
$$
D=\frac{U}{2h}\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\1&0&0\\0&0&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\boldsymbol\sigma_{\mathrm{visc}}
=\begin{pmatrix}0&2&0\\2&0&0\\0&0&0\end{pmatrix}\,\mathrm{Pa}.
$$
In particular, $D_{xy}=10\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ while the <shear rate> is $20\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$; the factor $2$ in $2\mu D$ recovers $\tau_{xy}=2\,\mathrm{Pa}$. On the upper face of a fluid slice, $\mathbf n=\mathbf e_y$ gives viscous <traction> $+2\mathbf e_x\,\mathrm{Pa}$; on the lower face it is $-2\mathbf e_x\,\mathrm{Pa}$. Constant <stress> can therefore transmit a <force> between the plates even though its <divergence>, and the net viscous <force> on each slice, vanish.

Compare planar extension $\mathbf u=(ax,-ay,0)$ with <solid-body rotation> $\mathbf u=(-\Omega y,\Omega x,0)$:
$$
D_{\mathrm{extension}}=\begin{pmatrix}a&0&0\\0&-a&0\\0&0&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\nabla\mathbf u_{\mathrm{rotation}}=\begin{pmatrix}0&-\Omega&0\\\Omega&0&0\\0&0&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
D_{\mathrm{rotation}}=0.
$$
Extension changes lengths and produces viscous <normal stresses> $\pm2\mu a$. Rotation preserves distances and gives zero <viscous stress>, despite its nonzero <velocity gradient> and <vorticity> $2\Omega\mathbf e_z$. For $a=\Omega=1\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and $\mu=0.10\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$, extension gives $\sigma_{\mathrm{visc},xx}=0.20\,\mathrm{Pa}$ and $\sigma_{\mathrm{visc},yy}=-0.20\,\mathrm{Pa}$; rotation gives zero. A rotating fluid may still have a <pressure gradient> supplying its centripetal <acceleration>.

\Image[viscosity-strain-versus-rotation.png]
{title=Deformation produces Newtonian viscous stress; rigid rotation does not}
{description=Blue arrows show instantaneous velocity. The dashed square is a material patch at $t=0$; its orange outline at $t=0.65\,\mathrm s$ is obtained from the exact trajectories of planar extension and solid-body rotation, with $a=\Omega=1\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. The marked corner makes rotation visible. Both motions preserve area, but only extension changes the patch's shape.}

For an isotropic <Newtonian fluid> in three-dimensional <compressible flow>, separate shape change from <volume> change:
$$
\boldsymbol\sigma_{\mathrm{visc}}
=2\mu\left(D-\frac{\nabla\cdot\mathbf u}{3}I\right)
+\zeta(\nabla\cdot\mathbf u)I.
$$
The first term is <deviatoric stress>, with zero <trace>; $\zeta$ is <volume viscosity>, also called <bulk viscosity>. For uniform expansion $\mathbf u=b\mathbf x$, $D=bI$ and $\nabla\cdot\mathbf u=3b$, so the shear contribution vanishes and the viscous <stress> is $3\zeta bI$. Thus <dynamic viscosity> and <volume viscosity> describe distinct responses, and <incompressible flow> removes only the volume-change term. https://notes.shreyasmandre.com/fluid-dynamics-notes/Ch2.html[Shreyas Mandre, Fluid Dynamics Lecture Notes, section 2.11.3, equation (2.49)]{ref}

= Reynolds number
{parent=Dynamic viscosity}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Re}=\rho UL/\mu=UL/\nu$}

The <Reynolds number> $\operatorname{Re}=UL/\nu$ compares inertial and viscous effects. Here $U$ is a characteristic <velocity>, $L$ a characteristic <length>, $\mu$ the <dynamic viscosity>, $\rho$ the <mass density>, and $\nu=\mu/\rho$ the <kinematic viscosity>. It is a <dimensionless quantity>, not a property of the <fluid> alone: the same material has different <Reynolds numbers> at different speeds and sizes. Specify the chosen scales when reporting it.

The comparison comes from the <Navier-Stokes equation>. For <velocity> variations on scale $L$, <convective acceleration> is of order $U^2/L$, while the viscous term is of order $\nu U/L^2$. Their ratio is $UL/\nu$. Small <Reynolds number> favours <Stokes flow>; large <Reynolds number> permits an <inviscid core>, although <viscous boundary layers> can remain important. Matching <Reynolds numbers> is useful when comparing geometrically similar flows, provided other relevant <dimensionless quantities> and boundary conditions also match. https://uclnatsci.github.io/2021/Electromagnetism-Fluids-and-Waves/fluids/scaling.html[UCL, Scaling arguments, section 34.1.1]{ref}

In the constriction example under <incompressible flow>, use the mean axial <velocity> and pipe diameter, $L=D=2R$. Taking $\rho=1000\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ and a stipulated <dynamic viscosity> $\mu=0.001\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$ gives
$$
\mathrm{Re}_1=\frac{1000(0.20)(0.020)}{0.001}=4000,\qquad
\mathrm{Re}_2=\frac{1000(0.80)(0.010)}{0.001}=8000.
$$
Halving the radius at fixed <volumetric flow rate> doubles this diameter-based <Reynolds number>, because mean <velocity> increases fourfold. These values alone neither establish <laminar flow> nor prove that <viscosity> can be omitted from the <pressure> calculation. Increasing $\mu$ to $0.10\,\mathrm{Pa\,s}$ with the same imposed flow gives $40$ and $80$, but strengthens viscous effects; small <Reynolds number> does not justify <Bernoulli's principle>.

The name recalls Osborne Reynolds's 1883 experimental investigation of water motion and resistance in pipes. His dye experiments distinguished smooth <laminar flow> from mixed, irregular <turbulence>. https://cir.nii.ac.jp/crid/1362544419765899904[Reynolds, 1883, original paper abstract and bibliographic record]{ref} https://uclnatsci.github.io/2021/Electromagnetism-Fluids-and-Waves/fluids/scaling.html[UCL, Scaling arguments, section 34.1.4]{ref} There is no universal transition value applicable to every geometry and disturbance environment. https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hkm2/PDFs/Moffatt_1977_GordonandBreach_Slogfdatohdt_149.pdf[H. K. Moffatt, transition discussion, p. 208]{ref}

= Mass diffusivity
{title2=$\kappa$}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

Mass diffusivity is the coefficient multiplying the Laplacian in a diffusion law for a concentration or density anomaly.

= Vorticity
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\omega=\nabla\times\mathbf u$}

Use <vorticity> to distinguish the local spin of a <fluid element> from the motion of its center along a <pathline>. Circular <streamlines> can occur both with and without <vorticity>: the two <velocity fields> below even have the same <circulation> around the unit <circle>.

\Image[vorticity-and-circulation-fields.png]{title=Solid-body rotation and a potential line vortex: equal unit-circle circulation, different local vorticity}

For a continuously differentiable <velocity field> $\mathbf u=(u_x,u_y,u_z)$, <vorticity> is the <curl> vector
$$
\boldsymbol\omega=\nabla\times\mathbf u=\left(\frac{\partial u_z}{\partial y}-\frac{\partial u_y}{\partial z},\frac{\partial u_x}{\partial z}-\frac{\partial u_z}{\partial x},\frac{\partial u_y}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial u_x}{\partial y}\right).
$$
Each <partial derivative> divides <velocity> by distance, so <vorticity> has units $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. In a planar <velocity field> independent of $z$, only $\omega_z=\partial_xu_y-\partial_yu_x$ survives. Positive $\omega_z$ points along $+\mathbf e_z$: the <right-hand rule> associates it with counterclockwise spin viewed from positive $z$ toward the plane. https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/fluid-mechanics/fluids1.pdf[David Tong, Fluid Mechanics, section 2.2]{ref}

The local rotational <angular velocity> is half the <vorticity>, $\boldsymbol\Omega_{\rm spin}=\boldsymbol\omega/2$. To see the factor, split the <velocity gradient> $L_{ij}=\partial_ju_i$ into its symmetric <rate-of-strain tensor> $E=(L+L^T)/2$ and antisymmetric <spin tensor> $W=(L-L^T)/2$. A nearby displacement $\delta\mathbf x$ has relative <velocity> $L\delta\mathbf x$; its rotational part satisfies $W\delta\mathbf x=(\boldsymbol\omega/2)\times\delta\mathbf x$. This is a proof outline of the local spin interpretation. The <rate-of-strain tensor> can also change the angle of a particular <material line>, so that line's <angular velocity> need not equal $\boldsymbol\omega/2$.

For <solid-body rotation>, $\mathbf u=(-\Omega y,\Omega x,0)$ gives $\boldsymbol\omega=(0,0,2\Omega)$ and $\boldsymbol\Omega_{\rm spin}=(0,0,\Omega)$. Taking $\Omega=1\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$, the <vorticity> is $2\mathbf e_z\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$, although the <angular velocity> is only $1\mathbf e_z\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$. For the potential <line vortex>, $\mathbf u=K\mathbf e_\theta/r$, the planar <curl> is $r^{-1}\partial_r(ru_\theta)=r^{-1}\partial_rK=0$ wherever $r>0$. Its circular <pathlines> therefore coexist with zero local spin.

This distinction makes <vorticity> useful for recognizing <shear flow> as well as swirling motion; taking the <curl> of the <Euler equations> also removes a <pressure gradient> and leads to the <vorticity equation>. A historical landmark is Hermann von Helmholtz's 1858 paper on <fluid vortex> motion, an early study devoted to <vortex dynamics>. https://zenodo.org/records/1448878[H. Helmholtz, Über Integrale der hydrodynamischen Gleichungen, welche den Wirbelbewegungen entsprechen (1858)]{ref}

= Vorticity of a linear shear flow
{parent=Vorticity}
{title2=$\omega_z=-\gamma,\quad\Omega_{{\rm spin},z}=-\gamma/2$}

Use a <linear shear flow> to see why straight <pathlines> can still carry local spin. Faster layers pull past slower ones, and a small symmetric paddle can turn clockwise while its center travels straight ahead.

\Image[shear-flow-vorticity.png]{title=Straight particle paths in linear shear coexist with clockwise local spin and material-line deformation}

For the planar <simple shear flow> $\mathbf u=(\gamma y,0,0)$ with constant signed <shear rate> $\gamma$, direct <differentiation> gives
$$
\omega_z=\partial_xu_y-\partial_yu_x=-\gamma,\qquad\Omega_{{\rm spin},z}=-\frac\gamma2.
$$
With $\gamma=1\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$, these are $-1\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and $-0.5\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$: both signs indicate clockwise orientation under the <right-hand rule>. Yet a particle initially at $(x_0,y_0)$ follows the straight <pathline> $x(t)=x_0+\gamma y_0t$, $y(t)=y_0$. The <curl> measures differences of <velocity> between neighboring particles, rather than bending of one particle's <pathline>.

The <velocity gradient> has $L_{xy}=\gamma$ as its only nonzero entry. Its <spin tensor> supplies clockwise rotation, while its <rate-of-strain tensor> supplies simultaneous deformation. For a <material line> making angle $\alpha$ to the horizontal, the equation $\dot{\delta\mathbf x}=L\delta\mathbf x$ gives $\dot\alpha=-\gamma\sin^2\alpha$. Initially horizontal and vertical <material lines> therefore have <angular velocities> $0$ and $-\gamma$; their mean is $-\gamma/2$. At $t=0.5\ \mathrm{s}$ with $\gamma=1\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$, an initially vertical displacement becomes $(0.5,1)$ times its original length and has angle $\arctan 2\approx63.435^\circ$, a clockwise change of $26.565^\circ$. The plotted cross deforms; an actual paddle's response also depends on its shape and interaction with the fluid. The exact local spin statement concerns the <spin tensor>, rather than every tracer's orientation.

= Vortex line
{parent=Vorticity}

A <vortex line> is an <integral> curve of the <vorticity> vector field: its tangent is parallel to $\boldsymbol\omega=\nabla\times\mathbf u$. In a two-dimensional <incompressible flow>, these lines are perpendicular to the flow plane and the <vorticity equation> gives $D\omega/Dt=0$. Since material cross-sectional area is preserved, the flux of <vorticity> through a material <vortex tube>, equal to its <circulation>, is constant.

= Vortex
{disambiguate=fluid mechanics}
{parent=Vorticity}
{wiki=Vortex}

= Fluid vortex
{synonym}

A fluid vortex is a region in which <velocity field> circulates about an axis or center. <Vorticity> measures local rotation, while <circulation> measures the integral of velocity around a closed path. A <phase vortex> is instead a <topological defect> defined by winding of an order parameter; superfluid examples connect the two notions.

= Vortex dynamics
{parent=Vortex (fluid mechanics)}

The evolution of a <vorticity> distribution together with the <velocity field> it induces. A transport equation advances <vorticity>; diagnostic inversion recovers <velocity> from it and the specified <boundary conditions>.

= Nonrotating layerwise-two-dimensional vortex dynamics
{parent=Vortex dynamics}
{title2=$\partial_t\zeta+J(\psi,\zeta)=0,\quad\nabla_h^2\psi=\zeta$}

In the limiting model of infinitely strong stable <density stratification>, small <Froude number> and fixed vertical scales suppress vertical displacement. Each horizontal level evolves by <two-dimensional vortex dynamics>; $z$ labels independent copies of the horizontal <Poisson equation>. The leading normalized <Ertel potential vorticity> is $Q/N^2=\zeta$. This is a constrained asymptotic model: finite <buoyancy frequency> restores vertical motion and coupling, and arbitrarily short vertical scales invalidate the independent-layer limit. https://pordlabs.ucsd.edu/wryoung/theorySeminar/pdf14/McIntyrePV.pdf[McIntyre's discussion of potential-vorticity inversion] explains the limiting independent-layer picture.

= Two-dimensional vortex dynamics
{parent=Vortex dynamics}
{title2=$\zeta_t+J(\psi,\zeta)=0,\quad\nabla^2\psi=\zeta$}

For inviscid planar <incompressible flow>, write <velocity> as $(-\psi_y,\psi_x)$ and <vorticity> as $\zeta=\nabla^2\psi$. Taking <curl> of the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> removes <pressure> and gives the displayed transport equation, with $J(\psi,\zeta)=\psi_x\zeta_y-\psi_y\zeta_x$. The <Poisson equation> then recovers the <streamfunction> at each instant. A velocity tangent to the plane cannot stretch its perpendicular <vorticity> vector.

= Steady planar vorticity as a local function of stream function
{parent=Two-dimensional vortex dynamics}
{title2=$\Delta\psi=F(\psi)\quad\text{locally where }\nabla\psi\ne0$}

In a steady inviscid planar <incompressible flow>, <vorticity> is constant along <streamlines>. A regular <stream function> supplies a local transverse coordinate, so locally $\Delta\psi$ depends only on $\psi$. A global single-valued function needs the same value on every component of each level set. This can fail: $\psi=x^3-x$ gives the steady Euler shear $(u,v)=(0,1-3x^2)$ with constant pressure, but $\psi(0)=\psi(1)=0$ while $\Delta\psi$ is respectively $0$ and $6$.

= Line vortex
{parent=Vortex (fluid mechanics)}
{title2=$\mathbf u=\frac{\Gamma}{2\pi}\frac{\mathbf e_z\times(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|^2}$}

= Point vortex
{synonym}

= Point vortices
{synonym}

An ideal straight <line vortex> in a two-dimensional <incompressible flow> has <circulation> $\Gamma$ and induces velocity
$$
\mathbf u(\mathbf r)=\frac{\Gamma}{2\pi}\frac{\mathbf e_z\times(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|^2}.
$$
Its <stream function>, with $\mathbf u=(\psi_y,-\psi_x)$, is $\psi=-\Gamma\log|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|/(2\pi)$. Positive $\Gamma$ means counterclockwise <circulation>. In an ideal point-vortex description, the vortex position is advected by the regular velocity produced by the other vortices and the boundaries; its own singular velocity is excluded.

= Motion of two point vortices
{parent=Line vortex}
{title2=$\dot{\mathbf d}=(\kappa_1+\kappa_2)J\mathbf d/(2\pi|\mathbf d|^2)$}

Take vortex strength to mean circulation and $J(x,y)=(-y,x)$. Each <point vortex> moves with the <velocity> induced by the other, so for $\mathbf d=\mathbf x_1-\mathbf x_2$ the displayed equation holds. Since $\mathbf d\cdot J\mathbf d=0$, separation is constant; weighting the two velocities also gives $d(\kappa_1\mathbf x_1+\kappa_2\mathbf x_2)/dt=0$. Equal strengths at opposite points of a circle of radius $a$ rotate with angular speed $\kappa/(4\pi a^2)$.

= Image vortex at a plane wall
{parent=Line vortex}
{title2=$U=\frac{\Gamma}{4\pi b}$}

The <method of images> enforces impermeability at a plane wall by reflecting a <line vortex> across it and reversing its <circulation>. The two logarithmic <stream functions> cancel on the wall, which is consequently a <streamline>. For a vortex of <circulation> $\Gamma$ at $(0,b)$ above $y=0$, the image of <circulation> $-\Gamma$ at $(0,-b)$ induces velocity $(\Gamma/(4\pi b),0)$ at the real vortex. The signed drift parallel to the wall is therefore $\Gamma/(4\pi b)$.

= Line-vortex trajectory in a quarter-plane
{parent=Image vortex at a plane wall}
{title2=$r\sin2\theta=\text{constant}$}

In the quadrant $x,y>0$, a <line vortex> of <circulation> $\Gamma$ at $(x,y)$ has three images: circulations $-\Gamma$ at $(-x,y)$ and $(x,-y)$, and $+\Gamma$ at $(-x,-y)$. Their induced velocity is
$$
\dot x=\frac{\Gamma x^2}{4\pi y(x^2+y^2)},\qquad\dot y=-\frac{\Gamma y^2}{4\pi x(x^2+y^2)}.
$$
In <polar coordinates>, these equations give $\dot r=\Gamma\cot(2\theta)/(2\pi r)$ and $\dot\theta=-\Gamma/(4\pi r^2)$. Direct <differentiation> yields $\frac d{dt}(r\sin2\theta)=0$, so the trajectory obeys $r\sin2\theta=\text{constant}$.

= Hydrodynamic Biot-Savart kernel
{parent=Vorticity}
{title2=$\mathbf u(\mathbf x)=\frac1{4\pi}\int\frac{\boldsymbol\omega(\mathbf y)\times(\mathbf x-\mathbf y)}{|\mathbf x-\mathbf y|^3}\,d^3y$}

In an unbounded incompressible fluid with localized <vorticity> and no added harmonic background, velocity is reconstructed by $\mathbf u(\mathbf x)=(4\pi)^{-1}\int\boldsymbol\omega(\mathbf y)\times(\mathbf x-\mathbf y)/|\mathbf x-\mathbf y|^3\,d^3y$. Its multipole expansion relates <hydrodynamic impulse> to a dipolar far-field velocity.

= Hydrodynamic impulse
{parent=Vorticity}
{title2=$\mathbf I=\frac12\int\mathbf x\times\boldsymbol\omega\,dV$}

<Hydrodynamic impulse> per unit density is $\mathbf I=\tfrac12\int\mathbf x\times\boldsymbol\omega\,dV$ for localized <vorticity>. In unforced all-space <incompressible flow> its derivative vanishes when far-field fluxes vanish. For a spherical all-space limit, $\mathbf I=(3/2)\lim_R\int_{V_R}\mathbf u\,dV$; a dipolar velocity tail makes the distinction from a bare <momentum> integral important.

= Dipolar velocity field of a localized eddy
{parent=Hydrodynamic impulse}
{title2=$\mathbf u_{\rm far}=[3\mathbf n(\mathbf I\cdot\mathbf n)-\mathbf I]/(4\pi r^3)$}

A localized <vorticity> distribution with nonzero <hydrodynamic impulse> induces $\mathbf u\sim[3\mathbf n(\mathbf I\cdot\mathbf n)-\mathbf I]/(4\pi r^3)$. This <curl>-free exterior velocity can remain long-ranged even when the <vorticity> is localized. In a random ensemble it produces dipolar longitudinal and transverse correlation tails.

= Vortex tube
{parent=Vorticity}

A <vortex tube> is a bundle of vortex lines bounded by a surface to which <vorticity> is tangent. <Circulation> measures its <vorticity> flux. Stretching a nearly incompressible tube reduces its cross-sectional area and increases its <vorticity>. The <Burgers vortex> is a local model in which this concentration balances viscous diffusion.

= Enstrophy
{parent=Vorticity}
{wiki}
{title2=$Z=\langle\omega^2\rangle/2$}

<Enstrophy> measures squared <vorticity>: a local density is $\omega^2/2$, and its spatial integral or ensemble mean gives a global measure. In homogeneous <incompressible flow>, <viscous dissipation> satisfies $\epsilon=\nu\langle\omega^2\rangle$. <Vortex stretching> can produce <enstrophy> in three dimensions; <kinematic viscosity> destroys it.

= Mean enstrophy balance
{parent=Enstrophy}
{title2=$\dot Z=\langle\omega_i\omega_jS_{ij}\rangle-\nu\langle|\nabla\times\boldsymbol\omega|^2\rangle$}

For unforced homogeneous <incompressible flow>, the <vorticity equation> gives $\dot Z=\langle\omega_i\omega_jS_{ij}\rangle-\nu\langle|\nabla\times\boldsymbol\omega|^2\rangle$. <Curl> and full-gradient squared averages agree because the <vorticity> is divergence free and boundary divergences average to zero. In established high-Reynolds-number <turbulence>, slow integral-time evolution makes production and destruction nearly balance.

= Planar vorticity velocity kernel
{parent=Vorticity}
{title2=$K(z)=\pm z^\perp/(2\pi|z|^2)$}

= Planar Biot-Savart kernel
{synonym}

The <velocity> induced by planar <vorticity> is a <convolution> with this kernel, whose sign depends on the <stream function> and vorticity conventions. Its magnitude is $(2\pi|z|)^{-1}$ and its derivative is bounded by $C|z|^{-2}$. Splitting the convolution at radius $R$ gives $\|K*\omega\|_\infty\leq R\|\omega\|_\infty+\|\omega\|_1/(2\pi R)$. The same singularity yields a <log-Lipschitz modulus>, enough for a <Yudovich characteristic flow>.

= Uniform-vorticity circular flow in an annulus
{parent=Vorticity}
{title2=$u_\phi=V(4a^2/r-r)/(3a)$}

Circular streamlines and constant <vorticity> imply $u_\phi=\omega r/2+C/r$. Zero velocity at $2a$ and positive azimuthal velocity $V$ at $a$ give
$$
u_\phi=\frac V{3a}(4a^2/r-r),\qquad \omega=-\frac{2V}{3a}.
$$
The radial <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> give $p_r=\rho u_\phi^2/r$, yielding outer-minus-inner pressure $(15-16\log2)\rho V^2/18$. The pressure difference is independent of circulation orientation.

= Vorticity cross-product identity
{parent=Vorticity}
{title2=$(\nabla\times U)\times v=(v\cdot\nabla)U-(\nabla U)^Tv$}

For differentiable vector fields, the displayed identity relates <vorticity> to the antisymmetric part of the <velocity gradient>. Together with the <curl of a cross product>, it converts the <adjoint linearized Navier-Stokes evolution> between component and rotational forms.

= Convective acceleration identity
{parent=Vorticity cross-product identity}
{title2=$(\mathbf u\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u=\nabla(|\mathbf u|^2/2)-\mathbf u\times\boldsymbol\omega$}

The local vector-calculus identity
$$
(\mathbf u\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u=\nabla(\tfrac12|\mathbf u|^2)-\mathbf u\times(\nabla\times\mathbf u)
$$
separates the convective <acceleration> into a kinetic-energy gradient and a <vorticity> cross-product contribution. Substitution into the steady <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> yields the vorticity form of the <Bernoulli equation>.

= Hydrodynamical helicity
{parent=Vorticity}
{title2=$H_k=\int\mathbf u\cdot\boldsymbol\omega\,dV$}
{wiki}

= Kinetic helicity
{synonym}

= Hydrodynamic helicity
{synonym}

Kinetic helicity measures the alignment and linking of a <velocity field> and its <vorticity>. It is the volume integral of $\mathbf u\cdot\boldsymbol\omega$, with boundary conditions needed for its conservation.

The integral measures linkage and twisting of <vortex lines> through the <velocity> and <vorticity> fields.

= Kinetic helicity density
{parent=Hydrodynamical helicity}
{title2=$q=\mathbf u\cdot\boldsymbol\omega$}

The local density of <kinetic helicity> is $q=\mathbf u\cdot\boldsymbol\omega$. Conservation of its volume integral does not imply that $q$ is a materially advected scalar.

= Helicity vector of a solenoidal Fourier mode
{parent=Kinetic helicity density}
{title2=$\tfrac12\operatorname{Re}(i\hat{\mathbf u}^*\times\hat{\mathbf u})=H\mathbf k$}

For a transverse complex velocity amplitude $\hat{\mathbf u}$ and real nonzero <wavevector> $\mathbf k$, write $\hat{\mathbf u}=\mathbf a+i\mathbf c$. Solenoidality makes both real vectors perpendicular to $\mathbf k$, so $\tfrac12\operatorname{Re}(i\hat{\mathbf u}^*\times\hat{\mathbf u})=-\mathbf a\times\mathbf c=H\mathbf k$. The corresponding spatial average of <kinetic helicity density> is $-Hk^2$. Cross-product order determines this sign.

= Kinetic helicity conservation law
{parent=Kinetic helicity density}

For smooth inviscid <barotropic fluid> motion without gravity or magnetic forces, let $w$ be the <specific enthalpy> with $\nabla w=\rho^{-1}\nabla p$, and set $q=\mathbf u\cdot\boldsymbol\omega$. Then
$$
\partial_tq+\nabla\cdot\left[q\mathbf u+\left(w-\frac{u^2}{2}\right)\boldsymbol\omega\right]=0.
$$
This follows by dotting the <barotropic vorticity transport> equation with $\mathbf u$ and using the <divergence of a cross product>. A volume integral is constant when the resulting boundary flux vanishes.

= Material conservation of kinetic helicity density
{parent=Kinetic helicity conservation law}

The <kinetic helicity conservation law> implies
$$
\frac{Dq}{Dt}=\boldsymbol\omega\cdot\nabla\left(\frac{u^2}{2}-w\right)-q\nabla\cdot\mathbf u.
$$
Therefore $q$ is materially constant exactly when the right side vanishes. <Incompressible flow> together with constancy of $w-u^2/2$ along vortex lines is sufficient. In compressible flow, the density-normalized quantity satisfies $D(q/\rho)/Dt=-(\boldsymbol\omega/\rho)\cdot\nabla(w-u^2/2)$.

= Absolute vorticity
{parent=Vorticity}
{wiki}

Absolute vorticity is the vorticity measured in an inertial frame. In a frame rotating with angular velocity $\boldsymbol\Omega$, it is $\boldsymbol\omega+2\boldsymbol\Omega$.

= Planetary vorticity
{parent=Absolute vorticity}
{title2=$f=2\Omega\sin\phi$}

The planetary contribution to absolute <vorticity> is supplied by the rotating frame. Its local vertical component is the <Coriolis parameter> $f=2\Omega\sin\phi$. On a <beta plane>, motion with northward velocity $v$ changes this contribution at the rate $\beta v$.

= Absolute-vorticity flux tensor
{parent=Absolute vorticity}
{title2=$T_{ij}=u_i\omega_j-\omega_i u_j$}

For a flow satisfying <incompressibility>, this antisymmetric tensor writes advection and <vortex stretching> in conservative form. With its first index as transport direction, $(\nabla\cdot T)_j=\partial_iT_{ij}$.

= Radial vorticity conservation in a shearing sheet
{parent=Absolute-vorticity flux tensor}
{title2=$\partial_t\omega_x+\partial_iT_{ix}=0$}

When the only nonpotential force points radially, its <curl> has no radial component. The radial <absolute vorticity> therefore obeys a local flux <conservation law>, although <vortex stretching> can change it along a fluid trajectory.

= Relative vorticity
{title2=$\boldsymbol\omega$}
{parent=Vorticity}

Relative vorticity is the curl of velocity measured in the chosen rotating frame. Adding the planetary vorticity gives <absolute vorticity>.

= Circulation
{disambiguate=physics}
{parent=Vorticity}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Gamma_C=\oint_C\mathbf u\cdot d\mathbf l$}

= Circulation
{synonym}

Use <circulation> to summarize how much a <velocity field> runs along an oriented <closed curve> $C$, such as a <circle> around a <fluid vortex>. Its definition is the <line integral>
$$
\Gamma_C=\oint_C\mathbf u\cdot d\mathbf l,
$$
where $d\mathbf l$ is tangent to $C$ in the chosen direction. Reversing that direction reverses the sign of <circulation>. Its units are $\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$, because <velocity> is multiplied by a length. Counterclockwise <circulation> in the horizontal plane is positive when the chosen <unit normal vector> is $+\mathbf e_z$, according to the <right-hand rule>.

For <solid-body rotation> $\mathbf u=(-\Omega y,\Omega x,0)$, a counterclockwise <circle> of radius $R$ has constant tangential <velocity> $\Omega R$. Multiplying by its circumference gives
$$
\Gamma_C=2\pi\Omega R^2.
$$
For the potential <line vortex> $\mathbf u=K\mathbf e_\theta/r$, the same <circle> instead gives $\Gamma_C=(K/R)(2\pi R)=2\pi K$, independent of $R$. Stipulate $\Omega=1\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$, $R=1\ \mathrm m$ and $K=1\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$: both <velocity fields> have $\Gamma_C=2\pi\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}\approx6.283185\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$, although their <vorticities> in the fluid are $2\mathbf e_z\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and zero respectively. On a <circle> of radius $2\ \mathrm m$, their <circulations> are $8\pi\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$ and $2\pi\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$. The comparison plot in <vorticity> shows this difference in radius dependence.

The bridge between <circulation> and <vorticity> is <Stokes theorem>:
$$
\Gamma_{\partial S}=\int_S\boldsymbol\omega\cdot\mathbf n\,dS.
$$
Here the <velocity field> must be continuously differentiable on a neighborhood of the entire spanning surface $S$ inside the fluid, and its boundary orientation must match the <unit normal vector> $\mathbf n$. For <solid-body rotation>, the disk is admissible and its <surface integral> is $2\Omega\pi R^2$. For the potential <line vortex>, the origin is singular and excluded from the fluid: a disk crossing it is not admissible. On an <annulus>, <Stokes theorem> instead gives $\Gamma_{R_{\rm out}}-\Gamma_{R_{\rm in}}=0$, with both <circulations> here measured counterclockwise; the induced inner boundary direction is clockwise. Nonzero <circulation> therefore does not imply nonzero local <curl> in the available fluid. https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/fluid-mechanics/fluids1.pdf[David Tong, Fluid Mechanics, section 2.2, circular-flow example]{ref}

This is useful because <Stokes theorem> lets a local <vorticity> measurement determine a loop's <circulation> when a suitable surface exists, while <Kelvin's circulation theorem> tracks <circulation> along a moving <material curve>. The beauty is the precise link between a local <derivative> and a global <line integral>, with the domain deciding which conclusions are valid.

= Kelvin's circulation theorem
{parent=Circulation (physics)}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$d\Gamma/dt=0$}

In an inviscid barotropic fluid subject to conservative body force, the <circulation> of a material loop is constant. For constant density, parameterize that loop by $\mathbf r(s,t)$ with $\mathbf r_t=\mathbf u$. Differentiating $\Gamma=\int\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf r_s\,ds$ gives an integral of $(D\mathbf u/Dt)\cdot d\mathbf r$ plus $\int\partial_s(|\mathbf u|^2/2)ds$. The latter vanishes on a closed loop, and the <Euler equations> make the former the integral of a gradient, also zero. For a barotropic density, $\nabla p/\rho$ is still a gradient of specific enthalpy. Constant circulation is consistent with changing local <vorticity> when a vortex tube changes its cross-sectional area.

= Irrotational circulation in an exterior domain
{parent=Kelvin's circulation theorem}

In smooth barotropic inviscid flow with conservative <body force>, <Kelvin's circulation theorem> preserves circulation of <material curves>. Initially zero <vorticity> therefore gives zero circulation on contractible loops for all times. A loop winding around an excluded obstacle can have a nonzero constant circulation: the planar field $\mathbf u=\Gamma\mathbf e_\theta/(2\pi r)$ is <irrotational> outside the origin but has circulation $\Gamma$ on a positively oriented enclosing loop.

= Irrotational flow
{parent=Vorticity}
{wiki}
{title2=$\nabla\times\mathbf u=\mathbf0$}

Use <irrotational flow> to identify where a <velocity potential> can describe the <velocity field> locally. An <irrotational flow> has $\boldsymbol\omega=\nabla\times\mathbf u=\mathbf0$ throughout the fluid region. For a continuously differentiable <velocity field>, the <Poincare lemma> supplies a <velocity potential> on sufficiently small neighborhoods. A global single-valued <velocity potential> requires zero <circulation> around every <closed curve>; a <simply connected domain> is a sufficient domain assumption.

The potential <line vortex> $\mathbf u=K\mathbf e_\theta/r$ is an <irrotational flow> on the punctured plane, or on an <annulus> $a<r<b$ with $a>0$, even though its <pathlines> are circular. Every sufficiently small loop bounding a disk in that fluid has zero <circulation>, by <Stokes theorem>. A loop winding once counterclockwise around the excluded origin has <circulation> $2\pi K$. Thus local <irrotational flow> and nonzero <circulation> around a hole are compatible: the enclosing disk passes outside the domain of the <velocity field>.

An <irrotational flow> is a kinematic condition on a <velocity field>, independent of whether a particular model neglects <viscosity> or assumes <incompressible flow>. Adding <incompressible flow> makes the local <velocity potential> satisfy the <Laplace equation>. This reduces the number of unknowns and makes <potential flow> a powerful starting point for flow around bodies and for <gravity waves>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-016-hydrodynamics-13-012-fall-2005/c472432debcf6ee250209b68cf18cc12_2005reading4.pdf[A. H. Techet, MIT Hydrodynamics reading 4, pp. 1–2]{ref}

= Circular vortex-sheet mode
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

For a circular patch in solid-body rotation next to stationary fluid, a sinusoidal boundary displacement couples an interior $r^k$ potential to an exterior $r^{-k}$ potential. The tangential velocity jump makes the interface Kelvin--Helmholtz unstable.

\Include[gravity-wave]{parent=Fluid mechanics}

= Stream function
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

= Streamfunction
{synonym}

A two-dimensional incompressible velocity field can be written either as $u=\psi_y$, $v=-\psi_x$ or with both signs reversed. The continuity equation then holds identically.

= Sinusoidal cellular flow
{parent=Stream function}
{title2=$\psi_0=-\sin x\sin y$}

Using $\mathbf u=(-\psi_y,\psi_x)$, the displayed <stream function> gives $\mathbf u_0=(\sin x\cos y,-\cos x\sin y)$. Its <streamlines> are level sets of $\psi_0$. At $(n\pi,m\pi)$, the <Jacobian matrix> is $\operatorname{diag}((-1)^{n+m},-(-1)^{n+m})$, so the points are <saddle equilibria>. At $(\pi/2+n\pi,\pi/2+m\pi)$ the eigenvalues are $\pm i$, giving <center equilibria> surrounded by closed <streamlines>. The grid lines $x=n\pi$ and $y=m\pi$ form <heteroclinic orbits> between adjacent saddles; circulation alternates between cells.

= Melnikov splitting of a periodically perturbed cellular flow
{parent=Sinusoidal cellular flow}
{title2=$M(X_0,t_0)=\frac{\pi\omega}{\sinh(\pi\omega/2)}\sin[\omega(t_0-\log\tan(X_0/2))]$}

Perturb the <sinusoidal cellular flow> by $\epsilon\cos x\cos y\sin(\omega t)$ in its <stream function>. On $y=0$, parameterizing the unperturbed orbit with $q_0(0)=(X_0,0)$ gives $x(s)=2\arctan e^{s+a}$, $a=\log\tan(X_0/2)$. The <heteroclinic Melnikov function for a periodic planar flow> has integrand $\operatorname{sech}^2(t-t_0+a)\sin(\omega t)$. Its odd part vanishes and the Fourier transform of $\operatorname{sech}^2$ gives the displayed result. For each fixed nonzero frequency its simple zeros produce transverse intersections at sufficiently small perturbation, hence exchange of fluid lobes across the former cell boundary. The leading splitting is exponentially small at high frequency; that estimate is not uniform in frequency and perturbation size.

= Linear planar strain and rotation streamlines
{parent=Stream function}
{title2=$\psi=\alpha xy-\frac\beta2(x^2+y^2)$}

The incompressible planar field $(\alpha x-\beta y,\beta x-\alpha y)$ has the displayed <stream function> under $(u,v)=(\psi_y,-\psi_x)$ and vorticity $2\beta$. In rotated coordinates $s=(x+y)/\sqrt2$, $t=(x-y)/\sqrt2$, $\psi=((\alpha-\beta)s^2-(\alpha+\beta)t^2)/2$. For positive parameters its nonstationary <streamlines> are closed ellipses if $\alpha<\beta$, hyperbolas and straight separatrices if $\alpha>\beta$, and parallel straight lines with a stationary diagonal if $\alpha=\beta$.

= Axisymmetric hydrodynamic mass flux function
{parent=Stream function}
{title2=$\rho\mathbf u_p=\nabla\psi\times\nabla\phi$}

For a steady axisymmetric compressible <fluid flow>, <mass conservation> permits this representation. The mass flow through an annulus between two stream surfaces is $2\pi\Delta\psi$. In a steady adiabatic wind without magnetic forces, the <specific entropy>, <specific angular momentum> and <Bernoulli function> are locally functions of $\psi$ on connected regular stream surfaces.

= Stokes streamfunction
{c}
{parent=Stream function}
{title2=$\psi(r,z)$}

For axisymmetric incompressible flow without swirl, $u_r=-\psi_z/r$ and $u_z=\psi_r/r$. These identities enforce incompressibility, and $2\pi\psi$ measures axial volume flux from the symmetry axis when its additive constant is chosen there.

= Streamline classification of a planar linear saddle or centre
{parent=Stream function}

For $(u,v)=(y,ax)$, the stream function $\psi=(y^2-ax^2)/2$ gives hyperbolic streamlines and a saddle when $a>0$, while $a<0$ gives elliptical streamlines and a centre.

= Linear planar flow with strain and rotation
{parent=Streamline classification of a planar linear saddle or centre}
{title2=$\psi=\epsilon xy-\frac{\gamma}{2}(x^2+y^2)$}

The incompressible linear flow $(u,v)=(\epsilon x-\gamma y,\gamma x-\epsilon y)$ has <streamfunction> $\psi=\epsilon xy-\gamma(x^2+y^2)/2$. Its velocity matrix squares to $(\epsilon^2-\gamma^2)I$. Thus strain-dominated flow has saddle trajectories, rotation-dominated flow has closed elliptical trajectories, and equal nonzero strain and rotation gives <simple shear flow> along parallel lines with a stationary line. Pure rotation is counterclockwise for positive $\gamma$.

= Streamline
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

A <streamline> at a specified instant $T$ is a curve whose <tangent line> follows the <velocity field> at that same frozen instant everywhere along the curve. One convenient <integral curve> parameterization is
$$
\frac{d\boldsymbol\gamma}{ds}=\mathbf u(\boldsymbol\gamma(s),T),\qquad T\text{ fixed}.
$$
Here $s$ is an auxiliary curve parameter, with units of seconds in this convention; advancing $s$ does not advance physical time. Any regular reparameterization preserves the tangent geometry. Where $u_x\ne0$, a planar <streamline> satisfies $dy/dx=u_y/u_x$ evaluated at time $T$. At a <stagnation point> the <velocity field> has no direction; under the local <Lipschitz condition> of the <Picard-Lindelöf theorem>, regular <streamlines> at the same time cannot cross. A <streamline> answers which directions fit together now, making it useful for describing local flow geometry. https://web.mit.edu/fluids-modules/www/potential_flows/LecturesHTML/lec02/tutorial/[MIT fluid mechanics modules, tutorial on streamlines, streaklines and pathlines]{ref}

In the analytical <velocity field> $(U,at)$ introduced under <pathline>, the <streamline> through the origin at time $T$ is
$$
y=\frac{aT}{U}x.
$$
At $T=2\ \mathrm s$ this is $y=2x$ when both coordinates are expressed in metres. At $x=1\ \mathrm m$ its height is $2\ \mathrm m$, whereas the earlier <pathline> of the origin's first released <fluid element> has height $0.5\ \mathrm m$. Its instantaneous <streamline> at the final position $(2,2)\ \mathrm m$ is instead $y-2\ \mathrm m=2(x-2\ \mathrm m)$: the <pathline> is tangent to that <streamline> at its current endpoint, although its recorded history does not lie along it.

\Image[stream-path-streak-lines-three-curves.png]
{title=Analytical comparison of a frozen-time streamline, one pathline and a streakline}

The green <streamline> uses the <velocity field> at $T=2\ \mathrm s$ throughout; the orange <pathline> uses times $0\leq t\leq2\ \mathrm s$; the purple <streakline> uses different release times at the single observation time $T=2\ \mathrm s$. The grey arrows all show the instantaneous <velocity> direction at $T$. These are exact analytical curves with chosen units, not measured dye shapes.

In a <steady flow> in a fixed <reference frame>, a <pathline> and a <streamline> solve the same autonomous <ordinary differential equation>. Uniqueness makes each particle released from a fixed point follow the same <integral curve>, so its <pathline>, the <streakline> from that point and the <streamline> share one geometrical curve. Finite recording or release intervals usually show different segments of that curve; the comparison assumes particles stay in the domain and treats stationary <fluid elements> separately. Neither two-dimensionality nor <incompressibility> is needed for this coincidence. Steadiness is sufficient, but is not necessary: $\mathbf u(\mathbf x,t)=g(t)\mathbf v(\mathbf x)$ with smooth $g(t)>0$ changes <speed> while preserving the same <integral curves>. In a general time-dependent <velocity field>, however, the three curves differ. Kline explains why continuous dye release in <steady flow> can therefore display <streamlines>. https://web.mit.edu/hml/ncfmf/05FV.pdf[S. J. Kline, Flow Visualization film notes, section II]{ref}

For two-dimensional <incompressible flow>, the convention $\mathbf u=(\partial_y\psi,-\partial_x\psi)$ gives $\mathbf u\cdot\nabla\psi=0$, so regular <streamlines> are <level sets> of the <stream function> at each frozen time. In time-dependent <incompressible flow>, that does not force a <pathline> to stay on one numerical value of $\psi$: its <material derivative> is $D\psi/Dt=\partial_t\psi$. The distinction is the difference between a geometric snapshot and a parcel's history.

= Stream tube
{parent=Streamline}

A <stream tube> is a narrow tube whose side boundary consists of <streamlines>. In steady flow no fluid crosses its sides, so continuity makes its <mass flux> $A\rho u$ constant. If flow follows a <magnetic field> with constant <magnetohydrodynamic mass loading>, its normal area obeys $A|\mathbf B|=\mathrm{constant}$.

= Velocity potential
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathbf u=\nabla\phi$}

Use a <velocity potential> to reconstruct an <irrotational flow> from one <scalar field>. A <velocity potential> $\phi(\mathbf x,t)$ satisfies
$$
\mathbf u=\nabla\phi.
$$
It has units $\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$ and is unchanged as a description of the <velocity field> when any function of time alone is added. For twice continuously differentiable $\phi$, the identity <curl of a gradient> proves zero <vorticity>. In <incompressible flow>, taking the <divergence> also gives $\nabla^2\phi=0$, the <Laplace equation>. This turns a three-component <velocity field> into a single <harmonic function> with appropriate <boundary conditions>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-016-hydrodynamics-13-012-fall-2005/c472432debcf6ee250209b68cf18cc12_2005reading4.pdf[A. H. Techet, MIT Hydrodynamics reading 4, pp. 1–3]{ref}

For <uniform flow> $\mathbf u=(U,0,0)$, take $\phi=Ux$. With $U=2\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, the change from $x=0$ to $x=3\ \mathrm m$ is $\Delta\phi=6\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$. By the <fundamental theorem for line integrals>, the <line integral> of <velocity> between these points equals that change along any path in the domain; a <closed curve> gives zero <circulation>.

For the potential <line vortex>, choose a local angle branch in <polar coordinates> and set $\phi=K\theta$. Then $u_r=\partial_r\phi=0$ and $u_\theta=r^{-1}\partial_\theta\phi=K/r$. One counterclockwise turn changes the continued <velocity potential> by $2\pi K$, although the <velocity field> returns to its original value. With $K=1\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$, this change is $2\pi\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$, exactly the <circulation>. A full <annulus> therefore admits local angle branches and a multivalued <velocity potential>, but no global single-valued <velocity potential> when $K\ne0$. Cutting the <annulus> along a radial line allows a single-valued angle branch on the remaining domain. The obstruction is <path independence>, not a failure of the local <gradient> formula.

= Potential flow around a circular cylinder
{parent=Velocity potential}

Uniform irrotational flow of speed $U$ past a circular cylinder of radius $a$, with zero circulation, has <velocity potential>
$$
\phi=U\left(r+\frac{a^2}{r}\right)\cos\theta.
$$
On the cylinder, the tangential speed is $-2U\sin\theta$, so the <Bernoulli equation> gives
$$
p(a,\theta)=p_\infty+\frac12\rho U^2(1-4\sin^2\theta).
$$

= Potential flow around a circular cylinder with circulation
{parent=Potential flow around a circular cylinder}

Adding circulation $\kappa$ gives the possibly multivalued <velocity potential>
$$
\phi=U\left(r+\frac{a^2}{r}\right)\cos\theta+\frac\kappa{2\pi}\theta.
$$
The velocity remains single-valued, and on the cylinder its tangential component is $-2U\sin\theta+\kappa/(2\pi a)$.

= Kutta–Joukowski theorem
{c}
{parent=Potential flow around a circular cylinder with circulation}
{wiki}

For steady two-dimensional inviscid flow of density $\rho$, far-field velocity $\mathbf U$, and circulation $\kappa$, the force per unit span is perpendicular to the flow. With counterclockwise circulation taken positive and $\mathbf U=U\mathbf e_x$, it is $-\rho\kappa U\mathbf e_y$.

= Advective and pressure contributions to circulatory lift
{parent=Kutta–Joukowski theorem}
{title2=$\oint(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf n)\mathbf u\,dl=\tfrac12\boldsymbol\Gamma\times\mathbf U$}

For <uniform flow> $\mathbf U$ plus a <line vortex> of counterclockwise <circulation> $\Gamma$, the outward advective <momentum flux> around a large circle is $\rho\boldsymbol\Gamma\times\mathbf U/2$. The <Bernoulli equation> supplies an equal <pressure> contribution. Their sum is the force on the fluid; the force on an obstacle per unit span is its negative, $\rho\mathbf U\times\boldsymbol\Gamma$. Retaining only the advective term loses half the lift and obscures its sign.

= Radially symmetric incompressible flow in a planar annulus
{parent=Velocity potential}

For liquid occupying $a(t)<r<b(t)$, radial symmetry and <incompressible flow> imply
$$
u_r(r,t)=\frac{a\dot a}{r},
\qquad
\phi(r,t)=a\dot a\log r+C(t).
$$
The <kinematic boundary condition> at the outer interface gives
$$
b^2-a^2=\text{constant},
$$
which expresses conservation of the liquid's area.

= Pressure in radially symmetric annular potential flow
{parent=Radially symmetric incompressible flow in a planar annulus}

If the pressure at $r=b(t)$ is $p_\infty$, the <unsteady Bernoulli equation> gives
$$
p(a,t)-p_\infty
=\rho\left[\frac d{dt}(a\dot a)\log\frac ba
-\frac{(a\dot a)^2}{2}\left(\frac1{a^2}-\frac1{b^2}\right)\right].
$$

= Small oscillation of a planar gas bubble in an annular liquid
{parent=Pressure in radially symmetric annular potential flow}

For $a=a_0(1+\epsilon)$, equilibrium outer radius $b_0$, and a bubble gas obeying $p_0\pi a^2=\text{constant}$, the <linearization> is
$$
\rho a_0^2\log\frac{b_0}{a_0}\,\ddot\epsilon
=-2p_\infty\epsilon.
$$
The bubble therefore undergoes <simple harmonic motion> with
$$
\omega^2=\frac{2p_\infty}{\rho a_0^2\log(b_0/a_0)}.
$$

= Plume
{disambiguate=fluid dynamics}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

A <plume (fluid dynamics)> is a localized buoyancy-driven flow generated by a source of heat or another density-changing substance. A plume can be laminar or turbulent; the transport regime is an additional condition.

= Laminar plume
{parent=Plume (fluid dynamics)}

A laminar plume is a narrow buoyancy-driven flow in which viscous diffusion balances inertia across the plume.

= Integral momentum-flux balance for a two-dimensional plume
{parent=Laminar plume}

For a steady incompressible plume with localized vertical body force $b(x)\delta(y)$, decay of velocity and shear at transverse infinity gives
$$
\frac{d}{dx}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\rho u^2\,dy=b(x).
$$

= Similarity scaling of a two-dimensional laminar plume
{parent=Laminar plume}

If $b(x)=Bx^{-1/5}$, balancing the momentum flux and transverse viscosity gives
$$
\Delta\sim\left(\frac{\mu^2}{\rho B}\right)^{1/3}x^{2/5},
\qquad
U\sim\left(\frac{B^2}{\rho\mu}\right)^{1/3}x^{1/5}.
$$

= Similarity equation for a two-dimensional laminar plume
{parent=Similarity scaling of a two-dimensional laminar plume}

With $u=-\psi_y$, $v=\psi_x$, $\eta=y/\Delta$, and $\psi=U\Delta f(\eta)$, the plume boundary-layer equation reduces to
$$
f'''+\frac15(f')^2-\frac35ff''=\delta(\eta).
$$

= Euler equations for an inviscid fluid
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Euler equations
{synonym}
{c}

= Euler momentum equation
{c}
{synonym}

= Euler equation for fluid motion
{c}
{synonym}

= Euler equation
{c}
{synonym}

Euler’s equations express momentum conservation in a fluid without viscosity.

= Linearized Euler equations
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}

The linearized Euler equations retain only first-order perturbations about a specified inviscid fluid state. Together with the linearized <continuity equation> and a pressure-density constitutive relation, they describe small-amplitude <acoustic waves>. About a uniform resting state, $\rho_0\partial_t\mathbf u'=-\nabla p'$, $\partial_t\rho'+\rho_0\nabla\cdot\mathbf u'=0$, and an isentropic pressure perturbation has $p'=c_0^2\rho'$. Gradients and advection of a moving background must be retained when its variation is not negligible.

= Conservative energy flux of a polytropic ideal gas
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}
{title2=$E=\tfrac12\rho u^2+\frac p{\gamma-1},\quad F_E=u_x(E+p)$}

For an unmagnetized <ideal gas> without gravity, the <pressure> equation gives internal-energy balance $\partial_te+\partial_x(u_xe)=-p\partial_xu_x$, where $e=p/(\gamma-1)$. The momentum equation gives kinetic-energy balance with source $-u_x\partial_xp$. Their sum is conservative with the displayed total-energy flux. Tangential <velocities> contribute to <kinetic energy> even when all derivatives are in the normal direction. Smooth <polytropic flow> advects its <entropy> parameter; a physical shock need not preserve that parameter across the discontinuity.

= Crocco form of the unsteady Euler equation
{c}
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}
{title2=$\partial_t\mathbf u+\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf u+\nabla\varepsilon=T\nabla s$}

For a fixed-composition inviscid fluid, <specific enthalpy> satisfies $dw=Tds+dp/\rho$. Combining this identity with the <vorticity cross-product identity> gives the displayed equation, with $\boldsymbol\omega=\nabla\times\mathbf u$ and $\varepsilon=u^2/2+\Phi+w$. It exposes entropy gradients as the thermodynamic source of a mismatch between <Bernoulli function> gradients and the vorticity term. In a steady <adiabatic process>, its projection along a <streamline> makes $\varepsilon$ constant there.

= Yudovich characteristic flow
{c}
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}
{title2=$\dot X(t)=u(t,X(t))$}

For a planar Euler velocity obtained from vorticity in $L^\infty_{\mathrm{loc}}(dt;L^1\cap L^\infty)$, the <planar vorticity velocity kernel> gives a locally integrable speed bound and <log-Lipschitz modulus>. Spatial mollification gives existence of global trajectories; the <Osgood uniqueness criterion> gives uniqueness and continuous dependence. Measurability in time and an initial trace are needed. This establishes the flow for a fixed Euler solution; nonlinear uniqueness of the Euler solution itself requires further comparison arguments.

= Bernoulli's principle
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$p/\rho+|\mathbf u|^2/2+\Phi=B$}

= Bernoulli principle
{c}
{synonym}

The <Bernoulli principle> relates <pressure>, <kinetic energy> per unit volume and <Newtonian gravitational potential> along a <streamline> of <steady flow> with <inviscid flow>. For constant <mass density>, the <Bernoulli equation> gives $p/\rho+u^2/2+\Phi$ constant along that <streamline>. Constancy across different <streamlines> requires further hypotheses, such as <irrotational flow>.

Precisely, suppose a <fluid> of constant positive <mass density> obeys the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid>, its <velocity field> and <pressure> are steady, and the body <force> per unit <mass> is $-\nabla\Phi$. Then
$$
B=\frac{p}{\rho}+\frac{|\mathbf u|^2}{2}+\Phi,\qquad \mathbf u\cdot\nabla B=0.
$$
Every term has units of <energy> per unit <mass>. For a uniform gravitational field, <Newtonian gravitational potential> is $\Phi=gz$, up to an additive constant. The <pressure> term represents work by surrounding <fluid>, so acceleration can exchange <pressure> work with <kinetic energy>. Dotting the steady <Euler equations> with $\mathbf u$ gives the displayed <directional derivative>: this is why constancy follows a <streamline>. https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/mem2/FLUIDS-IB/dyn.pdf[Cambridge, Fluid Dynamics IB, Dynamics, section 2.4]{ref}

Continue the horizontal water constriction example from <incompressible flow>. Assume negligible viscous losses, <steady flow> and approximately <uniform flow> at both measurement sections, so the <streamline> speeds there equal the mean speeds $0.20$ and $0.80\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Equal height cancels the <Newtonian gravitational potential> terms. The ideal <pressure> drop is
$$
p_1-p_2=\frac{\rho}{2}(U_2^2-U_1^2)=\frac{1000}{2}(0.80^2-0.20^2)=300\,\mathrm{Pa}.
$$
This is the <Venturi effect> in its ideal limit: the gain in <kinetic energy> density is supplied by <pressure> work. Cambridge's treatment uses this relation to connect a measured <pressure> difference to <volumetric flow rate>. https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/mem2/FLUIDS-IB/dyn.pdf[Cambridge, Fluid Dynamics IB, Dynamics, section 2.5]{ref}

The $300\,\mathrm{Pa}$ result is conditional, rather than a prediction of the actual viscous pipe. <Mass conservation> fixes $Q$ and the ratio of mean speeds even with <viscosity>, but nonuniform <velocity profiles> require an integrated <energy> balance, and wall friction dissipates mechanical <energy>. A pump would supply additional work. In particular, constant mean speed in a straight pipe does not imply constant <pressure>: <Poiseuille flow> needs a continuing <pressure gradient> to overcome <viscosity>. https://uclnatsci.github.io/2021/Electromagnetism-Fluids-and-Waves/fluids/bernoulli.html[UCL, Bernoulli's equation, assumptions and boundary limitations]{ref}

= Venturi effect
{c}
{parent=Bernoulli's principle}
{wiki}

In ideal steady flow, a contraction of a pipe increases the speed and lowers the pressure. For constant <mass density>, equal body-force potential at the ends and approximately uniform endpoint speeds, <conservation of mass> and the <Bernoulli equation> give $\dot M=S_1S_2\sqrt{2\rho(p_1-p_2)/(S_1^2-S_2^2)}$ for flow from a wider to a narrower section. Elevation changes contribute a body-force potential difference, and nonuniform profiles or viscous losses require additional information.

// Target: probability-and-statistics.bigb

= Axisymmetric inviscid flow between moving parallel plates
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}

For plates $z=\pm h(t)$, incompressibility, impermeability and regularity on the axis determine a z-independent radial velocity $u_r=-r\dot h/(2h)$ and vertical velocity $u_z=z\dot h/h$. A possible azimuthal velocity requires additional rotational data; continuity alone does not determine it. For a smooth regular initial profile $U(r)$ independent of $z$, its azimuthal Euler equation transports it as $u_\theta(r,t)=\lambda U(\lambda r)$, where $\lambda=\sqrt{h/h_0}$. If the rotation is solid-body with angular velocity $\Omega(t)$, the <vorticity equation> gives $\Omega=\Omega_0h/h_0$. Along a particle path, $r^2h$ and $r^2\Omega$ are constant, illustrating <conservation of angular momentum>.

= Radial velocity
{title2=$u_r$}
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}

Radial velocity is the component of a velocity field along the line from a chosen centre to the point of observation.

= Unsteady Bernoulli equation
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}
{c}

For time-dependent <irrotational flow> with $u=\nabla\phi$, constant <mass density>, and no body force, the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> integrate to
$$
\partial_t\phi+\frac12|\nabla\phi|^2+\frac p\rho=C(t).
$$
A time-dependent change of gauge in $\phi$ may set $C(t)=0$.

This is the unsteady potential-flow version of <Bernoulli's principle>, with a time-dependent potential gauge.

= Constant-tension balloon discharge
{parent=Unsteady Bernoulli equation}
{title2=$R^3\ddot R+2R^2\dot R^2=-K,\quad K=\gamma a^2/(2\rho L)$}

A spherical balloon feeding an inviscid uniform tube plug, with entrance excess <pressure> $2\gamma/R$, satisfies $L\dot u=2\gamma/(\rho R)$ and $4\pi R^2\dot R=-\pi a^2u$. Eliminating $u$ yields the displayed equation. Its <first integral> is $R^4\dot R^2+KR^2=C$. From rest at radius $R_0$, $C=KR_0^2$; integration gives $t=R_0^2K^{-1/2}(\pi/4-\theta/2+\sin(2\theta)/4)$, where $\theta=\arcsin(R/R_0)$. The ideal emptying time is $\pi R_0^2/(4\sqrt K)$. Independently prescribed initial flow changes $C$ and therefore this time.

= Inviscid startup in a pressure-driven tube
{parent=Unsteady Bernoulli equation}
{title2=$U(t)=\sqrt{2P/\rho}\tanh\left(\frac tL\sqrt{\frac P{2\rho}}\right)$}

For a slender tube of length $L$ supplied from a large reservoir with excess pressure $P$, negligible gravity and entrance inertia, the <unsteady Bernoulli equation> gives $L\dot U+U^2/2=P/\rho$. A stationary initial liquid column has the displayed speed and <volume flux> $\pi a^2U$ for tube radius $a$. The inertial term controls the early linear acceleration; the kinetic-energy term sets the terminal inviscid speed. This is a leading slender-tube model while the reservoir remains large and the tube full.

= Linearly elastic balloon discharge
{parent=Unsteady Bernoulli equation}
{title2=$\Omega^2=K\pi a^2/(\rho L)$}

An inviscid uniform tube plug driven by entrance excess pressure $KV$ obeys $\rho L\dot u=KV$ and $\dot V=-\pi a^2u$. From rest, the first zero of $V$ occurs after a quarter harmonic period, independent of its initial volume. The maximum speed is proportional to that initial volume. Entrance losses, a reservoir kinetic head or independently prescribed initial flow change this ideal model.

= Clebsch-potential variational derivation of incompressible Euler flow
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}
{c}

For $\mathbf u=\nabla\phi+\beta\nabla\alpha$, the density
$$
\mathcal L=-\beta\alpha_t-\frac12|\mathbf u|^2
$$
gives incompressibility and material conservation of $\alpha$ and $\beta$. Differentiating the Clebsch representation then yields the Euler momentum equation with
$$
p=-\frac12|\mathbf u|^2-\phi_t-\beta\alpha_t.
$$

= Bernoulli equation
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}
{c}

= Bernoulli's equation
{c}
{synonym}

For steady inviscid flow, pressure, kinetic energy density, and conservative potential are constant along a streamline.

= Bernoulli invariant
{c}
{parent=Bernoulli equation}
{title2=$b=u^2/2+\Phi+w$}

In a steady adiabatic inviscid fluid with a conservative body force, the <Bernoulli invariant> is the displayed specific energy, constant along each <streamline>. Dotting the <Euler equations> with velocity and using the <specific enthalpy> identity gives $\mathbf u\cdot\nabla b=0$. In <ideal magnetohydrodynamics>, magnetic work need not vanish; the <planar ideal-MHD Bernoulli invariant> includes the corresponding electromagnetic correction.

= Hydraulic control over a smooth hump
{parent=Bernoulli equation}
{title2=$D+h+Q^2/(2gh^2)=\text{constant}$}

A slowly varying, steady, inviscid channel flow has constant discharge $Q=uh$ per unit width and the displayed <Bernoulli equation>. The bed-height function of depth has its maximum at $h_c=(Q^2/g)^{1/3}$, where the <Froude number> is one. At a nondegenerate critical crest, a differentiable profile changes between the <subcritical flow> and <supercritical flow> branches. With subcritical upstream squared Froude number $F<1$ and upstream depth $h_1$, the shock-free supercritical downstream depth is $h_1(F+\sqrt{F^2+8F})/4$.

= Bernoulli function for planar constant-vorticity flow
{parent=Bernoulli equation}
{c}
{title2=$|\mathbf u|^2/2+\omega\psi+p/\rho=C$}

With the <streamfunction> convention $(u,v)=(\psi_y,-\psi_x)$, constant scalar <vorticity> gives $\mathbf u\times\boldsymbol\omega=-\omega\nabla\psi$. For steady inviscid constant-density flow without body force, this integrates to
$$
\tfrac12|\mathbf u|^2+\omega\psi+p/\rho=C
$$
throughout a connected fluid region. Reversing the streamfunction convention changes the sign of its term; the declared convention matters.

= Steady rotating-frame Bernoulli integral
{parent=Bernoulli equation}
{title2=$\tfrac12|\mathbf v|^2+p/\rho+\Phi-\tfrac12\Omega^2r_\perp^2$}

For <steady flow> of constant-density fluid in a uniformly rotating frame with conservative body forces, the displayed quantity is constant along each relative <streamline>. The <centrifugal potential> contributes the negative quadratic term, and the <Coriolis force> contributes no work because it is perpendicular to the relative velocity. The result follows by dotting the rotating <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> with that velocity.

= Bernoulli function
{parent=Bernoulli equation}
{c}
{wiki}

The Bernoulli function combines pressure, kinetic energy density, and conservative potential.

= Bernoulli equation over topography
{parent=Bernoulli equation}
{c}

For steady potential flow over small hills, linearized Bernoulli pressure combines a hydrostatic term $-\rho g\eta$ with a dynamic term $-\rho U\phi_x$.

= Draining time of a uniform tank through a siphon
{parent=Bernoulli equation}

If a tank and siphon tube have areas $A$ and $a$, the tube outlet is $H$ below an inlet initially submerged by $h_0$, and both free boundaries are at <atmospheric pressure>, continuity and Bernoulli's equation give
$$
t=\sqrt{2}\left(\frac{A^2}{a^2}-1\right)^{1/2}
\frac{\sqrt{H+h_0}-\sqrt H}{\sqrt g}
$$
for the free surface to reach the inlet.

= Integral momentum equation
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}
{wiki}

The integral momentum equation balances momentum flux, pressure, body force, and forces on a control volume.

= Holding force on a contracting nozzle
{parent=Integral momentum equation}
{title2=$F=\rho Q^2(A_0/A_1-1)^2/(2A_0)$}

For steady ideal flow through a horizontal contracting nozzle, <Bernoulli equation> gives inlet gauge pressure $p_0=\rho Q^2(A_1^{-2}-A_0^{-2})/2$. The <integral momentum equation> gives the fluid's force on the nozzle as $p_0A_0-\rho Q^2(A_1^{-1}-A_0^{-1})$ in the outlet direction. Simplification yields the displayed nonnegative magnitude. The external holding force has equal magnitude and the opposite direction.

= Force on a pipe junction
{parent=Integral momentum equation}
{wiki}

The force on a pipe junction follows by balancing inlet and outlet momentum fluxes and pressure forces.

= Conservation of mass in a pipe
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

For steady incompressible pipe flow, volume flux equals cross-sectional area times mean speed and is conserved through a junction.

\Include[viscous-fluid-flow]{parent=Fluid mechanics}

= Surface tension
{title2=$\gamma$}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

<Surface tension> $\gamma$ is the tensile <force> per unit length across a line drawn in a <liquid> interface. For a clean interface with <isotropy>, it has the same value in every tangential direction: each side pulls the other along the surface, perpendicular to the line. A straight segment of length $\ell$ therefore transmits a <force> of magnitude $\gamma\ell$ from one interface. Here we assume constant $\gamma>0$, with fixed <temperature> and composition. The same coefficient measures the reversible <work> required to create <surface area>:
$$
F_{\text{one interface}}=\gamma\ell,\qquad dE_{\mathrm s}=\gamma\,dA,\qquad
[\gamma]=\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}=\mathrm{J\,m^{-2}}.
$$
The equality of units follows from $1\,\mathrm J=1\,\mathrm{N\,m}$; the equality of the mechanical coefficients follows from the moving-rim argument below. https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/11-8-cohesion-and-adhesion-in-liquids-surface-tension-and-capillary-action[OpenStax, College Physics 2e, §11.8: surface tension and the sliding wire]{ref} https://arxiv.org/html/2011.11403v2[Tomas Bohr and Bernhard Scheichl, Surface Tension and Energy Conservation in a Moving Fluid, equations (5) and (13)]{ref}

For a concrete example, take an ideal flat soap film spanning a rectangular wire frame with a straight sliding rim of length $\ell$. Pull the rim outwards in the plane of the film, perpendicular to the rim. Each of the film's two <liquid>–<gas> interfaces pulls the rim back towards the film with <force> $\gamma\ell$, so the outward holding <force> is $F=2\gamma\ell$. The two faces are two interfaces, each with its own <surface tension>. With the assumed toy value $\gamma=0.072\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$ and $\ell=20\,\mathrm{mm}=0.020\,\mathrm m$, the required <force> is $2.88\,\mathrm{mN}$; one interface would contribute $1.44\,\mathrm{mN}$. This value of $\gamma$ is a stipulated model parameter, rather than a measured value for a particular soap solution.

\Image[surface-tension-film-force.png]
{title=Two film faces double the force on a sliding rim}
{description=The plan view shows the outward pull and the inward <surface tension> <force> on a sliding rim; the two interfaces overlap in this view. The graph compares one and two interfaces at the same assumed constant $\gamma=0.072\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$, with rim length in millimetres and <force> in millinewtons.}

Why does an interface resist being enlarged? Its <molecules> have a different environment from those in the bulk <liquid>; changing that environment has an <energy> cost. In the constant-<surface tension> model, reducing <surface area> lowers <surface energy>. This connects an easily measured rim <force> to the shape of a droplet, the <Young–Laplace equation> for its <pressure>, and the restoring mechanism of <capillary waves>. It is useful because one coefficient links <forces>, <energy> and geometry. https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/fluid-mechanics/fluids3.pdf[David Tong, Fluid Mechanics, §4.1.3, pp. 121–123]{ref}

The moving-rim argument also explains the factor of two without memorizing a special formula. An outward displacement $dx$ creates <surface area> $\ell\,dx$ on each face, hence $dA_{\mathrm{total}}=2\ell\,dx$. Slow pulling without <viscous dissipation> requires <work> $F\,dx=\gamma\,dA_{\mathrm{total}}$, giving $F=2\gamma\ell$. For one interface, the same argument gives $F=\gamma\ell$. A <surface tension> gradient instead produces <Marangoni stress>; the constant-$\gamma$ assumption excludes that additional effect.

= Surface energy
{parent=Surface tension}
{title2=$E_{\mathrm s}=\gamma A$}

For a <liquid> interface with constant <surface tension> $\gamma$, the mechanical <surface energy> is the <energy> associated with its <surface area> $A$:
$$
E_{\mathrm s}=\gamma A+C,\qquad
E_{\mathrm s}(A_2)-E_{\mathrm s}(A_1)=\gamma(A_2-A_1).
$$
The additive constant $C$ does not affect the <work> needed to change <surface area>. For several interfaces with the same <surface tension>, $A$ means their total <surface area>; the two faces of a thin soap film must both be counted. https://arxiv.org/html/2011.11403v2[Tomas Bohr and Bernhard Scheichl, Surface Tension and Energy Conservation in a Moving Fluid, equation (13)]{ref}

With assumed $\gamma=0.072\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$, creating $1\,\mathrm{cm^2}=10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m^2}$ of one interface requires $7.2\,\mathrm{\mu J}$ of reversible <work>. In the sliding-rim example illustrated under <surface tension>, moving a $20\,\mathrm{mm}$ rim outwards by $1\,\mathrm{mm}$ creates $4.0\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{m^2}$ across the two faces and requires $2.88\,\mathrm{\mu J}$. Independently, multiplying its $2.88\,\mathrm{mN}$ holding <force> by the displacement gives the same <work>.

This makes the preference for a round isolated droplet precise. At fixed <volume> $V$, the three-dimensional <isoperimetric inequality> gives
$$
A^3\geq36\pi V^2,
$$
with equality for a <sphere>. Thus a <sphere> minimizes <surface energy> among sufficiently regular closed shapes enclosing the same <volume>, when <surface tension> is uniform and other <energy> contributions are negligible. This is an application of the <isoperimetric inequality>, rather than a proof of it. Walls, <wetting>, and <gravity> can change the shape by adding constraints or other <energy> contributions. https://math.hmc.edu/funfacts/isoperimetric-inequality/[Francis E. Su et al., Isoperimetric Inequality, Math Fun Facts]{ref}

The differential rule $dE_{\mathrm s}=\gamma\,dA$ integrates directly to $E_{\mathrm s}=\gamma A+C$ because <surface tension> is constant. At fixed <liquid> <volume>, decreasing <surface area> can drive <viscous fluid flow>; the released <surface energy> can supply <viscous dissipation>. This mechanical bookkeeping is useful for predicting which deformations can release <energy>. If <surface tension> depends on <temperature> or composition, distinguish this mechanical area-<work> relation from the complete interfacial <thermodynamic free energy> and <internal energy>.

= Wetting
{parent=Surface tension}
{wiki}

= Contact line
{parent=Wetting}

The line where the interfaces of three phases meet, for example where a liquid film meets a solid and the surrounding gas. Continuum assumptions at this line can differ from the bulk <lubrication approximation>.

= Bond number
{c}
{parent=Surface tension}
{title2=$\mathrm{Bo}=\Delta\rho\,g\ell^2/\gamma$}
{wiki}

= Eotvos number
{c}
{synonym}

The Bond number compares hydrostatic stress over a length $\ell$ with capillary stress. For a gas bubble of radius $a$ and negligible density, $\mathrm{Bo}=\rho ga^2/\gamma$. Small Bond number is the usual sufficient criterion for capillarity to resist gravity-scale deformations. For the clean-bubble terminal speed, the <capillary number> is $\mathrm{Ca}=\mu U/\gamma=\mathrm{Bo}/3$.

= Oil lens capillary balance
{parent=Surface tension}
{title2=$h^2=2\Gamma/((\rho_w-\rho_o)g)$}

For a broad, thin oil lens below a flat ice-water interface, define the effective interfacial energy $\Gamma=\gamma_{io}+\gamma_{ow}-\gamma_{iw}$ from the relevant <surface tensions>. At fixed oil volume $\mathcal V=Ah$, the energy is $E=\Gamma\mathcal V/h+\frac12(\rho_w-\rho_o)g\mathcal Vh$. Setting its <derivative> to zero gives $h^2=2\Gamma/[(\rho_w-\rho_o)g]$ for $\Gamma>0$, and the positive <second derivative> proves a minimum. A contact angle measured through the oil gives $\Gamma=\gamma_{ow}(1-\cos\vartheta)$. Oil-ice tension alone does not determine this effective quantity. Complete wetting or rough underside pockets invalidate this finite, flat-lens approximation. With densities $1025$ and $850\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, a lens below $1\,\mathrm{cm}$ requires $0<\Gamma<0.08584\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$.

= Contact angle
{parent=Surface tension}
{title2=$\theta_c$}

The contact angle is measured through a liquid between its interface and a solid substrate at their contact line. In a gently sloping two-dimensional film on a horizontal substrate, its small-angle value is the limiting magnitude of the height slope. A zero contact angle means a tangential approach to the substrate; it does not imply bounded curvature, as the limiting <zero-flux thermocapillary film profiles> illustrate.

= Capillary number
{parent=Surface tension}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ca}=\mu U/\gamma$}
{wiki}

The capillary number compares viscous stresses with <surface tension>. Its velocity and viscosity conventions must be specified; a porous-medium displacement can use the <Darcy velocity> and the viscosity of the displaced fluid.

= Capillary pressure
{title2=$p_c=\gamma\kappa$}
{parent=Surface tension}
{wiki}

Capillary pressure is the pressure jump produced by a curved interface. For constant surface tension it is proportional to the interface curvature according to the <Young–Laplace equation>.

= Surfactant
{parent=Surface tension}
{wiki}

A surfactant is an interfacially active substance that changes surface tension. A concentration gradient therefore generates tangential <Marangoni effect>[Marangoni stress].

= Surfactant transport with exchange relaxation
{parent=Surfactant}
{title2=$D_t C=-C\nabla_s\cdot\mathbf u_s-Cu_n\kappa+D_s\Delta_sC-k(C-C_0)$}

The <material derivative> of surface concentration combines tangential and normal area change, <surface diffusion>, and relaxation towards a reservoir concentration $C_0$. The rate $k$ represents exchange; setting $k=0$ recovers transport without exchange. The concentration is measured per interfacial area, so stretching dilutes it.

= Translating surfactant-coated bubble
{parent=Surfactant transport with exchange relaxation}
{title2=$\lambda=\gamma_1C_0a/[3\mu(ka^2+2D_s)]$}

A spherical inviscid bubble moving slowly through a viscous liquid has a dipolar <surfactant> perturbation. The associated <Marangoni stress> reduces the tangential velocity from its clean-bubble value towards zero. In the linear regime, with $\mathbf u_s=A(I-\mathbf n\mathbf n)\mathbf U$, the consistent <Newtonian fluid stress tensor> gives $A=-1/[2(1+2\lambda)]$.

= Normal stress balance on a translating bubble
{parent=Translating surfactant-coated bubble}

The dipolar normal <traction> is balanced by buoyant <hydrostatic pressure> and the concentration-dependent normal capillary stress, in addition to the constant gas pressure and equilibrium Laplace pressure. A degree-one shape perturbation translates a sphere rather than deforming it; the balance fixes the terminal speed. A uniform gas pressure alone cannot balance this mode.

= Dipolar surfactant distribution
{parent=Translating surfactant-coated bubble}
{title2=$C'=B\mathbf U\cdot\mathbf n$}

Linear steady transport on a translating sphere gives $B=2AC_0a/(ka^2+2D_s)$. The components of its unit <normal vector> are degree-one <spherical harmonics>. The condition $2|A|Ua/(ka^2+2D_s)\ll1$ ensures the concentration perturbation is small relative to $C_0$.

= Surfactant stabilization of film rupture
{parent=Surfactant}

Outward interfacial flow from a thinning region dilutes surfactant there and raises its <surface tension>. The resulting <Marangoni effect>[Marangoni stress] pulls fluid back toward the thinning region, opposes further stretching, and slows rupture.

= Strong-surfactant long-wave rupture maximum
{parent=Surfactant stabilization of film rupture}
{title2=$K_m^2\sim1/(2\Gamma),\quad s_{\max}\sim3V^2/(4\mu\gamma h_0^5)$}

For strong <Marangoni stress> and large $\Gamma=\gamma h_0^2/(3V)$, the unstable band has $K\ll1$. Its rate is asymptotically $[3V/(\mu h_0^3)]K^2(1-\Gamma K^2)/3$. Maximizing this quadratic in $K^2$ gives the displayed finite-wavelength maximum. The growth timescale is not alone a bubble lifetime: initial disturbance size and thickness evolution also enter.

= Insoluble surfactant
{parent=Surfactant}

An insoluble surfactant remains on an interface. Without surface diffusion, its material concentration changes only through the local surface-area dilation rate.

= Surface diffusion
{parent=Insoluble surfactant}
{title2=$D_s\Delta_s C$}
{wiki}

Surface diffusion transports particles along an interface down a <concentration> gradient. On an isotropic smooth interface with constant diffusivity $D_s$, its flux is $-D_s\nabla_sC$ and its concentration source is $D_s\Delta_sC$, using the <surface gradient> and <surface Laplacian>.

= Conservation of insoluble surfactant on a moving interface
{parent=Insoluble surfactant}

For concentration $C$ per unit <surface area>, conservation on a moving material interface gives
$$
\frac{DC}{Dt}=-C\left[\nabla_s\cdot\mathbf u_s+u_n\nabla_s\cdot\mathbf n\right]+D_s\Delta_sC.
$$
The <material derivative> follows the surface particles. The two dilution terms are tangential expansion and curvature-induced area change from normal velocity $u_n$; the last term is <surface diffusion>.

= Quadrupolar surfactant distribution on a spherical interface
{parent=Conservation of insoluble surfactant on a moving interface}

For radius $a$ and tangential velocity $\mathbf u_s=\mathbf I_s\mathbf A\mathbf x$ with symmetric traceless $\mathbf A$, the <surface divergence> is $-3\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf A\mathbf n$. At small surface <Péclet number> $a^2|\mathbf A|/D_s$, neglecting advection of the concentration perturbation gives
$$
C-C_0=\frac{C_0a^2}{2D_s}\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf A\mathbf n.
$$
The result follows because this quadratic is a degree-two <spherical harmonic> with <surface Laplacian> $-6/a^2$ times itself and zero spherical mean.

= Marangoni effect
{c}
{parent=Surface tension}
{wiki}

= Marangoni stress
{c}
{synonym}

The Marangoni effect is interfacial flow driven by a surface-tension gradient. Tangential stress balances the surface gradient $\nabla_s\gamma$.

= Thermocapillary migration of an insulating bubble
{parent=Marangoni effect}
{title2=$\mathbf U=-a\gamma_T\mathbf H/(2\mu)$}

For a clean spherical gas bubble with negligible internal <dynamic viscosity> and <thermal conductivity>, a weak temperature gradient produces the displayed migration <velocity>, where $\gamma_T=d\gamma/dT$. The exterior <Papkovich–Neuber representation> can use only the scalar potential $\chi=-a^3\mathbf U\cdot\mathbf r/(2r^3)$. Its surface normal velocity equals $\mathbf U\cdot\mathbf n$, and its tangential <traction> balances minus the surface <surface tension> gradient from the <temperature dipole around an insulating sphere>. For the usual $\gamma_T<0$ the bubble moves toward warmer liquid. Small <Reynolds number>, <Péclet number>, <capillary number>, and fractional surface-tension variation justify the approximation; <surfactants> can oppose the temperature-driven <Marangoni stress>.

= Interfacial stress balance with variable surface tension
{parent=Marangoni effect}

Across a fluid interface, the jump in normal traction is the surface tension times curvature, while the tangential traction jump is the surface gradient of surface tension. For one common normal convention,
$$
(\boldsymbol\sigma_+-\boldsymbol\sigma_-)\mathbin\cdot\mathbf n
=\gamma\kappa\mathbf n-\nabla_s\gamma.
$$

= Marangoni immobilization of a bubble in straining flow
{c}
{parent=Interfacial stress balance with variable surface tension}

For an almost spherical inviscid bubble in exterior <Stokes flow> $\mathbf E\mathbf x$, let $\gamma=\gamma_0-\gamma_1(C-C_0)$ and $K=C_0a^2/(2D_s)$. The <quadrupolar surfactant distribution on a spherical interface> makes the tangential velocity $\mathbf u_s=\alpha\mathbf I_s\mathbf E\mathbf x$ satisfy
$$
\alpha=\frac5{5+2M},\qquad M=\frac{K\gamma_1}{\mu a}.
$$
The <Marangoni stress> approaches the stress needed to suppress surface motion as $M$ grows. The quadrupolar shape perturbation $r=a(1+\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf D\mathbf n)$ is
$$
\mathbf D=\frac{5\mu a}{\gamma_0}\frac{2+M}{5+2M}\mathbf E.
$$
These expressions require small deformation and small surface <Péclet number>; large $M$ alone does not justify a small concentration perturbation.

= Chemophoresis
{parent=Marangoni effect}

Chemophoresis is the motion of a particle or droplet driven by a gradient in chemical concentration. For a surfactant-coated bubble, adsorption converts the chemical gradient into a <Marangoni stress>.

= Rayleigh–Plateau instability
{c}
{parent=Surface tension}
{wiki}

The Rayleigh–Plateau instability makes a sufficiently long cylindrical fluid thread unstable to axisymmetric radius perturbations. Surface tension lowers area by amplifying wavelengths longer than the circumference-scale threshold.

= Capillary instability of an annular liquid lining
{parent=Rayleigh–Plateau instability}
{title2=$\beta=\frac{\sigma h_0^3}{3\mu}k^2(a^{-2}-k^2)$}

A thin liquid lining inside a rigid cylindrical tube has <pressure> perturbation $p_1=-\sigma(a^{-2}h_1+h_{1,xx})$ at leading order in $h_0/a$. <Lubrication theory> gives flux $q=-h_0^3p_x/(3\mu)$, so <mass conservation> gives the displayed growth rate. Long waves with $0<k^2a^2<1$ grow, while short waves are stabilized by <curvature>. The maximum occurs at $k^2a^2=1/2$.

= Surfactant stabilization of an annular liquid lining
{parent=Capillary instability of an annular liquid lining}
{title2=$\beta^2+q[\alpha+\lambda(q-1)]\beta+\alpha\lambda q^2(q-1)/4=0$}

For <insoluble surfactant> with $\sigma=\sigma_0-A\Gamma$ and positive base tension, let $q=k^2a^2$, $\lambda=h_0^3(\sigma_0-A\Gamma_0)/(3\mu a^4)$ and $\alpha=h_0A\Gamma_0/(\mu a^2)$. The coupled film-height and advected-<surfactant> amplitudes give the displayed growth equation. The unstable band remains $0<q<1$. Weak <surfactant> elasticity gives $\beta_{\max}=\lambda/4-3\alpha/8+O(\alpha^2/\lambda)$; strong elasticity gives $\beta_{\max}\sim\lambda/16$. The <Marangoni stress> reduces tangential surface motion; strong elasticity reduces lubrication mobility to the value for an effectively immobile interface.

= Young–Laplace equation
{c}
{parent=Surface tension}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta p=\gamma\kappa$}

The <Young–Laplace equation> relates the <pressure> jump across a stationary interface to its <curvature> and <surface tension>. Choose a unit <normal vector> $\mathbf n$ pointing from the inside region to the outside region, and define $\kappa=\nabla_s\cdot\mathbf n$ to be positive for a convex <sphere>. In mechanical <equilibrium>, with constant <surface tension> and no additional normal <viscous stresses>,
$$
\Delta p=p_{\mathrm{in}}-p_{\mathrm{out}}
=\gamma\kappa=\gamma(k_1+k_2).
$$
Here $k_1,k_2$ are the signed <principal curvatures> in this convention. Their sum is twice the corresponding signed <mean curvature>; stating the convention prevents a factor-of-two ambiguity. The <Young–Laplace equation> is a local <normal stress> balance for one interface. https://arxiv.org/html/2011.11403v2[Tomas Bohr and Bernhard Scheichl, Surface Tension and Energy Conservation in a Moving Fluid, equations (8)–(9)]{ref}

A spherical <liquid> droplet of <radius> $R$ in a <gas> has one interface, with $k_1=k_2=1/R$. An ideal thin soap bubble has two <liquid>–<gas> interfaces. Adding their <pressure> jumps gives
$$
\Delta p_{\mathrm{droplet}}=\frac{2\gamma}{R},\qquad
\Delta p_{\mathrm{soap\ bubble}}\simeq\frac{4\gamma}{R}.
$$
For the soap bubble, $\Delta p$ now means the inner <gas> <pressure> minus the outer <gas> <pressure>. The second formula assumes equal <surface tensions> on both faces and film thickness negligible relative to $R$, so their radii can be identified. Retaining distinct radii gives $2\gamma/R_{\mathrm{inner}}+2\gamma/R_{\mathrm{outer}}$. A <gas> bubble inside bulk <liquid> has only one interface and therefore uses $2\gamma/R$. https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/11-8-cohesion-and-adhesion-in-liquids-surface-tension-and-capillary-action[OpenStax, College Physics 2e, §11.8: pressure in a soap bubble]{ref}

With the assumed constant toy <surface tension> $\gamma=0.072\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$, a droplet with $R=1\,\mathrm{mm}$ has excess <pressure> $144\,\mathrm{Pa}$; at $R=0.5\,\mathrm{mm}$ it has $288\,\mathrm{Pa}$. A thin soap bubble assigned the same $\gamma$ and $R=1\,\mathrm{mm}$ has excess <pressure> $288\,\mathrm{Pa}$. These are differences from the surrounding <pressure>, rather than absolute <pressures>. Halving the <radius> doubles the <capillary pressure>; doubling the interface count also doubles it at fixed <radius> and <surface tension>.

\Image[surface-tension-droplet-pressure.png]
{title=Smaller droplets need larger excess pressure; two bubble interfaces double it}
{description=Left: a virtual outward displacement $dR$ of a <sphere> balances <pressure> <work> against increased <surface energy>. Right: excess <pressure> in pascals versus positive <radius> in millimetres for one and two interfaces, using the assumed constant toy $\gamma=0.072\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$.}

The spherical case has a short <work> argument. A virtual increase in <radius> gives $dV=4\pi R^2\,dR$ and $dA=8\pi R\,dR$. At mechanical <equilibrium>, the net <pressure> <work> balances the increase in <surface energy>:
$$
\underbrace{\Delta p\,4\pi R^2\,dR}_{\text{pressure work}}
=\underbrace{\gamma\,8\pi R\,dR}_{\text{surface energy increase}}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \Delta p=\frac{2\gamma}{R}.
$$
For a thin soap bubble, both interfaces gain approximately the same <surface area>, doubling the right-hand side and giving $4\gamma/R$. This is a virtual <work> balance between neighboring spherical configurations, rather than an assumption that a fixed-<volume> droplet actually expands. The general <Young–Laplace equation> replaces the spherical area-to-<volume> ratio by local <curvature>, making geometry directly measurable through <pressure>.

= Gibbs--Thomson relation
{c}
{parent=Young–Laplace equation}
{wiki}

= Gibbs-Thomson effect
{c}
{synonym}

The Gibbs--Thomson relation shifts the equilibrium chemical potential or composition at a curved interface by an amount proportional to its mean curvature. Small droplets therefore have a larger equilibrium solubility than flat interfaces.

= Curvature-induced melting-temperature depression
{parent=Gibbs--Thomson relation}
{title2=$T_I=T_m-\gamma T_m\mathcal K/(\rho L)$}

Curvature depresses the equilibrium <melting> <temperature> of solid convex into liquid. Here $\mathcal K$ is the sum of <principal curvatures>, equal to twice a common convention for <mean curvature>, and $T_m$ is absolute <temperature>. The coefficient uses <solid-liquid surface energy>, <mass density> and <latent heat>. This stabilizes fine corrugations of a <solidification> front.

= Capillary length
{title2=$\ell_c=\sqrt{\gamma/(\rho g)}$}
{parent=Surface tension}
{wiki}

The <capillary length> measures when <gravity> and <surface tension> are comparably important in shaping a <liquid> interface. For a <liquid> of uniform <mass density> $\rho$, beneath a <gas> whose <mass density> is negligible, with uniform <gravitational acceleration> $g>0$ and constant <surface tension> $\gamma>0$,
$$
\ell_c=\sqrt{\frac{\gamma}{\rho g}}.
$$
A deformation of height $a$ and horizontal length scale $L$ has <hydrostatic pressure> variation of order $\rho ga$ and small-slope <capillary pressure> of order $\gamma a/L^2$. Equating these gives $L\sim\ell_c$: <surface tension> smooths small features, whereas <gravity> matters for broad ones. For two resting <fluids>, replace $\rho$ by their positive <mass density> difference $\Delta\rho$ in this static balance. This change alone does not give the two-fluid <wave> dynamics, because both <fluids> can have inertia. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-357-interfacial-phenomena-fall-2010/04759cfc15c79928ca9f72a18b3864dd_MIT18_357F10_lec_all.pdf[John W. M. Bush, Interfacial Phenomena, chapters 5–6: curvature and hydrostatic pressure]{ref}

With the stipulated illustrative parameters $\gamma=0.072\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$, $\rho=1000\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, and $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, the <capillary length> is $\ell_c=2.71\,\mathrm{mm}$. These parameters define the ideal calculations below; they are not a measurement of a particular sample.

\Image[capillary-waves-crossover.png]
{title=The <gravity-capillary wave> crossover occurs at <wavelength> $2\pi\ell_c$, where <phase speed> is smallest}
{description=Calculated <phase velocity> and <group velocity> magnitudes, and the ratio of the <surface tension> and <gravity> terms in the deep-water <dispersion relation>, for the stipulated $\gamma$, $\rho$ and $g$. The <capillary length> is $2.71\,\mathrm{mm}$, while the equality <wavelength> is $17.02\,\mathrm{mm}$. Both panels show ideal theory, without experimental data.}

For a <sinusoid> with <wavenumber> $k>0$, the relevant horizontal scale is $L=1/k$, while its <wavelength> is $\lambda=2\pi/k$. Thus the <Bond number> for this scale and the reciprocal restoring-term ratio are
$$
\mathrm{Bo}_{1/k}=\frac{\rho g}{\gamma k^2}
=\frac{1}{(k\ell_c)^2}
=\left(\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\ell_c}\right)^2,
\qquad
\frac{\text{capillary term}}{\text{gravity term}}
=(k\ell_c)^2.
$$
Equality is at $k_c=1/\ell_c$, hence $\lambda_c=2\pi\ell_c=17.02\,\mathrm{mm}$ for these parameters. Using $L=\lambda$ instead produces a different numerical <Bond number>; state the length convention. The <capillary length> itself is not the equality <wavelength>. This distinction helps choose whether a measured ripple should be modeled as a <surface gravity wave>, a nearly pure <capillary wave>, or a <gravity-capillary wave>. https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/fluid-mechanics/fluids3.pdf[David Tong, Fluid Mechanics, §4.1.3, equations (4.23)–(4.24) and the wavelength factor $2\pi$]{ref}

= Dynamic meniscus
{parent=Surface tension}
{wiki}

A dynamic meniscus is a curved transition region joining a moving thin film to a bulk fluid reservoir.

= Capillary wave
{parent=Surface tension}
{wiki}

A <capillary wave> is a <wave> along a <fluid> interface whose restoring stress is predominantly supplied by <surface tension>. A curved <free surface> requires a <pressure> jump by the <Young–Laplace equation>; increasing its corrugation also costs <surface energy>. In the ideal limit, inertia carries the interface through its flat position and the resulting <oscillation> propagates. When <gravity> also contributes appreciably, use the combined <gravity-capillary wave> model. Within the <capillary wave> regime, shortening a ripple makes it travel faster. https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/fluid-mechanics/fluids3.pdf[David Tong, Fluid Mechanics, §4.1.3: the capillary restoring pressure]{ref}

The <laser-diffraction measurement of capillary waves> below turns this mechanism into a test: set the <frequency>, measure the <wavelength> independently with <light>, and ask whether the <dispersion relation> follows. The theory is useful both for predicting how ripple <wave packets> separate and for inferring <surface tension> from their motion.

= Gravity-capillary wave
{parent=Capillary wave}
{title2=$\omega^2=gk+(\gamma/\rho)k^3$}

= Capillary-gravity wave
{synonym}

A <gravity-capillary wave> has restoring contributions from both <gravity> and <surface tension>. Consider an initially resting, horizontally unbounded <liquid> layer of constant depth $h$, with uniform <mass density> $\rho$, <incompressible flow>, <inviscid flow> and <irrotational flow>. The upper <gas> exerts constant <pressure> and has negligible inertia. Assume a clean interface with constant <surface tension> $\gamma$: no <surfactant> stresses, imposed current or wind. The <wave amplitude> $a$ must satisfy $ka\ll1$ and $a\ll h$, so <linearized boundary conditions> apply. Deep water means $kh\gg1$, rather than a particular depth in metres. https://www.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-354j-nonlinear-dynamics-ii-continuum-systems-spring-2015/6163837c5efa5d6ed969e535ec8f890c_MIT18_354JS15_lectureNotes.pdf[Brenner, Peacock, Stocker, Reis and Dunkel, MIT Nonlinear Dynamics II, §23.1, pp. 105–106]{ref}

Let $z=0$ be the undisturbed <free surface>, with <liquid> below it. <Potential flow> gives a <velocity potential> $\phi$ with $\mathbf u=\nabla\phi$, and <incompressible flow> gives the <Laplace equation>. The linearized <kinematic boundary condition for a free-surface graph> and the <unsteady Bernoulli equation>, including <gravity> and the <Young–Laplace equation>, give
$$
\phi_{xx}+\phi_{zz}=0\quad(z<0),
\qquad
\eta_t=\phi_z,
\qquad
\phi_t+g\eta-\frac{\gamma}{\rho}\eta_{xx}=0\quad(z=0).
$$
Here $\eta(x,t)$ is the interface displacement; the signed small-slope <pressure> jump is $p_{\mathrm{liquid}}-p_{\mathrm{gas}}=-\gamma\eta_{xx}$. A <wave crest> has $\eta_{xx}<0$, so its <capillary pressure> is positive. Choose a right-travelling <Fourier mode> of positive <wavenumber> $k$ and <angular frequency> $\omega$:
$$
\eta=a\cos(kx-\omega t),
\qquad
\phi=\frac{a\omega}{k}e^{kz}\sin(kx-\omega t).
$$
The factor $e^{kz}$ decays into the deep <liquid>, satisfies the <Laplace equation>, and makes $\phi_z=\eta_t$ at $z=0$. Substituting in the dynamic <boundary condition> leaves
$$
-\frac{a\omega^2}{k}+ga+\frac{\gamma}{\rho}k^2a=0,
\qquad
\boxed{\omega^2=gk+\frac{\gamma}{\rho}k^3}.
$$
This derivation displays the two restoring contributions with the same sign. <Dimensional analysis> also checks the result: $\gamma/\rho$ has units $\mathrm{m^3\,s^{-2}}$, so both $gk$ and $(\gamma/\rho)k^3$ have units $\mathrm{s^{-2}}$. https://www.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-354j-nonlinear-dynamics-ii-continuum-systems-spring-2015/6163837c5efa5d6ed969e535ec8f890c_MIT18_354JS15_lectureNotes.pdf[MIT Nonlinear Dynamics II, §23.1, equations (547)–(551): boundary conditions and dispersion]{ref}

At finite depth, a flat impermeable bottom at $z=-h$ requires $\phi_z=0$. Replacing $e^{kz}$ by the normalized vertical factor $\cosh(k(z+h))/\cosh(kh)$ makes the surface ratio $\phi_z/\phi=k\tanh(kh)$. Eliminating the mode amplitudes then gives
$$
\omega^2=\left(gk+\frac{\gamma}{\rho}k^3\right)\tanh(kh).
$$
Thus the same model supplies its own depth check: use the deep-water formula only when $\tanh(kh)$ is sufficiently close to one for the required accuracy. For example, $kh=3$ reduces $\omega$ by about $0.248\%$ relative to deep water, while $kh=1$ reduces it by about $12.7\%$. These percentages are calculations from this factor. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-357-interfacial-phenomena-fall-2010/04759cfc15c79928ca9f72a18b3864dd_MIT18_357F10_lec_all.pdf[John W. M. Bush, Interfacial Phenomena, chapter 19, equation (19.4)]{ref}

In deep water the <phase velocity> magnitude follows a <wave crest>, while the <group velocity> magnitude follows the <wave envelope> of a narrow <wave packet>. Differentiating the positive-frequency <dispersion relation> gives
$$
c_p=\frac{\omega}{k}=\sqrt{\frac{g}{k}+\frac{\gamma k}{\rho}},
\qquad
c_g=\frac{d\omega}{dk}
=\frac{g+3\gamma k^2/\rho}{2\sqrt{gk+\gamma k^3/\rho}},
\qquad
\frac{c_g}{c_p}=\frac{1+3(k\ell_c)^2}{2\left(1+(k\ell_c)^2\right)}.
$$
For $k\ell_c\ll1$, the <surface gravity wave> limit has $c_g\simeq c_p/2$. For $k\ell_c\gg1$, the <deep-water capillary-wave dispersion relation> has $c_g\simeq3c_p/2$: a <wave crest> slips backward relative to the faster <wave envelope>, even while both advance. Neither speed is the oscillating <fluid element>'s own <velocity>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-357-interfacial-phenomena-fall-2010/04759cfc15c79928ca9f72a18b3864dd_MIT18_357F10_lec_all.pdf[John W. M. Bush, Interfacial Phenomena, chapter 19, equation (19.5) and the two group-speed limits]{ref}

There is a particularly neat coincidence at the crossover. Differentiate $c_p^2=g/k+\gamma k/\rho$: its unique minimum is at $k_c=\sqrt{\rho g/\gamma}=1/\ell_c$, exactly where the restoring terms are equal. Therefore
$$
c_{p,\min}=\left(\frac{4g\gamma}{\rho}\right)^{1/4},
\qquad c_g(k_c)=c_p(k_c).
$$
For the stipulated parameters in <capillary length>, the minimum is $0.231\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. At $\lambda=3\,\mathrm{mm}$, direct substitution gives $f=\omega/(2\pi)\simeq131\,\mathrm{Hz}$, $c_p\simeq0.394\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and $c_g\simeq0.580\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. This short <wavelength> is capillary dominated, but its <group velocity> is still slightly below the pure-capillary ratio $3c_p/2$ because <gravity> has not vanished.

= Laser-diffraction measurement of capillary waves
{parent=Capillary wave}

Zhu, Miao, Xu and Cao's 2007 experiment drove a triangular metal frame electromagnetically with a sinusoidal generator. In a roughly $3\,\mathrm{cm}$-deep, temperature-regulated pool, it produced nearly plane <capillary waves>. A $632.8\,\mathrm{nm}$ <laser> illuminated the surface obliquely; the moving ripples acted as a reflection <diffraction grating>. One split beam monitored laser stability, while a <CCD> recorded <diffraction orders> on a screen $6.76\,\mathrm m$ away. An air-supported optical table reduced ambient vibration. https://www.eg.bucknell.edu/~phys310/skills/papers/paperX_full.pdf[Zhu et al., Measurement of the dispersion relation of capillary waves by laser diffraction, American Journal of Physics 75 (2007), §II.A, p. 896; DOI 10.1119/1.2750379]{ref}

\Image[capillary-waves-diffraction.png]
{title=Drive a <capillary wave> at known <frequency> and measure its <wavelength> by <laser> <diffraction>}
{description=An original schematic of excitation and optical detection, with ray angles and surface displacement exaggerated for readability. The right panel calculates the deep-water <dispersion relation> for the stipulated parameters in <capillary length>; the $100$–$290\,\mathrm{Hz}$ band identifies a drive range to compare with the documented experiment. No diffraction intensities, measured points or fitted <surface tensions> are plotted.}

The <frequency> sweep was $100$–$290\,\mathrm{Hz}$ in $10\,\mathrm{Hz}$ steps, using distilled <water> and kerosene at $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. For each setting they averaged at least three measurements of first-order spot separation. Known optical geometry and the <grating equation> yielded $k=2\pi/\lambda$; generator readings yielded $\omega=2\pi f$. Agreement with the predicted log–log slope $3/2$ tests the capillary law, and its intercept gives <surface tension> if <mass density> is known. The authors identify surface contamination as a major error source; noncontact optical detection reduces additional disturbance. https://www.eg.bucknell.edu/~phys310/skills/papers/paperX_full.pdf[Zhu et al., §§II.B–III, equations (6)–(8), pp. 897–898]{ref}

The optical measurement can be understood from the <grating equation>. Let $\lambda_L$ be the <laser> <wavelength>, $\alpha$ the incident grazing <angle> above the mean surface, and $\beta_m$ the outgoing grazing <angle> of <diffraction order> $m$, with the sign of $m$ chosen so that
$$
\lambda\left(\cos\alpha-\cos\beta_m\right)=m\lambda_L.
$$
Thus calibrated spot positions determine outgoing <angles>, which determine the ripple <wavelength> $\lambda$, independently of the mechanical <dispersion relation>. This optical geometry treats the surface as a reflection <diffraction grating> at the <laser> <wavelength>. https://web.mit.edu/8.13/www/JLExperiments/JLExp001.pdf[MIT Department of Physics, Introductory Optics, §III.1, equation (4), p. 3: grazing-angle reflection grating]{ref}

The inferred <surface tension> in the full deep-water model is
$$
\gamma=\rho\frac{\omega^2-gk}{k^3}.
$$
This formula makes useful experimental controls explicit. Measure <mass density> at the sample's <temperature>; check depth with $kh$; keep the driven <wave amplitude> small enough that $ka\ll1$; and distinguish $f$ from <angular frequency> $\omega$. For the stipulated parameters, the $100$–$290\,\mathrm{Hz}$ window corresponds to calculated <wavelengths> about $3.62$–$1.76\,\mathrm{mm}$ and $kh\simeq52$–$107$ at $h=30\,\mathrm{mm}$. Depth corrections are negligible here, but the <gravity> contribution is about $4.5\%$–$1.1\%$ of the capillary contribution. These are ideal predictions, not digitized results from the experiment.

Omitting <gravity> would infer $\gamma_{\mathrm{cap}}=\rho\omega^2/k^3=\gamma+\rho g/k^2$, biasing <surface tension> upward. In the capillary limit, the differential estimate $d\gamma/\gamma\simeq d\rho/\rho+2\,d\omega/\omega-3\,dk/k$ also shows why careful spot-spacing calibration matters. A clean surface and weak <viscosity> are modeling assumptions: appreciable <damping>, <surfactant> stresses or large <wave amplitudes> call for additional physics. The experiment tests the short-wave regime; its drive window does not establish the crossover or the long-wave <surface gravity wave> limit.

= Deep-water capillary-wave dispersion relation
{title2=$\omega^2=(T/\rho)|k|^3$}
{parent=Capillary wave}

For a capillary wave on deep water with surface tension $T$ and density $\rho$, the dispersion relation is
$$
\omega(k)=S|k|^{3/2},
\qquad
S=\sqrt{T/\rho}.
$$
Its group velocity is $(3S/2)|k|^{1/2}$ in the direction of the wavevector.

= Surface response to a localized impulsive velocity
{parent=Deep-water capillary-wave dispersion relation}

If an initially flat one-dimensional surface receives velocity $-W$ on $|x|<\epsilon$ and zero velocity elsewhere, its linear capillary-wave response is
$$
\eta(x,t)=-\frac{2W}{\pi S}
\int_0^\infty
\frac{\sin(k\epsilon)\sin(Sk^{3/2}t)\cos(kx)}{k^{5/2}}\,dk.
$$

= Stationary-phase wake of an impulsive capillary wave
{parent=Surface response to a localized impulsive velocity}

Along $x=Vt>0$, if $\epsilon V^2/S^2\ll1$, the stationary wavenumber $k_0=4V^2/(9S^2)$ obeys $k_0\epsilon\ll1$. The <one-dimensional stationary-phase formula> then gives
$$
\eta(x,t)\sim
\frac{W\epsilon S t^2}{x^{5/2}}
\frac9{2\sqrt\pi}
\sin\left(\frac{4x^3}{27S^2t^2}-\frac\pi4\right).
$$
Only the integrated initial velocity $-2W\epsilon$ survives in this long-wave far field.

= Diffusion-limited relaxation of an interface
{parent=Capillary wave}

For a conserved scalar order parameter, a sinusoidal interface perturbation of wavenumber $q$ creates a harmonic chemical-potential field extending a distance $|q|^{-1}$ into each phase. Surface tension then produces a relaxation rate proportional to $-|q|^3$.

= Body force
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

A <body force> acts throughout a body's or <fluid>'s <volume>, rather than only through its surface. <Gravity> is the example used in <hydrostatic pressure>: a <gravitational field> $\mathbf g$ gives <force> per unit <volume> $\rho\mathbf g$, where $\rho$ is the <mass density>. More generally, a <body force> per unit <mass> $\mathbf f$ contributes $\rho\mathbf f$ to <momentum conservation>. <Forces> from <electromagnetism> are another example. Surface <pressure> <forces> produce <buoyancy> on an immersed body, while its <body force> from <gravity> acts downward.

= Elastic force dipole
{parent=Body force}
{title2=$f_i=-D e_i e_j\partial_j\delta(\mathbf x-\mathbf y)$}

Opposite forces separated along the unit direction $\mathbf e$, with fixed force times separation $D$, have this distributional <body force> limit. Its net force is zero. A symmetric <seismic moment tensor> is a sum of such dipoles along its principal directions.

= Hydrostatic pressure
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{title2=$dp/dz=-\rho_fg$}
{wiki}

<Hydrostatic pressure> is the <pressure> distribution in a resting <fluid>, balancing its <body force> from <gravity>. Take $z$ upward and a uniform downward <gravitational field> $\mathbf g=-g\mathbf e_z$, where $g>0$. The vertical <pressure gradient> satisfies
$$
\frac{dp}{dz}=-\rho_f g.
$$
A short derivation balances the vertical <forces> on a thin horizontal layer of <area> $A$ and thickness $\Delta z$: $p(z)A-p(z+\Delta z)A-\rho_fgA\Delta z=0$. Dividing by $A\Delta z$ and taking the <derivative> gives the equation above. For constant <mass density> $\rho_f$, <integration> from the surface at $z_s$ gives
$$
p(z)=p_{\mathrm{surface}}+\rho_fg(z_s-z)
=p_{\mathrm{surface}}+\rho_fgh,\qquad h=z_s-z.
$$
Thus <pressure> increases with depth $h$. For the stipulated <water> with <mass density> $1000\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ and <gravitational acceleration> $9.81\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, depths $0.20\ \mathrm m$ and $0.30\ \mathrm m$ give <gauge pressures> $1962\ \mathrm{Pa}$ and $2943\ \mathrm{Pa}$. Their difference produces the <buoyancy> of the example <cube>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_40.html#Ch40-S1[Feynman Lectures, II §40-1: hydrostatics]{ref}

= Gauge pressure
{parent=Hydrostatic pressure}
{title2=$p_g=p-p_{\mathrm{atmosphere}}$}

<Gauge pressure> is <pressure> measured relative to the surrounding <atmospheric pressure>; absolute <pressure> is instead measured relative to an ideal <vacuum>. At an open surface $p_{\mathrm{surface}}=p_{\mathrm{atmosphere}}$, so the constant-<mass density> <hydrostatic pressure> law becomes $p_g=\rho_fgh$. Here $p_g=0$ at the surface denotes equality with <atmospheric pressure>. It does not denote zero absolute <pressure>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-2-measuring-pressure[OpenStax, University Physics, §14.2: gauge and absolute pressure]{ref}

= Hydrostatic approximation
{parent=Hydrostatic pressure}
{wiki}

The hydrostatic approximation neglects vertical acceleration relative to gravity and vertical pressure gradients. It reduces vertical momentum balance to $p_z=-\rho g$.

= Hydrostatic balance
{parent=Hydrostatic approximation}
{title2=$p_z=-\rho g$}

Vertical <pressure gradient> balances gravitational force per unit volume. Subtracting the resting hydrostatic state and using <kinematic pressure> gives $p'_z=b'$ for a <Boussinesq approximation> buoyancy anomaly. The approximation neglects vertical <acceleration>; this diagnostic relation is what couples <buoyancy perturbation> to the vertical pressure profile.

= Volumetric flow rate
{title2=$Q$}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

= Volume flux
{synonym}

The volumetric flow rate is the volume of fluid crossing a section per unit time, $Q=\int_A\mathbf u\mathbin\cdot\mathbf n\,dA$.

= Mass flow rate
{parent=Volumetric flow rate}
{title2=$\dot M=\int_S\rho\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf n\,dS$}
{wiki}

The mass flow rate through an oriented cross-section is the integral of <mass density> times normal <velocity>. For constant density it is density times the <volumetric flow rate>. In a steady pipe without leaks, <conservation of mass> makes this rate independent of cross-section.

= Volume flux per unit width
{title2=$Q$}
{parent=Volumetric flow rate}

For a flow invariant in one horizontal direction, the <volume flux> per unit width is the <velocity> integrated across the flow depth. It has dimensions of area per unit time.

= Discharge coefficient
{title2=$C_d$}
{parent=Volumetric flow rate}
{wiki}

A discharge coefficient multiplies an ideal inviscid opening-flow rate to represent contraction and dissipative losses. Its value depends on the opening geometry and flow regime.

= Potential flow
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

Potential flow is irrotational flow represented as the gradient of a scalar potential.

= Corner sink in a semi-infinite channel
{parent=Potential flow}
{title2=$\psi=-my-\frac{2m}{\pi}\sum_{n\ge1}\frac{e^{-n\pi x}\sin(n\pi y)}n$}

For unit channel width, <incompressible flow> towards a corner withdrawal point has $\psi=0$ on the lower wall and $\psi=-m$ on the upper and left walls, with $\psi\to-my$ far downstream. For <irrotational flow>, <separation of variables> gives the displayed harmonic correction: the Fourier sine coefficients of $-m(1-y)$ are $-2m/(n\pi)$. Near the corner, $\psi\sim-2m\theta/\pi$ and radial velocity is $-2m/(\pi r)$, whose integrated inward flux across a quarter-circle is $m$.

= Rankine half-body
{c}
{parent=Potential flow}
{title2=$b=Q/(2U)$}

The region bounded by the dividing <streamlines> of a two-dimensional <point source> superposed on uniform flow. Its stagnation point is $-Q/(2\pi U)$ upstream and its far downstream width is $Q/U$. The boundary encloses fluid originating at the source rather than a rigid obstacle; the upstream stagnation point is reached only asymptotically by the upstream source trajectory.

= Radial source flow
{parent=Potential flow}
{title2=$u_r=Q/(2\pi r)$}

A two-dimensional incompressible line source with area flux $Q$ has radial <velocity> $Q/(2\pi r)$, obtained from flux conservation around a circle. It is irrotational away from the source, with potential $(Q/(2\pi))\ln r$. For a circular expanding interface, $Q=2\pi aa_t$ and $u_r=aa_t/r$, whose radial strain is $-aa_t/r^2$. That strain supplies a normal viscous stress even though the <velocity> is a potential flow.

= Potential dipole
{parent=Potential flow}
{title2=$\phi=\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf x/r^3$}

A potential dipole in three dimensions is the <potential flow> $\mathbf u=\nabla\phi$ with $\phi=\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf x/r^3$. Away from the origin its potential is a <harmonic function>, so it is also a constant-<pressure> <Stokes flow>. Its <velocity> decays as $r^{-3}$ and its <vorticity> is zero. It is distinct from a <stresslet>, whose leading <velocity> decays as $r^{-2}$.

= Point source
{parent=Potential flow}

A two-dimensional point source of strength $q$ has velocity potential $\phi=q\log r/(2\pi)$ and outward velocity $q\mathbf e_r/(2\pi r)$. Every closed contour enclosing it has volume flux $q$.

= Source dipole
{parent=Point source}
{title2=$\mathbf u=[3(\mathbf M\cdot\mathbf r)\mathbf r/r^5-\mathbf M/r^3]/(4\pi)$}

= Source doublet
{synonym}

A source dipole is the limit of an equal source and sink brought together with their source-strength times separation vector fixed at $\mathbf M$. Differentiating the <three-dimensional point source> field gives $\mathbf u=-(\mathbf M\cdot\nabla)[\mathbf r/(4\pi r^3)]$. It has <velocity potential> $-\mathbf M\cdot\mathbf r/(4\pi r^3)$, zero total <volume flux>, and $r^{-3}$ far-field decay. Unlike a <stresslet>, it is an irrotational potential singularity rather than a <force-dipole flow>.

= Three-dimensional point source
{parent=Point source}
{title2=$\mathbf u=Q\mathbf r/(4\pi r^3)$}

A point injection of volume at rate $Q$ produces the displayed radial <potential flow> in three dimensions. Its <velocity potential> is $-Q/(4\pi r)$, and every enclosing <sphere> has outward <volume flux> $Q$. The <divergence> vanishes away from the source and is $Q$ times the three-dimensional <Dirac delta> distribution at it. The field is also a <Stokes flow> with constant <pressure> away from its singularity.

= Hydrodynamic attraction of a plane wall to a point source
{parent=Point source}
{title2=$F=\rho Q^2/(16\pi a^2)$}

An incompressible <point source> of volume <flux> $Q$ at height $a$ above an impermeable plane has <velocity potential> $\phi=-Q(1/r_++1/r_-)/(4\pi)$, with an equal image source below the plane. On the wall, $u_s=Qs/[2\pi(s^2+a^2)^{3/2}]$. The <Bernoulli equation> gives $p-p_\infty=-\rho Q^2s^2/[8\pi^2(s^2+a^2)^3]$. Integrating the <pressure> deficit over the plane gives the displayed attraction force relative to ambient <pressure>. If source strength instead denotes the coefficient $Q/(4\pi)$ of radial inverse-square velocity, the force is $\pi\rho m^2/a^2$.

= Sink flow in a sector
{parent=Potential flow}

A line sink of strength $\alpha Q$ at the vertex of a sector $0<\theta<\alpha$ produces the irrotational outer flow
$$
u_r=-\frac Qr,
\qquad u_\theta=0,
\qquad \psi=-Q\theta.
$$
It satisfies no penetration at the radial walls but violates no slip there, so viscous boundary layers form along both walls.

= Similarity solution for a sink-flow boundary layer
{parent=Sink flow in a sector}

Near a radial wall, let $x$ measure distance from the sink along the wall and $y$ point into the fluid. With
$$
\delta=x\sqrt{\frac\nu Q},
\qquad
\eta=\frac y\delta,
\qquad
\psi=\sqrt{\nu Q}\,f(\eta),
$$
the <Prandtl boundary-layer equation> reduces to
$$
f'''=1-(f')^2,
\qquad
f(0)=f'(0)=0,
\qquad
f'(\infty)=-1.
$$
One physical solution has
$$
f'(\eta)=\frac{5-\cosh(\sqrt2\eta+c)}{1+\cosh(\sqrt2\eta+c)},
\qquad
c=\operatorname{arcosh}5.
$$

= Potential flow around a translating sphere
{parent=Potential flow}

For a sphere of radius $a$ translating at speed $U$ along the polar axis through fluid at rest at infinity, the laboratory-frame potential is
$$
\phi=-\frac{Ua^3}{2r^2}\cos\theta.
$$
It gives $u_r=Ua^3\cos\theta/r^3$ and $u_\theta=Ua^3\sin\theta/(2r^3)$.

= Spherically symmetric incompressible radial flow
{parent=Potential flow}

For radial flow outside a sphere, <incompressible flow> makes
$$
Q(t)=4\pi r^2u_r(r,t)
$$
independent of radius. Its velocity potential is $\phi=-Q/(4\pi r)$.

= Rayleigh equation for an inviscid spherical bubble
{c}
{parent=Spherically symmetric incompressible radial flow}
{title2=$R\ddot R+3\dot R^2/2=(p_g-p_\infty)/\rho$}

In radial <potential flow> outside a spherical bubble, <incompressibility> and the moving-boundary condition give $u_r=R^2\dot R/r^2$ and $\phi=-R^2\dot R/r$. The <unsteady Bernoulli equation>, with its gauge fixed at infinity, yields the displayed radius equation when surface tension and viscosity are neglected. Pressure continuity at the interface identifies the liquid pressure with the gas pressure $p_g$. The vacuum special case is <Rayleigh collapse of a spherical cavity>.

= Pressure-work energy balance for a spherical bubble
{parent=Rayleigh equation for an inviscid spherical bubble}
{title2=$K=2\pi\rho R^3\dot R^2,\quad\dot K=(p_b-p_\infty)\dot V$}

For spherical expansion in an unbounded <incompressible flow>, continuity gives $u_r=R^2\dot R/r^2$. Integrating the <kinetic energy> outside the bubble gives $K=2\pi\rho R^3\dot R^2$. The <Rayleigh equation for an inviscid spherical bubble> then yields $\dot K=(p_b-p_\infty)\dot V$, where $V=4\pi R^3/3$. With $p_b=p_\infty V_0/V$ and constant $p_\infty$, integration gives $K-K_0=p_\infty[V_0\log(V/V_0)-V+V_0]$. This neglects <viscosity>, <surface tension> and external body-force differences, which contribute additional terms in other bubble models.

= First integral of polytropic spherical-bubble motion
{parent=Rayleigh equation for an inviscid spherical bubble}
{title2=$R^3\dot R^2=\frac2\rho\int_{R_0}^R s^2[p_g(s)-p_\infty]ds$}

For gas pressure depending only on radius and initial speed zero, multiplying the <Rayleigh equation for an inviscid spherical bubble> by $2R^2\dot R$ integrates to the displayed identity. A polytropic pressure $p_g=K/R^{3\kappa}$ gives elementary power integrals when $\kappa\ne1$, and a logarithm when $\kappa=1$. Turning radii are the positive zeros of this first integral. At $\kappa=2$ and $p_g(R_0)=p_\infty/2$, they are $R_0$ and $R_0/2^{1/3}$; the inward and outward accelerations at them have opposite signs, giving bounded undamped oscillation in the <ideal> model.

= Rayleigh-Plesset equation
{c}
{parent=Spherically symmetric incompressible radial flow}
{title2=$R\ddot R+\tfrac32\dot R^2=(p_B-p_\infty-2\sigma/R-4\mu\dot R/R)/\rho$}
{wiki=Rayleigh–Plesset_equation}

The <Rayleigh-Plesset equation> balances the radial inertia of an incompressible liquid around a spherical bubble against internal and ambient <pressure>, <surface tension> and <viscous stress>. Removing the last two effects and setting the bubble pressure to zero gives the following vacuum-collapse case.

= Rayleigh collapse of a spherical cavity
{parent=Rayleigh-Plesset equation}
{c}

A vacuum cavity of radius $a(t)$ in an infinite inviscid incompressible fluid of density $\rho$ and far-field pressure $p_0$ obeys
$$
a\ddot a+\frac32\dot a^2=-\frac{p_0}{\rho}.
$$
If $a(0)=a_0$ and $\dot a(0)=0$, its collapsing branch satisfies
$$
\dot a=-\sqrt{\frac{2p_0}{3\rho}
\left(\frac{a_0^3}{a^3}-1\right)}.
$$

= Squeezing wedge flow
{parent=Potential flow}
{wiki}

Inviscid flow between closing hinged plates is a quadratic potential flow that expels fluid radially.

= Free surface
{title2=$z=\eta$}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

A free surface is a fluid boundary whose position is part of the solution and whose traction is prescribed by the adjoining medium. Its motion obeys a <kinematic boundary condition>, while its pressure or stress supplies a dynamic boundary condition.

= Surface gravity wave
{parent=Free surface}

A surface gravity wave is a free-surface oscillation restored by gravity. In inviscid deep water its angular frequency and horizontal wavenumber satisfy $\omega^2=gk$.

= Surface-wave breaking
{parent=Surface gravity wave}

A <surface gravity wave> breaks when its smooth free-surface motion becomes unstable and overturns or forms a strongly dissipative front. In the nondispersive <shallow-water approximation>, intersecting compressive <characteristic curves> signal failure of a smooth <simple wave>; a <hydraulic bore> replaces it at the integral-model level. Dispersion can instead produce an oscillatory bore, while physical breaking involves turbulence and energy loss beyond the inviscid equations.

= Normal modes of surface gravity waves in a rectangular tank
{parent=Surface gravity wave}
{title2=$\omega_{mn}^2=gk_{mn}\tanh(k_{mn}h)$}

For a tank with side lengths $a,b$, depth $h$, impermeable walls and no surface tension, the small-amplitude surface modes have horizontal factors $\cos(m\pi x/a)\cos(n\pi y/b)$ and $k_{mn}^2=(m\pi/a)^2+(n\pi/b)^2$. The velocity potential has vertical factor $\cosh(k_{mn}(z+h))$. Combining the linearized kinematic and dynamic conditions on the undisturbed surface gives $\omega_{mn}^2=gk_{mn}\tanh(k_{mn}h)$. The uniform $m=n=0$ elevation is excluded when the total water volume is fixed.

= Power-law near-shore ray asymptotics
{parent=Surface gravity wave}
{title2=$y-y_0\sim -K\sqrt{g\alpha}x^{1+p/2}/[\omega(1+p/2)]$}

For depth $h=\alpha x^p$ with $p>0$, stationary <surface gravity waves> conserve frequency $\omega$ and alongshore wavenumber $K$. Near shore their dispersion becomes $\omega^2\sim g\kappa^2h$, so $\kappa\sim\omega x^{-p/2}/\sqrt{g\alpha}$. The shoreward ray has $y'\sim-K\sqrt{g\alpha}x^{p/2}/\omega$, giving the displayed integrated scaling. Rays approach normally, while equal-phase crests approach parallel to the shore and their shore-normal spacing shrinks as $x^{p/2}$. These are asymptotics within the ray approximation; for $p<2$, its slow-variation assumption eventually fails arbitrarily close to the endpoint.

= Wave fetch
{parent=Surface gravity wave}

= Open-water fetch
{synonym}

Wave fetch is the distance over which wind acts on an uninterrupted water surface to generate waves. Growth also depends on wind speed and duration. A <polynya> can provide a local fetch inside otherwise extensive <sea ice>.

= Radiation stress
{parent=Surface gravity wave}
{title2=$S_{xx}=\mathcal E(2c_g/c_p-1/2)$}
{wiki}

= Wave radiation stress
{synonym}

The excess depth-integrated, time-averaged <momentum flux> of a <surface gravity wave> is its radiation stress. In deep water $c_g/c_p=1/2$, so $S_{xx}=\mathcal E/2=\rho_wga^2/4$. The force per transverse width on a reflecting object is the incident plus reflected minus transmitted radiation stress. Reflection increases force because the reflected wave carries momentum in the opposite direction.

= Surface-gravity-wave energy
{parent=Surface gravity wave}
{title2=$\mathcal E=\rho_wga^2/2$}

A linear <deep-water gravity wave> of surface-displacement amplitude $a$ has equal mean <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, and total mean <energy> per horizontal area $\mathcal E=\rho_wga^2/2$. Its energy transport speed is the <group velocity>. This amplitude is half the crest-to-trough height.

= Wave steepness
{parent=Surface gravity wave}
{title2=$ka$}

The dimensionless product of surface-wave amplitude and <wavenumber>, $ka$, measures orbital displacement relative to the wavelength. Small <wave steepness> permits a <Taylor expansion> of the velocity sampled by moving parcels. For a <deep-water gravity wave>, orbital amplitudes and the local steepness decrease exponentially with depth.

= Airy wave theory
{c}
{parent=Surface gravity wave}
{wiki}

<Airy wave theory> linearizes the motion of small-amplitude <surface gravity waves> in an inviscid, incompressible, irrotational fluid. At depth $h$, its dispersion relation is $\omega^2=gk\tanh(kh)$; deep water is the limit $kh\to\infty$.

= Deep-water gravity wave
{parent=Airy wave theory}

A small-amplitude deep-water wave with surface elevation $\eta=Ae^{i(kx-\omega t)}$ has dispersion relation $\omega^2=gk$ and a velocity potential that decays as $e^{kz}$ below the surface.

= Dispersive swell source inversion
{parent=Deep-water gravity wave}
{title2=$t=t_0+4\pi L/(gT)$}

For an impulsive distant source of <deep-water gravity waves>, a component of period $T$ travels at <group velocity> $gT/(4\pi)$. Its arrival at distance $L$ obeys the displayed law, so inverse period increases linearly with arrival time. Two arrivals determine both $L$ and $t_0$. Finite source duration, currents, refraction and passage through <sea ice> can invalidate the simple inversion.

= Viscous decay of a deep-water gravity wave
{parent=Deep-water gravity wave}

For small kinematic viscosity $\nu$, the mean viscous dissipation per unit horizontal area is $2\rho\nu gk^2|A|^2$. Since the mean wave energy is $\rho g|A|^2/2$, the amplitude obeys
$$
\frac{d|A|}{dt}=-2\nu k^2|A|,
\qquad
|A(t)|=|A(0)|e^{-2\nu k^2t}.
$$

= Linearized free-surface boundary conditions
{parent=Deep-water gravity wave}

For small-amplitude potential flow beneath a mean surface $z=0$, the linearized kinematic and dynamic conditions are
$$
\eta_t=\phi_z,
\qquad
\phi_t+g\eta=-\frac{p}{\rho}
\qquad(z=0).
$$

= Rectangular standing surface-gravity mode
{parent=Linearized free-surface boundary conditions}

In a rectangular box with Neumann side walls, a horizontal mode
$$
\cos\frac{m\pi x}{L_x}\cos\frac{n\pi y}{L_y}
$$
has horizontal wavenumber $k=[(m\pi/L_x)^2+(n\pi/L_y)^2]^{1/2}$, depth dependence $e^{kz}$ in infinitely deep fluid, and natural frequency $\Omega=\sqrt{gk}$.

= Resonance of a pressure-forced rectangular surface-gravity mode
{parent=Rectangular standing surface-gravity mode}

Pressure forcing of one rectangular free-surface mode reduces its amplitude to
$$
H''+gkH=-\frac{kp_0}{\rho}\cos(\omega t).
$$
At $\omega=\sqrt{gk}$, the inviscid undamped response contains $t\sin(\omega t)$ and grows without bound in the linear model.

= Streamfunction in polar coordinates
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

For planar incompressible flow, a streamfunction satisfies
$$
u_r=\frac1r\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\theta},
\qquad
u_\theta=-\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial r}.
$$

= Stagnation point
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

A stagnation point of a fluid flow is a point at which the <velocity field> vanishes. For a two-dimensional <streamfunction in polar coordinates>[streamfunction], it is a critical point satisfying both first spatial derivatives of the streamfunction equal to zero.

= Incompressible flow
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}
{title2=$\nabla\cdot\mathbf u=0$}

= Incompressibility
{synonym}

= Incompressibility condition
{synonym}

An <incompressible flow> preserves the <volume> of each moving <fluid element>. Its local condition is zero <velocity divergence>, $\nabla\cdot\mathbf u=0$. Combined with <mass conservation>, it says that each parcel's <mass density> stays unchanged as it moves. This does not require every parcel to have the same <mass density>: an initially nonuniform distribution can be transported without compression. Nor does <incompressibility> require a constant <velocity> or constant <pressure>.

For positive <mass density>, the <continuity equation> makes the equivalence precise:
$$
\frac{\partial\rho}{\partial t}+\nabla\cdot(\rho\mathbf u)=0,
\qquad
\frac{D\rho}{Dt}+\rho\nabla\cdot\mathbf u=0,
\qquad
\frac{D}{Dt}=\frac{\partial}{\partial t}+\mathbf u\cdot\nabla.
$$
The <material derivative> follows a <fluid element>. Thus $D\rho/Dt=0$ if and only if the <velocity field> is a <divergence-free vector field>. The distinction between following a parcel and observing a fixed position prevents confusing <incompressibility> with an unchanging <density> field. https://uclnatsci.github.io/2021/Electromagnetism-Fluids-and-Waves/fluids/conservation.html[UCL, Conservation law derivations, sections 30.1–30.2]{ref}

For a rigid pipe with impermeable walls, no leaks and <steady flow>, <mass conservation> at constant <mass density> implies a constant <volumetric flow rate>:
$$
Q=\int_A\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf n\,dA=A\overline U,
\qquad A_1\overline U_1=A_2\overline U_2.
$$
Here $\mathbf n$ points downstream, $A$ is the section's <area> and $\overline U$ is the mean normal <velocity>, not necessarily the centreline speed. No assumption of <inviscid flow> or a uniform <velocity profile> is needed. If unequal amounts entered and left a fixed segment, its <mass> would accumulate, contradicting <steady flow>.

For a worked example, take horizontal water flow with stipulated <mass density> $1000\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, inlet radius $R_1=0.010\,\mathrm m$, outlet radius $R_2=0.005\,\mathrm m$ and inlet mean <velocity> $\overline U_1=0.20\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. The circular <areas> give
$$
Q=\pi R_1^2\overline U_1=2\pi\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{m^3\,s^{-1}},
\qquad
\overline U_2=\frac{Q}{\pi R_2^2}=0.80\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}.
$$
The radius halves, the <area> falls to one quarter, and the mean <velocity> quadruples. The <mass flow rate> is $\rho Q=0.02\pi\,\mathrm{kg\,s^{-1}}$. The same amount of water passes both sections each second; narrowing changes its speed rather than compressing it. The <Reynolds number> and <Bernoulli's principle> discussions use this same example to examine viscous effects and the additional assumptions needed to calculate <pressure>.

\Image[fluid-flow-constriction.png]
{title=Continuity fixes the mean speeds; Bernoulli's principle gives a conditional ideal pressure drop}
{description=The pipe outline shows the radius ratio. Arrows represent section means, not a computed velocity field. The bars show the constant $Q$ and the ideal exchange between pressure and kinetic energy density at equal height; $p_2$ is the arbitrary pressure reference.}

= Rectilinear flow
{parent=Incompressible flow}

A <rectilinear flow> has velocity everywhere parallel to a fixed direction and independent of the coordinate in that direction, for example $U(y,z)\widehat x$. Its convective acceleration vanishes. A steady incompressible viscous rectilinear flow obeys the linear transverse Poisson equation $\nu\Delta U=\rho^{-1}dp/dx$, rather than an inertia-viscosity balance. This explains Reynolds-number independence of the spatial equation with fixed scaled forcing and boundary data.

= Streamfunction advection bracket
{parent=Incompressible flow}
{title2=$J(f,g)=f_zg_x-f_xg_z$}

For the two-dimensional <streamfunction> convention $u=\psi_z$, $w=-\psi_x$, the advective derivative of $\theta$ is $u\theta_x+w\theta_z=J(\psi,\theta)$. This antisymmetric <Jacobian determinant> bracket organizes nonlinear interactions in convection and vorticity equations.

= Cartesian streamfunction
{title2=$u=\psi_y,\ v=-\psi_x$}
{parent=Incompressible flow}

For a two-dimensional <incompressible flow>, a Cartesian streamfunction $\psi$ defines $u=\partial_y\psi$ and $v=-\partial_x\psi$. The continuity equation $u_x+v_y=0$ then holds identically.

= Divergence-free vector field
{parent=Incompressible flow}

A divergence-free vector field satisfies $\nabla\mathbin\cdot u=0$. It preserves volume under its flow, and its transport operator is skew-symmetric when boundary fluxes vanish.

= Streakline
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

A <streakline> at observation time $T$ is the set of present positions of all <fluid elements> that passed through one fixed release point $\mathbf a$ during a specified history of injection. For continuous release over $\tau_0\leq\tau\leq T$, solve a different <initial value problem> for each release time $\tau$:
$$
\frac{\partial\mathbf X}{\partial t}(t;\tau,\mathbf a)=\mathbf u(\mathbf X(t;\tau,\mathbf a),t),\qquad \mathbf X(\tau;\tau,\mathbf a)=\mathbf a,\qquad \mathcal S_T=\{\mathbf X(T;\tau,\mathbf a):\tau_0\leq\tau\leq T\}.
$$
Thus a <pathline> varies $t$ for one particle; a <streakline> varies $\tau$ while holding $T$ fixed. Interrupted release gives only the corresponding subsets of the <streakline>. A moving nozzle requires a time-dependent release position instead, so the standard fixed-point definition should not silently be applied to it. Continuous release makes <streaklines> easy to observe and useful for showing where material from one source has reached. https://web.mit.edu/13.021/demos/lectures/lecture2.pdf[MIT Marine Hydrodynamics, lecture 2, section 1.2]{ref}

In the analytical <velocity field> $(U,at)$ of <pathline>, a particle released at the origin at time $\tau$ follows
$$
x(t;\tau)=U(t-\tau),\qquad y(t;\tau)=\frac a2(t^2-\tau^2),\qquad t\geq\tau.
$$
Observe all particles together at time $T$, after continuous release from $\tau=0$:
$$
x=U(T-\tau),\qquad y=\frac a2(T^2-\tau^2),\qquad y=\frac{aT}{U}x-\frac{a}{2U^2}x^2,\qquad 0\leq x\leq UT.
$$
For $U=1\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, $a=1\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ and $T=2\ \mathrm s$, the <streakline> is the downward-curving <parabola> $y=2x-x^2/2$ in metre coordinates. Releases at $\tau=0,1,2\ \mathrm s$ occupy $(2,2)$, $(1,1.5)$ and $(0,0)\ \mathrm m$, respectively. At $x=1\ \mathrm m$, the <streakline> lies above the <pathline> $y=x^2/2$ and below the origin's <streamline> $y=2x$. All particles nevertheless have the same present <velocity> $(1,2)\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. The <streakline> slope $dy/dx=a\tau/U$ generally differs from the present <velocity> slope $aT/U$; they agree at the nozzle. The oldest particle is the downstream endpoint, which explains why the <pathline> and <streakline> meet there without being the same curve.

\Image[stream-path-streak-lines-release-family.png]
{title=Analytical release families observed at one and two seconds}

Each panel fixes one observation time. Coloured dots are the present positions of particles with the indicated release times; dashed <pathlines> show those particles' earlier histories. The black <streakline> connects present positions of the continuously released family, including release times between the sampled dots. After $T=1\ \mathrm s$, particles already present keep moving and new ones enter at the origin, so the later <streakline> is not a frozen record of the earlier one. Injection beginning only at $\tau=1\ \mathrm s$ would show just $0\leq x\leq1\ \mathrm m$ of the $T=2\ \mathrm s$ curve. Both panels are diffusion-free analytical models, not experimental photographs.

Kline's contracting-channel demonstration switches from marking an element to continuous bubble production at a marking wire: the resulting trace is a <streakline>. The apparatus therefore changes the curve being displayed by changing the marking history, even with the same channel. A wire produces a family of traces; conceptually each fixed emitting location supplies its own <streakline>. Individual bubbles are markers rather than perfect <fluid elements>: Jean Hertzberg's teaching notes describe <hydrogen> production by <electrolysis> at a thin <cathode> wire and warn that larger bubbles have more <buoyancy>. https://web.mit.edu/hml/ncfmf/05FV.pdf[S. J. Kline, Flow Visualization film notes, figure 8]{ref} https://www.flowvis.org/Flow%20Vis%20Guide/particles-3-in-water/[Jean Hertzberg and Flow Vis course students, Particles 3: In Water]{ref}

A dye version uses a small fixed injection port upstream in a transparent water channel, a dilute premix of dye with the channel water, illumination and a fixed <optical camera>. Start continuous injection at a recorded time $\tau_0$ and take a short exposure at $T$: the visible filament approximates a <streakline>, not the accumulated <pathline> of one particle. Repeat with the same port and imaging geometry after changing the flow with time; compare the filament against local directions estimated from short displacements of sparse markers at $T$. Agreement in a <steady flow> and possible disagreement when the flow changes are the predictions being tested; no measured curves are claimed here. The protocol follows Hertzberg's advice to use small ports, little injected volume and a dilute premix with matched <density> and <viscosity>. Check for nozzle disturbance by reducing injection rate, for <buoyancy> by watching the premix in still water, and for <diffusion> by comparing filament widths at different ages. Keep exposure short enough to suppress motion blur. Dye <concentration> approximates a <passive scalar> only when it has negligible feedback on the <velocity field>; finite port width, <diffusion> and out-of-plane motion limit how literally a visible filament can be read as a geometrical <streakline>. https://www.flowvis.org/Flow%20Vis%20Guide/do-not-disturb-dyes-in-liquids/[Jean Hertzberg and Flow Vis course students, Dye Techniques 1: Do Not Disturb]{ref}

= Kinematic boundary condition
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

A material fluid boundary has no relative normal flow; at a fixed impermeable graph $y=\eta(x)$ this is $v=u\eta_x$.

= Kinematic boundary condition for a free-surface graph
{parent=Kinematic boundary condition}

For a free surface $z=\eta(x,y,t)$ and velocity $(u,v,w)$, material conservation of $z-\eta$ gives
$$
w=\eta_t+u\eta_x+v\eta_y=\frac{D\eta}{Dt}
$$
on the surface.

= Linearized boundary condition
{parent=Kinematic boundary condition}

For a small boundary displacement and weak disturbance, evaluate boundary data on the undisturbed surface and discard products of small quantities.

= Dynamic boundary condition for an inviscid interface
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

At an interface between two inviscid fluids without surface tension, the pressure is continuous. For potential flows of equal density, the <unsteady Bernoulli equation> therefore equates
$$
\partial_t\phi+\frac12|\nabla\phi|^2
$$
on the two sides, up to a removable function of time.

= Pressure continuity
{parent=Dynamic boundary condition for an inviscid interface}
{title2=$[p]=0$}

For an inviscid interface with no <surface tension>, continuity of normal <traction> reduces to continuity of <pressure>. The same condition is used in a sharp <Darcy law> interface model that neglects <capillary pressure> and resolved interfacial viscous normal <stress>. For a general viscous interface, continuity of normal <traction> balances the full <stress> <tensor>; it does not by itself imply continuity of <pressure>.

= Wake
{disambiguate=physics}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

= Wake
{disambiguate=fluid dynamics}
{synonym}

= Fluid wake
{synonym}

A fluid wake is the region downstream of a body or velocity defect in which the flow differs from the surrounding stream.

= Shear layer
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

A shear layer is a thin region across which the tangential <velocity> changes rapidly. In an inviscid idealization it can collapse to a <vortex sheet>.

= Vortex sheet
{parent=Shear layer}
{wiki}

= Vorticity sheet
{synonym}

A vortex sheet is a surface across which tangential velocity is discontinuous while normal velocity remains continuous.

= Cylindrical vortex sheet
{parent=Vortex sheet}

A cylindrical vortex sheet is a vortex sheet supported on a cylindrical surface. Azimuthal Fourier disturbances have harmonic velocity potentials proportional to $r^m$ inside and $r^{-m}$ outside.

= Kelvin-Helmholtz instability
{parent=Vortex sheet}
{c}
{wiki=Kelvin–Helmholtz_instability}

For a planar vortex sheet separating equal-density streams of velocities $U_1$ and $U_2$, a mode of nonzero <wavenumber> $k$ has complex growth exponent
$$
\sigma=-ik\frac{U_1+U_2}{2}
\mathbin{\pm}\frac{|k|\,|U_1-U_2|}{2}.
$$
One sign has positive <growth rate>, so every nonzero wavenumber is unstable in the inviscid zero-thickness model.

= Bending-membrane Kelvin-Helmholtz dispersion relation
{parent=Kelvin-Helmholtz instability}
{title2=$(\sigma+ikU/2)^2=U^2k^2/4-\beta|k|^5/(2\rho)$}

For equal fluid density $\rho$ on opposite sides of a massless flat membrane, a velocity jump $U$ and bending pressure jump $\beta\eta_{xxxx}$ with $\beta>0$, a mode $e^{ikx+\sigma t}$ satisfies
$$
(\sigma+ikU/2)^2=U^2k^2/4-\beta|k|^5/(2\rho).
$$
The decaying upper and lower perturbation potentials are $A_+e^{-|k|y}$ and $A_-e^{|k|y}$. Their kinematic conditions are $-|k|A_+=(\sigma+ikU)\eta$ and $|k|A_-=\sigma\eta$. The linearized <Bernoulli equation> gives $p_- -p_+=-\rho[\sigma^2+(\sigma+ikU)^2]\eta/|k|$. Equating it to the bending pressure yields the relation. Bending stabilizes $|k|^3\ge\rho U^2/(2\beta)$. The maximum positive growth is at $|k|^3=\rho U^2/(5\beta)$ and is $|U|\,|k|\sqrt{3/20}$.

= Kelvin-Helmholtz dispersion relation with gravity
{c}
{parent=Kelvin-Helmholtz instability}
{title2=$\rho_1(U_1-c)^2+\rho_2(U_2-c)^2=g(\rho_2-\rho_1)/k$}

Two deep layers of <incompressible flow> with upper <mass density> $\rho_1$, lower <mass density> $\rho_2$, and tangential <velocities> $U_1,U_2$ have this <dispersion relation> when <surface tension> is absent. Decaying <velocity potentials> and the linearized <kinematic boundary condition> determine their amplitudes; the <dynamic boundary condition for an inviscid interface> gives the displayed relation. For $k>0$ the squared departure of the <phase velocity> $c$ from the density-weighted mean <velocity> is $g(\rho_2-\rho_1)/[k(\rho_1+\rho_2)]-\rho_1\rho_2(U_1-U_2)^2/(\rho_1+\rho_2)^2$. A negative value gives <Kelvin-Helmholtz instability> despite stable <density stratification>.

= Top-hat planar jet
{parent=Kelvin-Helmholtz instability}

A top-hat planar jet has one constant streamwise velocity in a layer and another constant velocity outside it. In the inviscid model, the two layer boundaries are parallel <vortex sheets>.

= Varicose mode of a planar jet
{parent=Top-hat planar jet}

In a varicose mode, the upper and lower jet interfaces move in opposite directions, so the jet thickness varies while its centreline remains fixed.

= Sinuous mode of a planar jet
{parent=Top-hat planar jet}

In a sinuous mode, the upper and lower jet interfaces move in the same direction, so the jet centreline bends while its thickness is unchanged to first order.

= Equal-density top-hat planar-jet dispersion relation
{parent=Top-hat planar jet}

For a jet of velocity $U$ occupying $|y|<h$ in stationary fluid of the same density, disturbances proportional to $e^{ikx+\sigma t}$ satisfy
$$
\sigma^2+\coth(|k|h)(\sigma+ikU)^2=0
$$
for a <varicose mode of a planar jet>, and
$$
\sigma^2+\tanh(|k|h)(\sigma+ikU)^2=0
$$
for a <sinuous mode of a planar jet>. Both have unstable growth rate
$$
\operatorname{Re}\sigma
=\frac{|kU|}{2}\sqrt{1-e^{-4|k|h}}.
$$

= Equality of top-hat jet parity growth rates
{parent=Equal-density top-hat planar-jet dispersion relation}
{title2=$\gamma=\tfrac12|kV|\sqrt{1-e^{-4|k|L}}$}

For a <top-hat planar jet> in stationary fluid of the same <mass density>, odd and even <velocity potentials> have the same <temporal growth rate>. Put $s=\tanh(|k|L)$. Their <dispersion relations> use $s$ and $1/s$, but $\sqrt{s}/(1+s)=\sqrt{1/s}/(1+1/s)$. Their real <phase velocities> are respectively $Vs/(1+s)$ and $V/(1+s)$. The common <growth rate> is strictly below $|kV|/2$ for finite $|k|L>0$, approaching the isolated <vortex sheet> value when the sheets decouple.

= Finite-depth vortex-sheet dispersion relation
{parent=Kelvin-Helmholtz instability}

For an equal-density vortex sheet a distance $h$ above a rigid wall, with lower and upper base velocities $U$ and zero, a mode $e^{i(kx-\omega t)}$ satisfies
$$
(\omega-kU)^2\coth(|k|h)+\omega^2=0.
$$
The wall changes the inertia of the lower perturbation through the factor $\coth(|k|h)$.

\Include[compressible-flow]{parent=Fluid mechanics}
\Include[linear-acoustics]{parent=Fluid mechanics}
\Include[astrophysical-fluid-dynamics]{parent=Fluid mechanics}
\Include[geophysical-fluid-dynamics]{parent=Fluid mechanics}

= Dispersion of a solute by flow
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

Dispersion of a solute by flow combines molecular diffusion with spatially varying advection, often producing an enhanced large-scale longitudinal diffusivity.

= Irreversible capture of an advecting tracer
{parent=Dispersion of a solute by flow}
{title2=$C_t+UC_x=D_{\rm eff}C_{xx}-\lambda C,\quad S_t=\lambda C$}

A first-order irreversible transfer from fluid concentration $C$ to stored concentration $S$, measured per matching fluid storage volume, obeys the displayed balances. A sustained inlet can produce a stationary decaying fluid profile, but the stored amount then grows rather than becoming stationary. A finite pulse instead has $C\to0$ and a finite final deposit. Decay, desorption or finite adsorption capacity is additional physics, not a consequence of the linear capture term alone.

= Transverse dispersion
{parent=Dispersion of a solute by flow}
{title2=$t_{\rm mix}\sim H^2/D_T$}

Spreading of solute across the mean flow direction through diffusion and pore-scale velocity variations. In a reflecting layer of depth $H$, a constant transverse dispersion coefficient gives mixing time of order $H^2/D_T$. Repeated exchange between different longitudinal speeds produces <Taylor dispersion> on times long compared with this mixing time.

= Péclet number
{title2=$\operatorname{Pe}=UL/D$}
{c}
{parent=Dispersion of a solute by flow}
{wiki}

The Péclet number compares advection over length $L$ at speed $U$ with molecular diffusion of diffusivity $D$.

= Taylor dispersion
{c}
{parent=Dispersion of a solute by flow}
{wiki}

Taylor dispersion is enhanced longitudinal spreading produced when transverse molecular diffusion repeatedly moves solute between streamlines of different velocity.

= Longitudinal moment hierarchy for shear transport
{parent=Taylor dispersion}
{title2=$\partial_t m_j=\kappa\partial_y^2m_j+ju m_{j-1}+\kappa j(j-1)m_{j-2}$}

For a <passive scalar> in a planar <shear flow> $u(y,t)$, let $m_j(y,t)=\int_{\mathbb R}x^j\chi(x,y,t)\,dx$. Integrating the <advection-diffusion equation> by parts in $x$ gives the displayed hierarchy for $j\geq0$, with negative-index terms omitted. Sufficient decay and finite moments justify the integrations. Insulating walls give <Neumann boundary conditions> for every moment. Solving the first two equations then determines the long-time growth of the second moment without solving the full scalar field.

= Oscillating shear dispersion in an insulating channel
{parent=Longitudinal moment hierarchy for shear transport}
{title2=$K=\kappa+U^2a/[4(a^2+\omega^2)],\quad a=\kappa\pi^2/L^2$}

For $u=U\cos(\omega t)\sin(\pi y/L)$ on $|y|<L/2$, positive <diffusivity> $\kappa$, nonzero frequency and insulating walls, a transversely uniform zeroth moment remains constant. Its first moment is proportional to $g(t)\sin(\pi y/L)$, where $g'+ag=U\cos(\omega t)$. The periodic solution is $g=U[a\cos(\omega t)+\omega\sin(\omega t)]/(a^2+\omega^2)$. The <longitudinal moment hierarchy for shear transport> then gives $m_2=2Ktm_0+O(1)$ after both transverse and periodic transients. The spatial average of $\sin^2(\pi y/L)$ is $1/2$ and the time average of $\cos^2(\omega t)$ is $1/2$, producing the factor $1/4$. At exactly zero frequency the steady enhancement is instead $U^2/(2a)$; averaging indefinitely over slow cycles does not commute with setting the frequency to zero.

= Effective diffusivity of a periodic sinusoidal shear
{parent=Taylor dispersion}
{title2=$\kappa_e=\kappa+\tfrac12\langle Ug\rangle=\kappa+\tfrac12\kappa m^2\langle g^2\rangle$}

For a <passive scalar> transported by $U(t)\sin(my)$ with transverse period $2\pi/m$, positive <diffusivity> $\kappa$ and periodic $U$, let $g$ be the unique periodic solution of $g'+\kappa m^2g=U$. A <multiple-scale expansion> has first corrector $-g(t)\sin(my)\partial_X\overline\chi$. Averaging the next-order equation over the space-time cell gives the displayed longitudinal <diffusion coefficient>. Averaging $gg'+\kappa m^2g^2=Ug$ proves the second form. This is a long-time, long-wavelength effective equation, after transverse and periodic transients have decayed, rather than an exact finite-time closure for arbitrary concentrations.

= Impulsively kicked sinusoidal shear dispersion
{parent=Effective diffusivity of a periodic sinusoidal shear}
{title2=$\kappa_e=\kappa+\frac{U_1^2}{4T}\coth(\kappa m^2T/2)$}

When velocity consists of impulses $U_1$ spaced by $T$, the periodic corrector jumps by $U_1$ and decays between kicks. With $a=\kappa m^2$, $g(t)=U_1e^{-at}/(1-e^{-aT})$ for $0<t<T$. Integrating $g^2$ in the <effective diffusivity of a periodic sinusoidal shear> formula yields the displayed result. For $aT\gg1$, independent transverse phases give longitudinal variance $U_1^2/2$ per kick and enhancement $U_1^2/(4T)$. For $aT\ll1$, the enhancement is $U_1^2/(2\kappa m^2T^2)$ to leading order, the steady-shear result for average speed $U_1/T$. The zero-diffusivity limit does not commute with the long-time limit: without transverse diffusion the displacement is ballistic.

= Taylor dispersion in a parabolic porous-layer velocity profile
{c}
{parent=Taylor dispersion}
{title2=$D_{\rm eff}=D+U^2h^2/(210D)$}

For <interstitial velocity> $u=6U\xi(1-\xi)$, $\xi=z/h$, reflecting transverse walls and molecular <diffusion> $D$, solve $D\chi''=u-U$ with zero mean and zero normal derivative. One solution is $\chi=(Uh^2/D)(\xi^3-\xi^4/2-\xi^2/2+1/60)$. Averaging the <advection-diffusion equation> gives enhancement $-\overline{(u-U)\chi}=D\overline{(\chi')^2}=U^2h^2/(210D)$. The approximation requires transverse equilibration and slowly varying longitudinal concentration.

= Taylor dispersion in a linear porous-layer velocity profile
{c}
{parent=Taylor dispersion}
{title2=$D_{\rm eff}=D_L+U^2H^2/(30D_T)$}

For pore velocity $u(y)=2Uy/H$ on $0<y<H$, with reflecting transverse boundaries, solve $D_T\chi''=u-U$, $\chi'(0)=\chi'(H)=0$, $\overline\chi=0$. The <Taylor dispersion> enhancement is $D_T\overline{(\chi')^2}=U^2H^2/(30D_T)$. Here $U$ is the mean advective pore velocity, not the <Darcy velocity>, and $D_L$ is the original longitudinal dispersion. The averaged equation applies after transverse equilibration and on suitably long longitudinal scales.

= Taylor dispersion in plane Couette flow
{c}
{parent=Taylor dispersion}

For $u(y)=Uy/h$ between reflecting walls at $y=\pm h$, the flow-induced diffusivity is
$$
D_T=\frac{2U^2h^2}{15D}.
$$

\Include[rheology]{parent=Fluid mechanics}
\Include[porous-media-flow]{parent=Fluid mechanics}
\Include[reduced-gravity]{parent=Fluid mechanics}

= Laminar round jet
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

A laminar round jet is an axisymmetric momentum-driven flow issuing into otherwise still fluid. Viscous diffusion broadens the jet while its axial momentum flux remains constant.

= Conserved momentum flux of a laminar round jet
{parent=Laminar round jet}

For axial velocity $w(r,z)$ in the boundary-layer approximation,
$$
M=\int_0^\infty r w^2\,dr
$$
is independent of $z$. If $W$ and $\delta$ are characteristic centre-line speed and width, then $W\delta\sim M^{1/2}$, while the momentum balance gives $W\delta^2\sim\nu z$. Thus
$$
\delta\sim\frac{\nu z}{M^{1/2}},
\qquad
W\sim\frac{M}{\nu z}.
$$

= Similarity solution for a laminar round jet
{parent=Laminar round jet}

With axisymmetric streamfunction $\psi=\nu z g(r/z)$, the regular normalized similarity profile is
$$
g(\eta)=\frac{12M\eta^2}{32\nu^2+3M\eta^2}.
$$
Its volume flux grows linearly with $z$, expressing <fluid entrainment>.

= Fluid entrainment
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

= Entrainment
{synonym}

Fluid entrainment is the incorporation of surrounding fluid into a moving current, jet, or plume. It increases volume flux while diluting transported momentum, buoyancy, or concentration.

= Entrainment velocity
{parent=Fluid entrainment}
{title2=$u_e$}

The mean speed, measured normal to a chosen plume or jet boundary, at which ambient fluid enters the turbulent region. The boundary and averaging convention must be specified: this speed is not the plume's axial velocity. In the <Batchelor entrainment hypothesis> for a rising <Boussinesq> plume, $u_e=\alpha w$, where $\alpha$ is the dimensionless <entrainment coefficient> and $w$ the characteristic axial speed. Multiplying by the boundary perimeter gives the entrained <volume flux> per axial length.

= Interfacial power model for entrainment
{parent=Fluid entrainment}
{title2=$P_I=S\tau u_I=c_D\rho_lS u_I^3$}

An interfacial stress $\tau=c_D\rho_lu_I^2$ supplies local stress power $P_I=S\tau u_I$. Assuming a fixed fraction $C_W$ becomes global <potential energy> gives $\dot E_P=C_WP_I$. Together with <potential energy of two-layer entrainment>, this gives $\dot h=2C_Wc_Du_I^3/(g'h)$. This is an additional local power-transfer closure. The shaft power of a rotating lid is its torque times its prescribed angular speed and need not equal $P_I$. Geometric factors and a constant transfer fraction may be absorbed into the empirical coefficients only when explicitly assumed time-independent.

= Potential energy of two-layer entrainment
{parent=Fluid entrainment}
{title2=$\dot E_P=\tfrac12\rho_lS(g'h)\dot h$}

In a closed tank of cross-sectional area $S$, entrainment into a uniform upper layer conserves the density deficit $(\rho_l-\rho_u)h$. Thus $B=g'h$ is constant. With depth $z$ increasing downward, gravitational <potential energy> is $-gS\int_0^H\rho(z)z\,dz=E_*+\rho_lSBh/2$, so its rate of increase is proportional to the rate of deepening. This includes homogenization throughout the upper layer, not merely lifting a thin parcel across the interface.

= Potential energy of mixed-layer deepening with an initial buoyancy jump
{parent=Potential energy of two-layer entrainment}
{title2=$\Delta\mathcal P/\rho_0=\Delta b\,a d/2+N^2a d^2/4+N^2d^3/12$}

Let an initial upper mixed layer have finite depth $a$, buoyancy $b_1$, and fixed upper boundary; beneath it let $b=b_1-\Delta b+N^2z$ with the initial interface at $z=0$. Homogenization down to $z=-d$ conserves <buoyancy> and gives mixed buoyancy $b_1-[\Delta b\,d+N^2d^2/2]/(a+d)$. Integrating gravitational <potential energy> over the initial and final profiles gives the displayed increase per unit area. Its derivative is $\rho_0[\Delta b\,a/2+N^2a d/2+N^2d^2/4]$. Specifying absorbed mixing power is not enough to determine deepening unless the initial depth or an alternative upper-reservoir model is supplied.

= Fixed-energy turbulent mixed layer
{parent=Fluid entrainment}
{title2=$u^2h=A\Omega^2R^3$}

In a slowly deepening mixed layer driven by a rotating boundary, a phenomenological balance between energy input, turbulent loss and acceleration of entrained fluid can keep total <kinetic energy> approximately fixed. For a tank of radius $R$ this gives $u^2h=A\Omega^2R^3$, so velocity decreases as $h^{-1/2}$. The closure does not make the speed constant and is an additional physical assumption rather than a direct consequence of <mass conservation>.

= Fixed-energy two-layer entrainment law
{parent=Fixed-energy turbulent mixed layer}
{title2=$h/h_0=(1+A\Omega t)^{2/5}$}

For <potential energy of two-layer entrainment> with constant $B=g'h$, the <fixed-energy turbulent mixed layer> assumption gives $u_U^2h=C_K^2\Omega^2R^2h_0$. If $u_I=C_Iu_U$ and the <interfacial power model for entrainment> applies with fixed coefficients, integration gives the displayed depth law with $A=5C_Wc_DC_I^3C_K^3\Omega^2R^3/(Bh_0)$. The relation applies only before the layer reaches the tank bottom. Its <interfacial Richardson number> $Bd_I/(h u_I^2)$ stays constant for fixed interface thickness, whereas it decreases like $h^{-1}$ in the constant-speed entrainment model.

= Rotating-disc mixed-layer depth law
{parent=Fixed-energy turbulent mixed layer}
{title2=$h/R\propto[\Omega^2R/(N^2\delta)]^{1/3}(\Omega t)^{2/9}$}

With constant interface thickness and the local <entrainment> exponent $3/2$, the <fixed-energy turbulent mixed layer> satisfies $\dot h=C\Omega^4R^6/(N^3\delta^{3/2}h^{7/2})$. Integration gives a linear growth law for $h^{9/2}$. Once an initial-depth offset is negligible, $h/R$ scales as $[\Omega^2R/(N^2\delta)]^{1/3}(\Omega t)^{2/9}$. The law applies before the mixed layer reaches the tank bottom and after the initial forcing transient.

\Include[turbulent-plume]{parent=Fluid mechanics}

= Natural ventilation
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{wiki}

Natural ventilation exchanges indoor and outdoor fluid through openings using pressure differences created by buoyancy or wind.

= Ventilation after a heating reduction
{parent=Natural ventilation}
{title2=$x_s=\sqrt{1-x}-\lambda$}

For an ideal two-layer <natural ventilation> transient following a heating change $Q\mapsto\lambda Q$, let $V_0$ and $\theta_0$ be the initial flow and temperature excess, $s=V_0t/(A_rH)$, $\eta=h/H$, and $x=\eta(1-\theta/\theta_0)$. The integrated buoyancy pressure gives $V/V_0=\sqrt{1-x}$, while <heat> balance gives $x_s=\sqrt{1-x}-\lambda$. A material interface satisfies $\eta_s=\sqrt{1-x}$, so $\eta=x+\lambda s$. For $0<\lambda<1$, $x$ increases toward $1-\lambda^2$ and $\eta_s\geq\lambda$, ensuring finite-time filling of the room. After filling, $y=\theta/\theta_0$ obeys $y_s=\lambda-y^{3/2}$ and tends to $\lambda^{2/3}$. This model assumes well-mixed individual layers, negligible wall heat capacity and a sharp advected interface.

= Well-mixed ventilation temperature balance
{parent=Natural ventilation}
{title2=$\mathcal V\dot\theta=\mathcal H-|q|\theta$}

For a distributed <heat> source, a well-mixed interior with <temperature> excess $\theta$ gains temperature-volume flux $\mathcal H$ and loses $|q|\theta$ by ventilation in either direction. With signed upward throughflow, $q|q|=K_A(G_T\theta\pm W)$, where $G_T=g\alpha_TH_b$ and $W$ is wind <pressure> divided by <mass density>. The plus sign is assisting wind. Steady temperatures satisfy $\theta^2(G_T\theta+W)=\mathcal H^2/K_A$ for assistance, or $\theta^2|G_T\theta-W|=\mathcal H^2/K_A$ for opposition. Direction restrictions must be applied to roots.

= Opposing-wind ventilation bistability
{parent=Well-mixed ventilation temperature balance}
{title2=$\varepsilon=G_T\mathcal H/(\sqrt{K_A}W^{3/2})$}

For $0<\varepsilon<2/(3\sqrt3)$, the <well-mixed ventilation temperature balance> has one stable upward-flow <temperature> and two downward-flow temperatures. The heat-removal curve on the latter interval is $\sqrt{K_A}\theta\sqrt{W-G_T\theta}$, maximal at $2W/(3G_T)$. Its lower-temperature root is stable and its higher-temperature root unstable. The two meet at a <saddle-node bifurcation>. The unstable root separates the basins of the stable wind-dominated and buoyancy-dominated states.

= Ventilation switching under wind and heating changes
{parent=Opposing-wind ventilation bistability}

Increasing wind or reducing heating both reduce the control parameter of <opposing-wind ventilation bistability>. However, wind moves the reversal threshold $\theta_w=W/G_T$, whereas heating does not. A sufficiently abrupt wind increase can place a hot upward-flow state in the cooler state's basin. Reducing positive heating at fixed wind cannot make an upward-flow state cross $\theta_w$, because its <temperature> time derivative there is positive. Slow variation follows a persistent stable branch; a fold destroys the cooler branch when wind is weakened or <heat> input increased.

= Effective opening area for pressure-driven ventilation
{parent=Natural ventilation}
{title2=$q=A_e\sqrt{\delta p/\rho}$}

An effective opening area may absorb both the discharge coefficient and the factor of two in an orifice law. If $A_e=\sqrt2C_dA_{\rm geom}$, its discharge is $q=A_e\sqrt{\delta p/\rho}$. Two equal openings carrying the same flux share the total <pressure> drop, so $q^2=A_e^2\Delta p/(2\rho)$. They do not share equal drops when an internal volume source makes their fluxes unequal. State the effective-area convention before comparing numerical factors in <natural ventilation> models.

= Displacement ventilation
{parent=Natural ventilation}
{wiki}

Displacement ventilation supplies relatively cool air at low level and removes warmer air at high level. In the ideal two-layer model, buoyant plumes feed a warm upper layer above a sharp density interface while the supply occupies the lower layer.

= Plume-fed doorway ventilation
{parent=Displacement ventilation}
{title2=$z_i^5=(8/27)\gamma^3W^2(D-z_i)^3$}

For a <pure plume> with $g'_p=\gamma B^{2/3}z^{-5/3}$, a steady sharp interface at $z_i$ has $Q_p=B^{1/3}z_i^{5/3}/\gamma$ and warm-layer <reduced gravity> $g'=B/Q_p$. A freely discharging upper doorway of height $D$ and width $W$ has available head $D-z_i$. Equating plume supply to the <broad-crested weir> flux gives the displayed implicit height relation, with $0<z_i<D$. Warm air above the doorway stores heat but adds no available head at its crest in this uniform-layer model.

= Two-plume three-layer displacement ventilation
{parent=Displacement ventilation}
{title2=$g_1'/g_2'=(\psi+\psi^{2/3})/(1+\psi)$}

Two independent point-source <pure plumes> with $\psi=B_1/B_2<1$ remove lower-layer volume $Q=C(B_1^{1/3}+B_2^{1/3})h_1^{5/3}$. If plume 1 feeds the middle layer, its reduced gravity is $g_1'=B_1/[CB_1^{1/3}h_1^{5/3}]$. The upper outflow carries both sources, so $g_2'=(B_1+B_2)/Q$, yielding the displayed ratio. Opening flow is driven by the integrated hydrostatic head $g_1'(h_2-h_1)+g_2'(H-h_2)$. A plume crossing the lower interface has altered buoyancy relative to its new ambient and must be continued with nonzero incoming mass and momentum fluxes rather than reset as a zero-flux pure plume.

= Source-volume blocking of displacement ventilation
{parent=Displacement ventilation}
{title2=$Q_c=(A_e^2BH)^{1/3}$}

A source volume $Q$ makes roof outflow exceed floor inflow by $Q$. At zero floor inflow, an entraining plume cannot support a steady cold lower layer; the warm layer reaches the floor. With <reduced gravity> $g'=B/Q$, the entire hydrostatic head drives roof discharge $Q=A_e\sqrt{g'H}$. This gives the stated threshold. Equal splitting of <pressure> head is inappropriate at blocking. The same result in physical-area notation is $Q_c=(2C_d^2A_{\rm geom}^2BH)^{1/3}$.

= Ventilated filling-box relaxation
{parent=Displacement ventilation}
{title2=$\tau_h,\tau_b$}

For total plume flux $P(h)$, fixed ceiling exhaust $F$, zero ambient <buoyancy>, and uniform upper-layer <reduced gravity> $b$, the ideal two-layer balances are
$$
A\dot h=F-P(h),\qquad \frac{d}{dt}\bigl[A(H-h)b\bigr]=B-Fb.
$$
At $P(h_*)=F$ and $b_*=B/F$, <linearization> yields the decay times $\tau_h=A/P'(h_*)$ and $\tau_b=A(H-h_*)/F$. For $P(h)\propto h^{5/3}$, $\tau_h=3Ah_*/(5F)$. Upper-layer <buoyancy> relaxation usually dominates when $h_*\ll H$. Exact equilibrium is approached asymptotically; a fixed small relative tolerance takes order $\max(\tau_h,\tau_b)\log(1/\varepsilon)$ after the initial transient.

= No steady displacement layer without ambient supply
{parent=Displacement ventilation}

Consider entraining plumes supplied with total source <volume flux> $S$, a separate lower-layer ambient inflow $L$, and ceiling exhaust $F=S+L$. Write $P(h)=S+E(h)$, where $E(h)$ is total <fluid entrainment> from the lower layer. <Volume conservation> gives $A\dot h=L-E(h)$. A steady positive lower layer therefore requires $L=E(h)>0$. If $L=0$ and $E(h)>0$ for every $h>0$, no positive steady <displacement-ventilation interface height> exists. In a shifted <pure plume> approximation $P_i(h)=\lambda B_i^{1/3}(h+z_i)^{5/3}$ matched to source flux $S_i$ at $h=0$, this follows immediately from $P_i(h)>S_i$ for $h>0$. This is a statement about the ideal two-layer model, not a prohibition of all possible stratification in a real ventilated room.

= Multiple-plume displacement ventilation
{parent=Displacement ventilation}
{title2=$h_*,b_*$}

Suppose $n$ independent <axisymmetric pure plumes> share total <buoyancy flux> $B$ equally, have negligible source <volume flux>, and are supplied with a separate ambient ventilation flux $F$ at floor level. Their combined flux is $P(h)=\lambda n^{2/3}B^{1/3}h^{5/3}$. <Volume conservation> at a steady <displacement-ventilation interface height> and <buoyancy flux> conservation give
$$
h_* = \left(\frac{F}{\lambda n^{2/3}B^{1/3}}\right)^{3/5},\qquad b_* = \frac BF.
$$
A positive two-layer state needs $0<h_*<H$, sources small relative to $h_*$, and plumes sufficiently separated to remain independent. Splitting the sources increases total <fluid entrainment> and reduces the interface height by $n^{-2/5}$. A finite source flux cannot simultaneously be neglected and supply all the replacement air; <no steady displacement layer without ambient supply> explains this restriction.

= Displacement-ventilation interface height
{title2=$h$}
{parent=Displacement ventilation}

At steady state, the displacement-ventilation interface lies where the total plume <volume flux> equals the imposed ventilation flux. For one triangular-profile <wall line plume> with kinematic <buoyancy flux> $B_0$, entrainment coefficient $\alpha$, and ventilation flux $A$ per unit span,
$$
h=\left(\frac{2}{3\alpha}\right)^{2/3}\frac{A}{B_0^{1/3}}.
$$

= Single-opening exchange flow
{parent=Natural ventilation}

A tall opening between fluids of different densities can support simultaneous inflow below a neutral pressure level and outflow above it. For a symmetric opening with a Boussinesq hydrostatic pressure difference and equal discharge coefficients, the neutral level lies at mid-height.

= Neutral pressure level
{parent=Single-opening exchange flow}

The neutral pressure level is the height at which indoor and outdoor hydrostatic pressures agree. Flow through an opening reverses direction across this level.

= Stratified-room plume-exchange model
{parent=Natural ventilation}

A stratified-room plume-exchange model balances the upward volume transported by a warm plume against downward transport by cold wall plumes. A global buoyancy balance relates the source flux to the buoyancy carried out through the vents.

= Homologous spherical flow
{title2=$\mathbf u=q(t)\mathbf r$}
{parent=Fluid mechanics}

A homologous spherical flow has velocity proportional to radius. Its <velocity gradient> is $q(t)I$ and its <velocity divergence> is $3q(t)$.

= Self-similar ansatz
{parent=Homologous spherical flow}

A self-similar ansatz expresses evolving fields through a dimensionless similarity coordinate such as $x=r/R(t)$, reducing a partial differential equation to ordinary differential equations in $x$.
